var e=[{id:`mj-Fl0MSWtHIOo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:0,title:`How to Build an Outbox with EF Core Interceptors`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:37`,minutes:15,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Outbox`],story:{headline:`The Night the Orders Shipped Twice`,paragraphs:[`It's 14:00 during a blue-green cutover on Black Friday. The orders API has been humming along all night when the message broker's connection blips for eight seconds. By then, SaveChangesAsync has already done its job — the order row is committed, the money is captured. But the very next line in the endpoint, the one that publishes OrderPlaced, throws. The exception bubbles out of the request like the whole operation failed.`,`The client's retry policy does exactly what it's told: it retries. Now you have two orders for one checkout, zero OrderPlaced events delivered, and a support queue filling up by dawn. The database says success, the caller says failure, and the broker says nothing at all. That's the dual-write problem, and no amount of careful try/catch fixes it — catching the error just means silently dropping the event instead.`,`The real fix is refusing to make two systems share one transaction. In this session we walk the full refactor: from a SaveChanges override that publishes events, to EF Core interceptors, to an outbox that commits the event in the same transaction as the fact that produced it — and a background dispatcher that delivers it while the request path stays boring.`],problem:`Publishing domain events after SaveChangesAsync is non-atomic: a failed publish throws inside an already-committed EF Core operation, so retries duplicate writes and swallowed errors silently lose events. The database write and the broker write can never succeed together.`},tech:{stack:[`EF Core`,`SaveChangesInterceptor`,`Outbox Pattern`,`MediatR`,`JSON Serialization`,`Background Dispatcher`],note:`SaveChangesInterceptor hooks scan the ChangeTracker for domain events; the outbox interceptor turns them into OutboxMessage rows committed atomically with business data, and a background dispatcher delivers them at-least-once.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why publishing after SaveChanges is a trap`,body:[`So here's where we start: a DbContext with SaveChangesAsync overridden. Call base first so the changes hit the database, then pull every entry from the ChangeTracker that implements our entity base class, collect their domain events, and hand them to the publisher. It reads beautifully. It demos beautifully. And it fails in production in the most annoying way possible.`,`The problem is that 'publish after save' is two systems pretending to share a transaction. If the third of five publishes fails, that exception is thrown inside your original EF Core operation — the caller sees a failure, the database sees a success. Retry the request and you've written the order twice. Catch the exception and move on, and you've silently dropped a domain event. Neither answer survives a postmortem.`,`So before we touch any code, here's the question I want in your head: what if the event were just... a row? Something the same transaction already knows how to commit? Hold that thought — first we need a cleaner place to hang this behavior than an override.`],quote:`Two systems, one transaction isn't atomicity — it's a coin flip with extra steps.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Publish after SaveChanges`,`Simple, zero new tables`,`Non-atomic; failed publish throws on a committed write`,`Demos only`],[`try/catch + log failures`,`No exceptions escape the request`,`Silently loses domain events`,`Never`],[`Distributed 2PC`,`Atomic across systems`,`Fragile, slow, poor broker support`,`Legacy only`],[`Outbox pattern`,`Atomic commit, resumable delivery`,`Extra table, dispatcher, idempotent consumers`,`Production`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Build an Outbox with EF Core Interceptors`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fl0MSWtHIOo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: interceptors, not overrides`,body:[`First refactor: get this logic out of the DbContext override. EF Core gives us interceptors — hook points into executing database commands and into the save pipeline, before and after. You'll meet IInterceptor first, and if you F12 into it, it's empty. It's a marker interface, nothing more. Don't implement it raw.`,`What you want is the SaveChangesInterceptor base class, which already implements IInterceptor with virtual methods. SavingChangesAsync fires before the save; SavedChangesAsync fires after it completes. For our publish-after-save version, we override SavedChangesAsync. The hook hands us the event data and the result, and we take a DbContext parameter — the base type, not our concrete class — so we can reach into the ChangeTracker and grab the entities carrying domain events.`,`Why bother with an interceptor at all? Because it composes. Today it's publish-domain-events; tomorrow it's the outbox; next quarter it's audit rows. You stack them with AddInterceptors and your DbContext never grows another override.`],quote:`The SaveChanges override works fine — right up until the second feature needs the same hook.`,table:{headers:[`Hook`,`When it fires`,`Use for`,`Fit`],rows:[[`IInterceptor (raw)`,`Nothing — empty marker interface`,`Type constraint only`,`Never directly`],[`SavingChangesAsync`,`Before the save and its transaction commit`,`Appending outbox rows to the context`,`Outbox writes`],[`SavedChangesAsync`,`After the save completes`,`Post-save side effects`,`Naive publishing`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Build an Outbox with EF Core Interceptors`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fl0MSWtHIOo`,at:`1:25`}},{heading:`03 · The DI lifetime trap nobody warns you about`,body:[`Now the wiring, and here's where people get bitten. Register the interceptor in DI — AddSingleton<PublishDomainEventsInterceptor>(). It depends on IPublisher, which is registered as transient. Singleton depending on transient? Perfectly safe. Singleton depending on a scoped service? That's a captive dependency — the scoped instance lives as long as your singleton, and now you have a bug that only shows up under load.`,`To attach it, override OnConfiguring on the DbContext. That override hands you the service provider, so it's UseSqlServer, then AddInterceptors, resolving the interceptor with GetRequiredService. One line, and every save in the application flows through your hook.`],quote:`A singleton holding a scoped service isn't a shortcut — it's a race condition with a long memory.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Build an Outbox with EF Core Interceptors`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fl0MSWtHIOo`,at:`4:36`}},{heading:`04 · The refactor: outbox, one transaction to rule them all`,body:[`Here's the change that actually fixes reliability: the outbox pattern. We define an OutboxMessage record — an Id, the domain event's Name, the Content as the event serialized to JSON, the datetime it occurred, and a ProcessedOn datetime that starts null.`,`Then a second interceptor, hooked at SavingChangesAsync — before the save — scans the ChangeTracker for domain events and appends OutboxMessage rows to the context. Now when SaveChangesAsync runs, the business rows and the event rows go into the database in one transaction. Commit succeeds for both or neither. The event can't be lost, because it is literally a row in the same commit as the fact that produced it.`,`And notice what we didn't do: we never touch the broker in the request path. The request's job is to make the state change and record the event. Delivery is somebody else's job now.`],quote:`Write the event where you write the fact — same transaction, same commit, same fate.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Build an Outbox with EF Core Interceptors`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fl0MSWtHIOo`,at:`5:06`}},{heading:`05 · Delivery semantics: at-least-once or bust`,body:[`So who delivers? A background dispatcher polls the outbox for messages where ProcessedOn is null, publishes each one, then stamps ProcessedOnDateTime. Crash between publish and stamp? You'll publish that message again on restart. That's not a bug — that's at-least-once delivery, and it's the strongest guarantee this design can honestly make.`,`Which means your consumers must be idempotent: dedupe on the OutboxMessage Id, because a duplicate OrderPlaced is far cheaper than a lost one. And plan the hygiene — clean up processed rows on a schedule, or that outbox table becomes your new slow query.`],quote:`At-least-once delivery makes consumer idempotency part of your architecture, not an optional extra.`,table:{headers:[`Semantics`,`How`,`Consumer contract`,`Fit`],rows:[[`At-most-once`,`Fire and forget`,`Tolerates loss`,`Metrics, telemetry`],[`At-least-once`,`Outbox + redelivery on crash`,`Must be idempotent (dedupe on Id)`,`Domain events`],[`Exactly-once`,`Transactional broker + consumer coordination`,`Heavy coordination cost`,`Rarely worth it`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Build an Outbox with EF Core Interceptors`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fl0MSWtHIOo`,at:`7:40`}}],diagrams:[{id:`outbox-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Outbox topology: the broker leaves the request path`,caption:`Business rows and outbox rows commit in one transaction; a background dispatcher owns delivery.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph REQ["Request Path"]
        H["Command Handler"] --> C["AppDbContext"]
        C --> I["Outbox Interceptor"]
        I --> TX["One DB Transaction"]
    end
    TX --> O["Orders Table"]
    TX --> M["Outbox Table"]
    subgraph DISP["Background Dispatcher"]
        P["Poll unprocessed"] --> B["Publish via Broker"]
        B --> U["Stamp ProcessedOn"]
    end
    M --> P
    U --> M
    B --> K["Idempotent Consumers"]`},{id:`interceptor-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Interceptor sequence: event as a row, not a network call`,caption:`SavingChangesAsync appends OutboxMessage rows before the commit; the dispatcher publishes and stamps ProcessedOn afterwards.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant H as Command Handler
    participant C as AppDbContext
    participant I as Outbox Interceptor
    participant DB as Database
    participant W as Dispatcher
    participant B as Broker

    H->>C: SaveChangesAsync()
    C->>I: SavingChangesAsync hook
    I->>I: Scan ChangeTracker for domain events
    I->>C: Add OutboxMessage (Id, Name, Content JSON, OccurredOn)
    C->>DB: One transaction: business rows + outbox rows
    DB-->>C: COMMIT (all or nothing)
    C-->>H: Success
    loop Poll unprocessed
        W->>DB: SELECT outbox WHERE ProcessedOn IS NULL
        W->>B: Publish domain event
        W->>DB: UPDATE ProcessedOnDateTime
    end`}],takeaways:[`Never publish to a broker in the request path after SaveChangesAsync — one broker blip turns a committed order into a thrown exception and a duplicate on retry.`,`Inherit SaveChangesInterceptor, never raw IInterceptor — the marker interface is empty; the base class hands you virtual SavingChangesAsync and SavedChangesAsync hooks for free.`,`Register interceptors as Singleton only when their dependencies are Singleton or Transient — a Scoped dependency inside a Singleton interceptor is a captive-dependency bug that surfaces under load.`,`Hook SavingChangesAsync to append OutboxMessage rows so business data and events commit in one transaction — all rows or none, never a lost event.`,`Stamp ProcessedOnDateTime after publishing and dedupe consumers on the OutboxMessage Id — crash-redelivery is a feature of at-least-once, so idempotency is mandatory, and schedule cleanup of processed rows.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Build an Outbox with EF Core Interceptors`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fl0MSWtHIOo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`change-data-capture`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-1tPVVDEDGtE`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:1,title:`How To Implement Rate Limiting In .NET + BONUS: Rate Limiter Algorithms 101`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 9:43`,minutes:10,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`Rate limiter`],story:{headline:`11:45 when batch reconciliation started: The Retry Storm That Ate Order Summary`,paragraphs:[`It's 09:30 during a latency regression audit and the pager goes off: order summary latency at 40 seconds, connection pool exhausted, timeouts cascading into checkout. The culprit isn't a bad deploy or a rogue query — it's a partner integration whose SDK retries failures three times with zero backoff, running a sync job every single second. Our 'cheap' read endpoint is drowning in its own popularity.`,`We survive the night by hand-throttling the partner at the load balancer, which is exactly as fun as it sounds. The postmortem lands on one uncomfortable sentence: this endpoint had no ceiling. Any client — or one buggy retry loop — could queue unlimited work against a downstream that could only serve a handful of concurrent summaries.`,`The real fix took an afternoon: .NET's built-in rate limiting middleware, one named policy at a time. Four algorithms, a ten-line config, and the next 15:40 as connection pools saturated page never happens.`],problem:`One aggressive retry loop could send unbounded concurrent requests at an endpoint whose database pool could only serve a handful at a time — we needed permits at the door, not heroics at 3 AM.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET 8`,`AddRateLimiter`,`UseRateLimiter`,`Minimal APIs`,`Fixed Window`,`Sliding Window`,`Token Bucket`,`Concurrency Limiter`],note:`The talk's moving parts: rate limiting middleware registered via AddRateLimiter, four limiter algorithms configured as named policies, and RequireRateLimiting attaching them to Minimal API endpoints.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: 'just handle more traffic' isn't a plan`,body:[`Here's the thing nobody tells you: adding rate limiting is a ten-line change, but choosing the algorithm is the actual engineering. In .NET it all starts with builder.Services.AddRateLimiter(), where you register named policies — fixed, sliding, token bucket, concurrency — and then app.UseRateLimiter() flips the middleware on. From there, a single .RequireRateLimiting("fixed") on a Minimal API endpoint is your entire contract.`,`Why not just throw hardware at it? Because the bottleneck usually isn't your API — it's the downstream thing it protects, like a database connection pool with a fixed size. And why not rate limit only at the gateway? You should, but the app-level limiter is the one that understands endpoint semantics: order summaries and payment captures should never share the same budget.`],quote:`A rate limiter is a promise your API makes about its worst day, not its best.`,table:{headers:[`Enforcement point`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Load balancer / gateway`,`Global view of all traffic`,`Blind to endpoint semantics`,`Coarse, first line`],[`App middleware (UseRateLimiter)`,`Per-endpoint named policies, no infra changes`,`Per-instance, not cluster-wide`,`Default choice`],[`Downstream (DB pool)`,`Physically bounded`,`Overload surfaces as timeouts`,`Last resort`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Rate Limiting In .NET + BONUS: Rate Limiter Algorithms 101`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tPVVDEDGtE`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: fixed window and its dirty secret`,body:[`Start with the dumbest thing that could work: a fixed window. Window = 10 seconds, PermitLimit = 3, QueueLimit = 3, QueueProcessingOrder.OldestFirst. Fire three requests, they sail through. Fire a fourth and it doesn't fail — it parks in the queue until the window rolls over, then gets processed. That's the spinning loader you saw in the demo.`,`But here's the dirty secret: windows have seams. Three requests at second nine and three more at second eleven means six requests in roughly two seconds — double your limit. Shrinking the window doesn't fix it; it just makes the seams more frequent. And if a client's retry loop happens to sync with your boundary, you've built them a metronome.`],quote:`Fixed windows are easy to explain — and easy to burst, twice, right at the seam.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Rate Limiting In .NET + BONUS: Rate Limiter Algorithms 101`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tPVVDEDGtE`,at:`0:42`}},{heading:`03 · Smoothing the spike: sliding window vs token bucket`,body:[`Two cures for the seam. The sliding window chops your window into segments — say a 10-second window sliced into 10 one-second segments — and carries recent history forward, so a burst at the tail genuinely eats into the next window's budget. The cost is a little extra memory and bookkeeping per policy.`,`The token bucket thinks in refill rates instead of intervals: the bucket holds, say, 100 tokens, refills 20 per second, and each request spends one. Quiet clients accumulate credit; bursty clients spend it down. That's why every cloud SDK's throttling looks like this. Why not token bucket everywhere? Because it's a burst model, not a hard quota — if a compliance rule says 'max 3 per 10 seconds, period,' a bucket that saved up 30 tokens would happily violate it.`],quote:`Sliding window smooths time; token bucket smooths demand.`,table:{headers:[`Algorithm`,`Traffic shape`,`Best for`,`Watch out for`],rows:[[`Fixed window`,`Hard cap per interval`,`Simple quotas, compliance caps`,`2x burst at window seams`],[`Sliding window`,`Smoothed cap`,`Fair limits without seams`,`Memory for segment history`],[`Token bucket`,`Sustained rate + burst`,`Bursty clients, SDK-style throttling`,`Saved tokens can exceed quota`],[`Concurrency`,`Max in-flight`,`Protecting pools and slow I/O`,`Slow holders starve throughput`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Rate Limiting In .NET + BONUS: Rate Limiter Algorithms 101`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tPVVDEDGtE`,at:`1:16`}},{heading:`04 · Queue or reject: what does the caller actually experience?`,body:[`QueueLimit is the most consequential number in the whole config, and people set it by vibes. Queue the request and the caller waits: with 3 permits per 10 seconds and a queue of 3, your worst-case wait is roughly QueueLimit × Window ÷ PermitLimit — about 10 seconds. That's a latency loan, and slow callers holding connections while they wait is its own capacity problem.`,`Set QueueLimit to zero and the character changes completely: no queue, fail fast. One catch — the middleware's default rejection is a 503, which retry loops read as 'try again soon' or worse, 'try again now.' Set RejectionStatusCode to 429 and emit Retry-After, or your rate limiter becomes a retry-storm amplifier — which, if you remember the 09:15 during the morning rush page, is exactly the disease we're treating.`],quote:`A queue is a latency loan — charge interest, or don't lend.`,table:{headers:[`QueueLimit`,`Caller experience`,`Server cost`,`When to use`],rows:[[`0`,`Instant rejection, retry later`,`Minimal; connections freed fast`,`Public APIs, aggressive clients`],[`≤ PermitLimit`,`Bounded wait (~QueueLimit × Window ÷ Permits)`,`Held connections while queued`,`Trusted internal callers`],[`> PermitLimit`,`Long opaque waits`,`Latency and memory pile-up`,`Almost never`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Rate Limiting In .NET + BONUS: Rate Limiter Algorithms 101`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tPVVDEDGtE`,at:`1:50`}},{heading:`05 · The odd one out: concurrency limiters protect downstreams`,body:[`The concurrency limiter is the odd one out — it doesn't measure time at all. It counts in-flight requests and releases the permit when the response completes. That makes it the perfect tool for the actual 3 AM problem: capping simultaneous load on a downstream. If your connection pool is 20, a concurrency limiter at 10–15 keeps one endpoint from ever monopolizing the pool.`,`Why not use it as your rate limit? Because it meters occupancy, not arrival rate — one slow client holding a permit for 30 seconds starves everyone queued behind them. Pair it with request timeouts, or stack it alongside a time-based limiter: token bucket for the shape of traffic, concurrency for the floor beneath it.`],quote:`Rate limiters meter time; concurrency limiters meter occupancy.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Rate Limiting In .NET + BONUS: Rate Limiter Algorithms 101`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tPVVDEDGtE`,at:`4:25`}}],diagrams:[{id:`rate-limit-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path through the rate limiting middleware`,caption:`Where the limiter sits in the pipeline: permit, queue, or reject — all decided before the endpoint ever runs.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    C["Client / Partner SDK"] --> MW["UseRateLimiter middleware"]
    MW --> P{"Policy 'fixed' check"}
    P -- "permit available" --> EP["Order summary endpoint"]
    P -- "queue has room" --> Q["Queue - OldestFirst"]
    Q -- "window renews" --> EP
    P -- "queue full" --> RJ["Reject: 503 default / 429 configured"]
    EP --> DB[("Orders DB pool")]`},{id:`fixed-window-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Fixed window lifecycle: 3 permits, 3 queued, then reject`,caption:`The exact demo flow — three requests pass, the fourth queues until the 10-second window renews, and an overfull queue fails fast.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant MW as RateLimiter middleware
    participant L as Fixed window limiter
    participant EP as Order endpoint
    C->>MW: Requests 1-3
    MW->>L: Acquire permit
    L-->>MW: Granted
    MW->>EP: Invoke
    EP-->>C: 200 OK
    C->>MW: Request 4
    MW->>L: Acquire permit
    L-->>MW: Exhausted - enqueue, QueueLimit 3
    Note over L: 10s window expires
    L-->>MW: Dequeue oldest, grant permit
    MW->>EP: Invoke
    EP-->>C: 200 OK after wait
    C->>MW: Request 8, queue full
    L-->>MW: Reject
    MW-->>C: 503 default / 429 if configured`}],takeaways:[`Ship every public endpoint with a limiter from day one — a fixed window of 3 permits per 10 seconds is a fine default; tune from measured p95, not vibes.`,`Keep QueueLimit ≤ PermitLimit: worst-case queued wait ≈ QueueLimit × Window ÷ PermitLimit (3 × 10s ÷ 3 = 10 seconds).`,`Override the middleware's default 503 with RejectionStatusCode = 429 plus Retry-After — a 503 invites exactly the retry storms you're defending against.`,`For bursty clients, token bucket: set capacity = max burst and replenishment = sustained rate (e.g., 100 tokens refilled at 20/sec = 5 seconds of 5x burst headroom).`,`Size concurrency limiters to your scarcest downstream: cap in-flight at ~50-70% of the connection pool (10-15 permits for a 20-connection pool) and always pair with a request timeout.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Rate Limiting In .NET + BONUS: Rate Limiter Algorithms 101`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tPVVDEDGtE`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`rate-limiting`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-4UP6L8cDBN8`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:2,title:`Publish Subscribe Messaging In .NET With Redis Channels`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:28`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`Docker`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The Stale Price That Survived Three Deploys`,paragraphs:[`It's 14:30 right after a canary deploy and the on-call phone is buzzing: the EU storefront is showing yesterday's flash-sale prices a full day after the sale ended. The pricing service swears it published the invalidation event. The catalog service swears it never received one. Both are telling the truth — because that invalidation travels over a Redis pub/sub channel, and the catalog pod happened to be mid-restart during the rolling deploy when the message flew past. Nobody was listening. The message didn't wait.`,`The next morning, the team rebuilt the demo they should have built on day one: two .NET worker services — a publisher and a subscriber — wired over a Redis channel, publishing every five seconds, proving fan-out by spinning up a second subscriber. Then they deliberately killed the subscriber mid-stream and watched the next message vanish without a trace. That's the moment pub/sub stops being magic and starts being a decision.`,`This masterclass is that rebuild: the ConnectionMultiplexer, the channel constant, the Docker one-liner, and the fine print about fire-and-forget that separates a harmless cache signal from a lost order.`],problem:`How do you wire two .NET worker services together over Redis pub/sub channels — and know exactly which delivery guarantees you're giving up when you do?`},tech:{stack:[`Redis`,`StackExchange.Redis`,`.NET Workers`,`Docker`,`Pub/Sub Channels`],note:`Redis plays double duty here: the in-memory cache everyone knows, plus a topic-style pub/sub layer most people never touch. StackExchange.Redis's ConnectionMultiplexer and ISubscriber are the only two handles a .NET worker needs.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just call the other service?`,body:[`So here's where my head goes first. I've got two worker services — a publisher and a subscriber — both freshly scaffolded from the .NET Worker template, dutifully logging to the console every second. The obvious lazy move is: publisher makes an HTTP call straight to the subscriber. Done, right? Except now I need to know the subscriber's address, its health, how many instances it scaled to at 3 AM, and I've welded the two services together at the hip.`,`Publish/subscribe flips that around. I publish to a channel name and walk away — I don't know or care who's listening. And here's the part that surprises people: you don't need RabbitMQ or Kafka for this. Redis, the thing you already run as a cache, has first-class pub/sub support. Channels behave like topics in a message broker — publish once, and every subscriber attached to that channel receives its own copy.`,`So the question isn't 'can Redis do this?' It's 'what am I trading away by using the lightest possible fan-out instead of a real broker?' Hold that thought — we'll come back to it in block four.`],quote:`Pub/sub is how services talk without knowing each other's phone numbers.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Direct HTTP call`,`Simple, request/response, easy to debug`,`Tight coupling, needs addresses, breaks on scale-out`,`One-off internal calls`],[`Redis pub/sub channel`,`Loose coupling, fan-out for free, Redis you already run`,`Fire-and-forget, no persistence, no acks`,`Live signals: cache invalidation, presence`],[`Full message broker (RabbitMQ)`,`Durable queues, acks, retries, routing`,`Another system to operate and monitor`,`Anything you'd mourn losing`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Publish Subscribe Messaging In .NET With Redis Channels`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4UP6L8cDBN8`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: multiplexer, subscriber, one channel constant`,body:[`Implementation time — and this is where the trade-offs actually live. First move on both services: install StackExchange.Redis. Everything hangs off a ConnectionMultiplexer — that's your pooled connection to the Redis instance. It's expensive to create and thread-safe, so you build it once per process and treat it like a database connection pool, not a disposable object.`,`From the multiplexer, connection.GetSubscriber() hands you an ISubscriber — that's your pub/sub handle. On the publisher side, inside the ExecuteAsync loop, it's just subscriber.PublishAsync(channel, message). The channel name lives in a constant — 'test-channel' — because a typo'd channel string doesn't throw an exception, it silently publishes into the void. There's an optional CommandFlags parameter if you want to tweak behavior; leave it default and move on.`,`The subscriber side is the same two objects with the mirror-image call: SubscribeAsync(channel, handler), where the handler is a delegate receiving the channel and the message. Inside that delegate you do whatever you want — log it, invalidate a cache, trigger a job. One multiplexer, one handler per channel. That's the entire handshake.`],quote:`One multiplexer per process, one handler per channel — that's the entire handshake.`,table:{headers:[`API call`,`Side`,`What it does`,`Gotcha`],rows:[[`ConnectionMultiplexer.Connect`,`Both`,`Opens the pooled connection to Redis`,`Expensive — create once, reuse forever`],[`GetSubscriber()`,`Both`,`Returns the ISubscriber pub/sub handle`,`Cheap — derive it from the multiplexer`],[`PublishAsync(channel, message)`,`Publisher`,`Fires a message into the channel`,`Fire-and-forget — no ack comes back`],[`SubscribeAsync(channel, handler)`,`Subscriber`,`Registers the delegate receiving (channel, message)`,`Only receives while connected`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Publish Subscribe Messaging In .NET With Redis Channels`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4UP6L8cDBN8`,at:`3:28`}},{heading:`03 · The demo that sells it: two subscribers, one channel`,body:[`To make any of this real you need a Redis instance, and I refuse to make that hard: docker run with a container name, a port mapping, and the redis image, and you've got a broker running locally in under a minute. No config file, no install wizard. Both workers point their multiplexers at it and we're live.`,`Start the solution and watch the console: the publisher fires 'test message' into the channel every five seconds, and the subscriber logs 'received message' with the channel and payload. Now the money shot — launch a second subscriber instance. Both of them get every single message. That's the topic semantics doing their job: full fan-out to every live listener, no routing keys, no consumer groups, no queue bindings to configure.`,`This is the cheapest fan-out you will ever build, and that's genuinely its superpower. But notice what the demo quietly assumed: that every subscriber was up and connected the whole time. Let's break that assumption on purpose.`],quote:`Channels behave like topics: every live listener gets a copy, every dead listener gets nothing.`,table:{headers:[`Listeners on channel`,`What happens`,`Ops burden`],rows:[[`1 subscriber`,`Receives every published message`,`Trivial`],[`N subscribers`,`Each gets its own copy — full fan-out`,`Still trivial — zero config`],[`0 subscribers`,`Message evaporates silently`,`None — and that is the trap`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Publish Subscribe Messaging In .NET With Redis Channels`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4UP6L8cDBN8`,at:`5:21`}},{heading:`04 · The fine print: fire-and-forget, and when to graduate`,body:[`Now the part I wish someone had told me at 11:20 during end-of-month billing. Redis pub/sub is fire-and-forget. No persistence, no acknowledgements, no replay. If a subscriber is restarting, or the network blips for a second, every message published in that window is simply gone — Redis doesn't queue them up for later. The channel is a live wire, not a mailbox.`,`So why not just use something durable everywhere? Because durability costs you: Redis Streams add consumer groups and replay but change your programming model, and a broker like RabbitMQ adds a whole system to operate. Pub/sub is unbeatable for signals where the next message heals you — cache invalidation, presence, live dashboards, config refresh. If you miss one invalidation, the next one fixes it, and a short cache TTL backstops the gap.`,`The discipline is knowing which contract you're holding before you bet on it. A lost 'refresh your cache' ping is an annoyance. A lost 'charge this card' event is an incident. Pick the mechanism to match the blast radius.`],quote:`Pub/sub delivers to whoever is listening right now — and to no one else, ever.`,table:{headers:[`Mechanism`,`Persistence`,`Delivery guarantee`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Redis Pub/Sub`,`None`,`At-most-once to connected listeners`,`Live signals, cache invalidation`],[`Redis Streams`,`Yes, in-memory log`,`At-least-once with consumer groups`,`Event history, replayable feeds`],[`RabbitMQ`,`Durable queues`,`At-least-once with acks and retries`,`Orders, payments, anything critical`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Publish Subscribe Messaging In .NET With Redis Channels`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4UP6L8cDBN8`,at:`6:56`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Fan-out topology: one publisher, N subscribers, one channel`,caption:`Both workers hold a ConnectionMultiplexer; the publisher's ISubscriber fires into the channel, Redis pushes a copy to every connected subscriber handler.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph PUB["Publisher Worker Service"]
        W["BackgroundService<br/>ExecuteAsync loop"] --> M["ConnectionMultiplexer"] --> IS["ISubscriber"]
    end
    subgraph REDIS["Redis in Docker"]
        CH[("test-channel")]
    end
    subgraph SUBS["Subscriber Worker Services"]
        SA["Subscriber A<br/>SubscribeAsync handler"]
        SB["Subscriber B<br/>SubscribeAsync handler"]
    end
    IS -- "PublishAsync every 5s" --> CH
    CH -- "push copy" --> SA
    CH -- "push copy" --> SB`},{id:`sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Message lifecycle — including the message nobody receives`,caption:`Subscribe first, then publish: every live listener gets a push. A subscriber that is down or restarting at publish time simply misses the message — Redis does not buffer it.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant P as Publisher Worker
    participant R as Redis in Docker
    participant A as Subscriber A
    participant B as Subscriber B
    A->>R: SubscribeAsync test-channel handler
    B->>R: SubscribeAsync test-channel handler
    loop every 5 seconds
        P->>R: PublishAsync test-channel test message
        R-->>A: push message
        R-->>B: push message
    end
    Note over A: A restarts mid-loop
    P->>R: PublishAsync test-channel test message
    R-->>B: push message
    Note over P,R: Message published while A is down is lost forever — fire and forget`}],takeaways:[`Create one ConnectionMultiplexer per process and reuse it — it's thread-safe and pools sockets; building one per publish call can explode into hundreds of TCP connections under load.`,`Treat Redis pub/sub as at-most-once: a subscriber down for even one second loses every message published in that window — always pair it with a reconciliation backstop or a short cache TTL.`,`Define channel names as shared constants across services — a typo'd channel string doesn't throw, it silently publishes into the void.`,`docker run -d --name redis-pubsub -p 6379:6379 redis gets you a working message broker in under 60 seconds — no install, no config file.`,`Pub/sub is for signals where the next message heals you (cache invalidation, presence, dashboards); the moment a lost message costs money, graduate to Redis Streams or RabbitMQ for acks and replay.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Publish Subscribe Messaging In .NET With Redis Channels`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4UP6L8cDBN8`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Wul0NlAj5fA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:3,title:`How to Scale SignalR in .NET with Redis`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:29`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`SignalR`,`Azure`,`.NET`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The Night Half Our Users Saw Ghost Orders`,paragraphs:[`16:45 on a pre-release Friday. Our order-tracking dashboard lights up with tickets: "My order says Processing, but the courier app says it was delivered an hour ago." We had two SignalR app servers behind a load balancer, and a routine config deploy had quietly dropped the sticky session rule. Clients started hopping between servers mid-session — and each server keeps its own in-memory connection map.`,`The logs were maddening. The update endpoint on server B called the hub context, the call succeeded, and zero clients received anything. Server B had never met those connections — it was a complete SignalR application that didn't know the other one existed. We didn't need more servers. We needed the servers we had to talk to each other.`,`The fix was a Redis backplane: every hub message published once, fanned out to every instance. But that night taught us the second lesson the hard way — the moment you add a backplane, its network latency becomes your new limiting factor for everything you call 'real-time'.`],problem:`Scaling SignalR across instances splits the connection map — messages published on one server silently never reach clients connected to another, and once you add a backplane, its network latency becomes the ceiling on your real-time experience.`},tech:{stack:[`SignalR`,`Redis Backplane`,`.NET 10`,`Azure SignalR`,`Sticky Sessions`,`OpenTelemetry`],note:`SignalR hubs on each .NET instance keep local connection maps; Redis pub/sub acts as the backplane that fans every hub message out to all instances, with Azure SignalR Service as the managed alternative.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why one server is a beautiful lie`,body:[`Let's be honest about why we love SignalR: it abstracts WebSockets for us, falls back to server-sent events, and degrades to polling as a last resort. It manages connection lifetimes for every connected client. On a single box, it's magic — you inject a hub context, call Clients.User, done.`,`But here's the trap. That connection map lives in memory, on that one server. Put a second instance behind a load balancer and you now have two SignalR applications that don't know about each other and can't communicate. Worse: without session affinity, the same client can land on different servers for different requests. Your 'real-time' feature is now real-time for some users, some of the time.`],quote:`SignalR doesn't scale by adding servers — it scales by teaching servers to talk.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single server`,`Zero extra infra, simplest mental model`,`Single point of failure, hard capacity ceiling`,`Prototypes, small internal tools`],[`Sticky sessions only`,`No code changes`,`Doesn't fix cross-server sends; fragile under deploys`,`Never sufficient alone`],[`Redis backplane`,`Reuses existing Redis, full fan-out`,`You own latency and Redis ops`,`Self-hosted scale-out`],[`Azure SignalR Service`,`Managed, same principles apply`,`Cost, vendor coupling`,`Azure-native teams`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Scale SignalR in .NET with Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wul0NlAj5fA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what the backplane actually moves`,body:[`So we introduce a backplane — Redis in our case. Here's the part people get wrong: the backplane does not take over your connections. Every app server still owns connection management for the clients physically connected to it. That part doesn't change at all.`,`What changes is message flow. When any server publishes a hub message, it goes through the backplane, and Redis fans it out to every instance — each instance then delivers to whichever of its own local connections match. One publish, everywhere. And if you'd rather not run Redis yourself, Azure SignalR Service follows many of the same principles. We picked Redis partly because this app already uses it for caching — one dependency, two jobs.`],quote:`The backplane doesn't move your connections — it moves your messages.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Stays local`,`Goes through backplane`],rows:[[`Connection lifetime`,`Yes — per server`,`No`],[`Negotiate / transport setup`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Hub message fan-out`,`No`,`Yes — every publish`],[`Group/user targeting`,`Resolved locally on delivery`,`Message itself is broadcast`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Scale SignalR in .NET with Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wul0NlAj5fA`,at:`1:15`}},{heading:`03 · The latency tax you can't refactor away`,body:[`Now the uncomfortable truth: network latency to the backplane is your limiting factor for real-time. Every message now takes a detour — publish to Redis, fan out, deliver. If your app servers aren't close to the backplane — ideally the same machine, at minimum the same data center — you're adding hops to every single real-time message.`,`Push it far enough and you defeat the entire purpose of using SignalR. A chat message that arrives 400ms late isn't real-time anymore, it's just slow polling with extra infrastructure. This matters specifically when your app servers are also your SignalR servers — which is the common case.`],quote:`Real-time is a latency budget, and the backplane spends first.`,table:{headers:[`Placement`,`Added latency per message`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Same host as app server`,`Sub-millisecond`,`Ideal`],[`Same data center / AZ`,`Low single-digit ms`,`Fine for most real-time UX`],[`Cross-region to Redis`,`Tens to hundreds of ms`,`Defeats the purpose — don't`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Scale SignalR in .NET with Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wul0NlAj5fA`,at:`3:29`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it up: IHubContext in a .NET 10 Web API`,body:[`Enough theory — how does this land in code? SignalR comes installed by default in a .NET 10 Web API; you enable it with builder.Services.AddSignalR(). Define your hub, decorate it with [Authorize] so it's protected, and use a strongly-typed client interface so your pushes are compile-checked instead of magic strings.`,`Then, anywhere in your backend, inject IHubContext via dependency injection. In our order flow, the update-status endpoint grabs the hub context and calls Clients.User(userId), sending the updated order straight to that one user. SignalR maintains the client connections; this lets us reach a specific client any time we want, from any layer of the backend.`],quote:`The hub defines the contract; IHubContext is how the rest of your backend whispers to it.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Scale SignalR in .NET with Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wul0NlAj5fA`,at:`4:36`}},{heading:`05 · Proving fan-out: stamp every push with an instance ID`,body:[`Here's the observability trick I now refuse to ship without: every message we push carries an instance ID, pulled from the OpenTelemetry attribute service.instance.id. The client UI can show exactly which server delivered the update.`,`Why do we care? Because 'the backplane is configured' and 'the backplane is working' are different claims. When the split-brain incident happened, those instance IDs turned a multi-hour mystery into a five-minute diagnosis — we could see server B publishing while clients sat on server A. If you can't see which instance sent it, you can't prove the fan-out works in production.`],quote:`If you can't see which instance sent it, you can't prove the backplane works.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Scale SignalR in .NET with Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wul0NlAj5fA`,at:`6:00`}}],diagrams:[{id:`backplane-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Scaled SignalR topology with a Redis backplane`,caption:`Connections stay local per server; only hub messages traverse the Redis pub/sub fan-out.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    U1["Browser 1"] -->|WebSocket| LB["Load Balancer"]
    U2["Browser 2"] -->|WebSocket| LB
    U3["Browser 3"] -->|WebSocket| LB
    LB --> S1["SignalR Server 1<br/>local connection map"]
    LB --> S2["SignalR Server 2<br/>local connection map"]
    S1 <-->|pub/sub fan-out| RB[("Redis Backplane")]
    S2 <-->|pub/sub fan-out| RB
    S1 -.->|delivers to own clients| U1
    S2 -.->|delivers to own clients| U2`},{id:`message-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One order update, fanned out to every instance`,caption:`The endpoint publishes once via IHubContext; the backplane guarantees every server gets the message, but only the server holding the user's connection delivers it.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as "Update Status Endpoint"
    participant Ctx as IHubContext
    participant Redis as Redis Backplane
    participant S1 as Server 1
    participant S2 as Server 2
    participant U as User Connection
    API->>Ctx: Update order status
    Ctx->>Redis: Publish OrderUpdated(order, instanceId)
    Redis->>S1: Fan-out message
    Redis->>S2: Fan-out message
    S1->>U: OrderUpdated(order, instanceId)
    Note over S2: User not connected here — no-op`}],takeaways:[`One SignalR instance means one in-memory connection map — the moment you run two, you need a backplane, or messages published on server B silently never reach clients pinned to server A.`,`Place your app servers and the Redis backplane in the same data center — ideally the same host — because backplane network latency is the hard floor on every 'real-time' message you send.`,`Connection management stays local per server even with a backplane; only hub messages traverse Redis, so size the backplane for message throughput, not connection count.`,`Inject IHubContext with a strongly-typed client interface and target Clients.User(userId) for per-user pushes; keep every hub locked down with [Authorize].`,`Stamp every push with service.instance.id from OpenTelemetry so your dashboards can prove fan-out across instances — that's how you catch a split-brain in minutes instead of hours.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How to Scale SignalR in .NET with Redis`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wul0NlAj5fA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`realtime-channels`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Oiepl-LLfIs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:4,title:`Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 9 (full tutorial)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 15:28`,minutes:15,difficulty:3,tags:[`SignalR`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The Dashboard That DDoS'd Itself: Killing the 5-Second Poll`,paragraphs:[`Black Friday eve. Our order ops dashboard has every support agent watching a wall of order cards, and every single card polls GET /orders/{id} every 5 seconds. Four hundred open cards means 80 requests per second before a single order changes. Then marketing fires off a 'flash sale' email, concurrent viewers triple, the API connection pool saturates, and p99 on the write path — the PUT that updates order status — climbs from 40ms to 4 seconds. The tool built to watch the fire became the fire.`,`At 3 AM the picture was absurd: Redis CPU flat at 3%, API pegged at 100%. I diffed two consecutive poll responses for the same order — byte-identical, and it stayed that way 99% of the time. We were paying full HTTP cost (auth, serialization, cache read, GC) to learn 'no change.' Caching harder wouldn't fix this. The direction of the data flow was wrong.`,`The fix took one afternoon. A sealed OrderNotificationHub extending HubBase, one persistent WebSocket per browser, and an IHubContext<OrderNotificationHub> injected into the PUT endpoint that fires SendAsync("SendOrderStatusUpdate", order) the moment the Redis write commits. Poll traffic went to zero, updates landed in under 100ms, and the WebSocket frames tab in DevTools became our new favorite window.`],problem:`Polling pays the full cost of an HTTP request to hear 'nothing changed' — and at scale, the monitoring dashboard becomes the outage.`},tech:{stack:[`SignalR`,`.NET 9`,`HubBase`,`IHubContext`,`IDistributedCache`,`Redis`,`WebSocket`,`Hub Groups`],note:`SignalR provides the persistent push channel — HubBase for lifecycle and group membership, IHubContext to broadcast from Minimal API endpoints — while Redis behind IDistributedCache holds the order state those pushes describe.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why dumb polling fails`,body:[`Let's do the uncomfortable math first. A 5-second poll isn't 'nearly real-time' — it's a tax that scales with your audience, not with your data. Four hundred open order cards is 80 requests per second before a single order changes. Triple the viewers on sale day and you've tripled load to learn... nothing. Request rate is a function of how many people are watching, and the number of watchers is unbounded.`,`So why not just shorten the interval? Latency gets better, waste gets worse. Why not long-polling? Better, but you're still holding connections open, eating reconnect churn, and hand-rolling half of what SignalR gives you for free: transport negotiation (WebSockets first, SSE and long-polling as fallback), automatic reconnection, and an RPC-ish surface with SendAsync. On .NET 9 this is a few lines of middleware and one hub class.`,`The mental flip I want you to make: stop asking 'how often should the client check?' and start asking 'when does the server know something changed?' The server always knows first — it just committed the change. Push from there.`],quote:`Polling scales with your audience. Pushing scales with your changes. Only one of those is finite.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Polling (5s)`,`Dead simple, survives any firewall`,`Wasted requests scale with viewers; stale by up to the interval`,`Prototypes only`],[`Long-polling`,`Works without WebSockets`,`Held connections, reconnect churn, hand-rolled state`,`Legacy fallback`],[`SignalR push`,`Sub-100ms updates, one connection per client, auto-reconnect`,`Hub infra + sticky sessions when scaling out`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 9 (full tutorial)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oiepl-LLfIs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the Hub is a contract, not a controller`,body:[`Here's the entire server-side setup: a Hubs folder, a public sealed OrderNotificationHub extending HubBase. That base class hands you OnConnectedAsync and OnDisconnectedAsync — your lifecycle hooks for presence, logging, and, crucially, group membership. You can also define arbitrary methods clients invoke, like SendOrderStatusUpdate(Order order) — but notice something: in this app that method stays empty, because the data flows server-to-client.`,`People get weird about the empty hub, like it's dead code. It's not — it's the endpoint the WebSocket connects to and the type your IHubContext is keyed on. The two handles that actually matter live on the base class: Clients, an IHubClients for addressing connections, and Groups, an IGroupManager for routing. Everything else is plumbing.`,`And resist the urge to put business logic in the hub. A hub instance is per-invocation, short-lived, tied to one connection. Your endpoints and services own the workflow; the hub is just the mouth.`],quote:`An empty hub is a feature: the server talks, the client listens, and nobody pretends the hub is a controller.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 9 (full tutorial)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oiepl-LLfIs`,at:`2:12`}},{heading:`03 · The IHubContext escape hatch`,body:[`Now the question everyone asks: the update happens in my PUT endpoint, not in the hub — how do I talk to connected clients from there? You can't grab the hub instance; it exists per connection and your endpoint has no idea which one. The built-in answer is IHubContext<OrderNotificationHub>: inject it into the endpoint and it exposes the same Clients and Groups surface.`,`So the flow in PUT /orders/{id}/status is: write the new status through IDistributedCache to Redis, and only after that write succeeds, call hubContext.Clients...SendAsync("SendOrderStatusUpdate", order). Order matters. Push before the commit and the write fails, and you've told a hundred browsers a fact that isn't true — now the UI and the database disagree, the exact class of bug polling accidentally avoided.`,`One nuance: SendAsync is effectively fire-and-forget from your request path's perspective, but don't fire-and-forget the error handling. An unobserved hub exception shouldn't fail the write that already succeeded — but it also shouldn't vanish silently. Log it.`],quote:`Push after the commit. A notification about data you didn't save is a lie with good timing.`,table:{headers:[`Push origin`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Hub method`,`Direct access to Clients/Groups`,`Only reachable from client calls; wrong direction here`,`Client-to-server flows`],[`IHubContext in endpoint`,`Push exactly where state changes; singleton`,`Event name is a magic string`,`Default choice`],[`BackgroundService + IHubContext`,`Timed or streamed pushes off the request path`,`Extra infra for marginal cases`,`Scheduled jobs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 9 (full tutorial)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oiepl-LLfIs`,at:`4:37`}},{heading:`04 · Groups: send it to who's watching`,body:[`The demo uses Clients.All and it works great — until two people have the app open. Every order status update goes to every browser, and each client drops 99% of the frames it paid bandwidth for. Worse: if your payload is the full order, you've just leaked every order to every connected user. That's not an optimization problem, that's an authorization bug.`,`The fix is groups, and the right time to join them is OnConnectedAsync: Groups.AddToGroupAsync(Context.ConnectionId, "order-{orderId}") when the client tells you which order it's watching — or a per-tenant group. Then the PUT endpoint pushes to Clients.Group("order-{id}") and the message routes to exactly the cards that care. Server-side routing, one line from your code's perspective.`,`Why not filter client-side? Because filtering after receipt still means sending, and sending means leaking. Route at the server.`],quote:`Broadcast is a demo feature. Groups are the production feature.`,table:{headers:[`Addressing`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Clients.All`,`One line, great for demos`,`Leaks data, wastes bandwidth`,`Global announcements`],[`Clients.Group(name)`,`Server-side routing, per-entity`,`Must manage join/leave lifecycle`,`Per-order, per-tenant`],[`Clients.Client(connId)`,`Precise and private`,`You must track connection IDs`,`Direct replies`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 9 (full tutorial)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oiepl-LLfIs`,at:`7:36`}},{heading:`05 · Serialization and the wire: reading frames at 3 AM`,body:[`Last mile: the wire. SignalR serializes with System.Text.Json, camelCase by default, so the Order object you hand SendAsync is exactly the JSON the client receives — your DTO is your API. Keep it lean. Remember the original gotcha: when you build these values server-side, some of them can be null. Don't ship an entity full of null fields the client ignores; project to a small OrderStatusUpdate record.`,`And learn to love DevTools: Network tab, click the orderhub WebSocket, Messages — you will literally watch every SendAsync frame fly. If the UI doesn't update but the frame is there, it's a client-side handler name mismatch, because 'SendOrderStatusUpdate' is a string contract with no compiler on either end. If the frame isn't there, your push never fired — check that the group name matches what you joined in OnConnectedAsync.`,`Also know your transports: negotiate picks WebSockets first and falls back to SSE or long-polling on hostile networks. Same API surface either way, so you write it once.`],quote:`The event name in SendAsync is a string contract — the WS frames tab in DevTools is your compiler.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 9 (full tutorial)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oiepl-LLfIs`,at:`9:50`}}],diagrams:[{id:`architecture`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Push topology: REST for state, WebSocket for news`,caption:`Clients hold one persistent WebSocket to the hub while REST endpoints own the data; the PUT endpoint pushes through IHubContext only after Redis confirms the write.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Browser["Browser Client"]
        UI["Order Dashboard UI"]
        WS["orderHub WebSocket"]
    end

    subgraph Backend["ASP.NET Core Backend (.NET 9)"]
        HUB["OrderNotificationHub : HubBase"]
        CTX["IHubContext for OrderNotificationHub"]
        POST["POST /orders"]
        PUT["PUT /orders/{id}/status"]
        GET["GET /orders/{id}"]
        CACHE["IDistributedCache"]
    end

    REDIS[("Redis")]

    UI -->|"HTTP"| GET
    UI ---|"persistent connection"| WS
    WS --- HUB
    POST --> CACHE
    PUT --> CACHE
    GET --> CACHE
    CACHE --> REDIS
    PUT -->|"push after commit"| CTX
    CTX -->|"Clients.Group(...).SendAsync"| HUB
    HUB -->|"SendOrderStatusUpdate(order)"| WS`},{id:`update-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Life of a status update`,caption:`The full request path for one status change: persist first, then route the push to exactly the group watching that order.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Browser Dashboard
    participant H as OrderNotificationHub
    participant E as PUT status endpoint
    participant R as Redis via IDistributedCache

    Note over C,H: OnConnectedAsync joins group "order-{id}"
    C->>H: negotiate + WebSocket connect
    H-->>C: connection established

    C->>E: PUT /orders/{id}/status = Shipped
    E->>R: write new status
    R-->>E: write confirmed
    E->>H: IHubContext.Clients.Group("order-{id}")
    H-->>C: SendAsync("SendOrderStatusUpdate", order)
    C-->>C: UI updates — no reload, no poll`}],takeaways:[`Retire any poll interval under 15 seconds: 10,000 clients polling every 5s is 2,000 req/s of reads that say 'no change' — one SignalR WebSocket per client drops that to near zero when nothing happens.`,`Never inject the Hub itself into your endpoints — inject IHubContext<T> (a singleton) and call Clients/Groups exactly where the state change commits.`,`Join every connection to a group in OnConnectedAsync (e.g. order-{id}) and push to the group: broadcasting to All when 1% of clients care wastes 99% of your frames and can leak data.`,`Push only after the cache/DB write confirms, and ship a lean DTO of a few hundred bytes — not the full entity with null fields the client ignores.`,`Debug at the wire: DevTools → Network → hub WebSocket → Messages shows every SendAsync frame; a silent UI is almost always an event-name string mismatch, because that contract has no compiler.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 9 (full tutorial)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oiepl-LLfIs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`realtime-channels`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-92_320zm5Uo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:5,title:`Rate Limiting Minimal APIs and Controllers the Right Way`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:06`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`Rate limiter`],story:{headline:`The Night One Cron Job 429'd Four Hundred Users`,paragraphs:[`10:00 at peak checkout volume. PagerDuty lights up and the support inbox starts flooding: "your API is broken, everything says 429." The dashboard confirms it — the rate limiter is rejecting nearly half of all traffic, and no deploy has gone out in twelve hours.`,`Four minutes of log digging tells the real story: a single authenticated client — a partner's reporting cron job whose retry loop had gone feral — was responsible for over 90% of the requests. And our brand-new rate limiter, configured exactly like every tutorial shows, was global: one fixed window, one shared bucket of permits for the entire application. That one client was drinking the whole reservoir while four hundred innocent users queued up behind it, catching 429s for sins they didn't commit.`,`The fix took twenty minutes and didn't involve raising a single number. We kept the same budget — five requests per minute — but partitioned it per authenticated user. The feral cron got throttled into its own lane; nobody else ever saw a 429 again. The lesson stuck: the limiter was never the hard part. Deciding what a "unit of fairness" is — and wiring it through two different mechanisms for Minimal APIs and controllers — is.`],problem:`A global rate limiter is one shared permit bucket for the whole app: a single misbehaving authenticated user drains it and every other user gets throttled. The fix is partitioning the limiter per authenticated identity — which only works if authentication runs before the rate limiter in the pipeline — and wiring that one policy through two different doors: RequireRateLimiting on minimal API endpoints, [EnableRateLimiting] attributes on controllers.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`RateLimiter middleware`,`Fixed Window`,`JWT Bearer`,`Minimal APIs`,`MVC Controllers`,`PartitionedRateLimiter`,`dotnet user-jwts`],note:`ASP.NET Core's rate limiting middleware running a fixed-window algorithm, partitioned per user from JWT bearer claims, applied to both Minimal API endpoints and controllers, with dotnet user-jwts minting throwaway test tokens.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why global rate limiting fails`,body:[`Here's the thing every tutorial gets backwards. You Google '.NET rate limiting', copy the snippet — AddRateLimiter, a fixed window, five requests per minute — and it works beautifully in the demo. Then it hits production and it turns out to be a single shared bucket for every caller. One user's retry loop, one aggressive scraper, and suddenly your legitimate users are collecting 429s for a sin they didn't commit.`,`The first instinct is 'just raise the limit.' Don't. If the noisy client is doing 90% of the traffic, raising the global cap from 100 to 1,000 gives the abuser 900 extra requests and your normal users exactly the same experience. The math never works. The unit of fairness has to be the authenticated user — same budget, but a private bucket per identity.`,`And that's the reframe I want you to sit with: rate limiting isn't about protecting your server from too much traffic. It's about distributing a scarce resource fairly between callers. Global limits distribute it to whoever shouts loudest. Per-user limits distribute it evenly.`],quote:`A global rate limit is a shared piggy bank — the loudest user empties it and everyone else pays.`,table:{headers:[`Partitioning`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Global (one bucket)`,`Trivial to configure`,`One noisy user starves everyone`,`Never for multi-user APIs`],[`Per IP address`,`No auth required`,`NAT and office users share one bucket`,`Anonymous public endpoints`],[`Per user identity`,`Fair; throttles abusers precisely`,`Requires auth to run before the limiter`,`Authenticated APIs — the default`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Rate Limiting Minimal APIs and Controllers the Right Way`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=92_320zm5Uo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the pipeline: identity before limits`,body:[`Once you decide to partition per user, you've signed up for a pipeline constraint that bites people constantly: the limiter needs HttpContext.User to be populated. That means UseAuthentication has to run before UseRateLimiter. If your limiter sits in front of auth, every request looks anonymous — and anonymous is one giant partition, so you're back to a global limit with extra steps.`,`Walk the order with me: the request arrives, authentication middleware validates the JWT and populates the user, authorization rejects anyone without access, and only then does the rate limiter see a real identity it can partition on. Notice what falls out of that ordering for free: a request with no token dies with a 401 before the limiter ever counts it, and a request with a valid token but no permission dies with a 403. The limiter only ever judges authenticated, authorized callers.`,`This is also why 'why not just rate limit at the reverse proxy?' is only half an answer. NGINX or your API gateway can do per-IP beautifully, but it can't see your JWT claims unless you teach it to. The per-user partition lives where the identity lives — inside the app.`],quote:`If your limiter runs before your auth, every user looks anonymous — and anonymous is one giant partition.`,table:{headers:[`Position`,`Middleware`,`Why it must sit here`],rows:[[`1st`,`UseAuthentication`,`Validates the JWT and populates HttpContext.User`],[`2nd`,`UseAuthorization`,`Rejects forbidden callers before spending limiter capacity`],[`3rd`,`UseRateLimiter`,`Partitions on the now-populated user identity`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Rate Limiting Minimal APIs and Controllers the Right Way`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=92_320zm5Uo`,at:`0:40`}},{heading:`03 · Picking the algorithm: fixed window and its dirty secret`,body:[`AddRateLimiter hands you four algorithms: fixed window, sliding window, token bucket, and concurrency. For a first per-user policy I reach for fixed window — say five permits per sixty-second window. It's the cheapest to reason about, the cheapest in memory per partition, and for read endpoints it's almost always good enough.`,`But be honest about its dirty secret: window boundaries. A client that fires five requests at 11:59.9 and five more at 12:00.1 just pushed ten requests through in two hundred milliseconds, and your limiter called both batches legal. Sliding window smooths that out at the cost of tracking more state per partition. Token bucket is what you want when legitimate clients have bursty shapes — it lets them spend saved-up permits in a burst, then refills at a steady rate.`,`My rule: fixed window until you can name the client behavior that hurts, then upgrade just that one policy. Don't pay sliding-window memory costs across ten thousand user partitions because one partner might someday burst.`],quote:`Fixed windows are cheap until two windows kiss — then your 'five per minute' just allowed ten in two seconds.`,table:{headers:[`Algorithm`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Per-user fit`],rows:[[`Fixed window`,`Cheapest, simplest to reason about`,`Boundary burst: 2x limit across a window edge`,`Great default for read endpoints`],[`Sliding window`,`Smooths boundary bursts`,`More memory per partition`,`Fair APIs with steady traffic`],[`Token bucket`,`Allows controlled bursts`,`Refill tuning is fiddly`,`Bursty clients and webhooks`],[`Concurrency`,`Caps in-flight requests`,`Doesn't limit rate over time`,`Long-running or expensive endpoints`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Rate Limiting Minimal APIs and Controllers the Right Way`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=92_320zm5Uo`,at:`1:32`}},{heading:`04 · One policy, two doors: Minimal APIs vs Controllers`,body:[`Here's the asymmetry that trips everyone: you define the policy exactly once — AddRateLimiter with a named policy like 'per-user', built on a PartitionedRateLimiter that keys off the user's identity claim. But then you attach it through two different doors depending on the surface. Minimal API endpoints chain .RequireRateLimiting("per-user") onto the route. Controllers get the [EnableRateLimiting("per-user")] attribute on the class or the action.`,`And there are gotchas on both doors. RequireRateLimiting is opt-in per endpoint — add a shiny new minimal API endpoint next quarter and forget the chain, and it silently has no limit at all. The controller attribute on a class applies to every action inside it, which is usually what you want but occasionally very much not. And your partitioned limiter needs a fallback for an empty or anonymous identity, because something will always slip through unauthenticated.`,`The mental model I keep: the policy is the law, written once. RequireRateLimiting and EnableRateLimiting are just the two different ways the law gets served in the two courtrooms of minimal APIs and controllers.`],quote:`One policy, two doors: RequireRateLimiting for endpoints, EnableRateLimiting for controllers.`,table:{headers:[`Surface`,`Wiring`,`Gotcha`],rows:[[`Minimal API endpoint`,`.RequireRateLimiting("per-user")`,`Opt-in per route — new endpoints silently have no limit`],[`Controller / action`,`[EnableRateLimiting("per-user")]`,`Class-level attribute covers every action — check overrides`],[`Policy definition`,`AddRateLimiter + PartitionedRateLimiter on user claim`,`Needs a fallback partition for anonymous identity`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Rate Limiting Minimal APIs and Controllers the Right Way`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=92_320zm5Uo`,at:`6:31`}},{heading:`05 · Proving it with throwaway JWTs`,body:[`Last piece — proving it. Hit the endpoint with no token and you get a 401, which is correct but tells you nothing about the limiter. So how do you get a token without spinning up an identity server at 3 AM? dotnet user-jwts create. It's a little CLI that mints throwaway, dev-only JWTs and wires them into your app's development config. Two minutes, zero infrastructure.`,`Then script the dance: five requests with token A — five 200s. The sixth in the same window — 429. A request with token B — 200, because user two has a fresh partition nobody touched. That last step is the whole point: it's the proof that you built fairness, not just a throttle. If your test only checks the 429, you've verified the cage but not that the cages are separate.`,`And this belongs in CI, not in a Postman collection on someone's laptop. The day someone refactors the middleware order and auth lands after the limiter, this test fails loudly instead of your users failing silently.`],quote:`If you can't script the 401 → 200×5 → 429 dance, you don't have a rate limit — you have a hope.`,table:{headers:[`Step`,`Command / Request`,`Expected`],rows:[[`Mint dev token`,`dotnet user-jwts create`,`JWT bound to development config`],[`No token`,`GET /weather`,`401 Unauthorized`],[`Burst x5`,`GET /weather with JWT A`,`200 OK each`],[`6th call`,`GET /weather, same JWT, same window`,`429 Too Many Requests`],[`Second user`,`GET /weather with JWT B`,`200 OK — fresh partition`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Per-user rate limiting in the request pipeline`,caption:`Authentication populates the identity, authorization filters it, and only then can the rate limiter partition fairly per user.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Client with JWT bearer"] --> B["Authentication middleware"]
    B -- "invalid or missing token" --> X["401 Unauthorized"]
    B -- "identity populated" --> C["Authorization middleware"]
    C -- "forbidden" --> Z["403 Forbidden"]
    C -- "allowed" --> D["Rate limiter middleware"]
    D --> E{"Partition by user identity"}
    E -- "permits remaining in window" --> F["Minimal API endpoint / Controller"]
    E -- "window exhausted" --> Y["429 Too Many Requests"]
    F --> G["200 OK"]`},{id:`per-user-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One window, two users: the partition proof`,caption:`User-1 exhausts their five permits and gets a 429 while user-2 sails through on a fresh partition — the exact behavior a global limiter cannot give you.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant RL as RateLimiter middleware
    participant EP as Weather endpoint
    C->>RL: GET /weather (no token)
    RL-->>C: 401 Unauthorized
    C->>RL: GET /weather (JWT user-1)
    RL->>RL: partition user-1 — consume permit 1 of 5
    RL->>EP: forward request
    EP-->>C: 200 OK
    C->>RL: GET /weather x4 (JWT user-1)
    RL->>RL: permits 2 through 5 consumed
    EP-->>C: 200 OK x4
    C->>RL: GET /weather (6th call, same window)
    RL-->>C: 429 Too Many Requests
    C->>RL: GET /weather (JWT user-2)
    RL->>EP: fresh partition for user-2
    EP-->>C: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Partition by authenticated user, never globally — one noisy client doing 90% of the traffic will 429 every innocent user queued behind it.`,`Start with a fixed window of 5 permits per 60 seconds per user on read endpoints; only pay for sliding window or token bucket once you can name the burst you're defending against.`,`Pipeline order is load-bearing: UseAuthentication → UseAuthorization → UseRateLimiter, or every request collapses into one anonymous partition.`,`One policy, two doors: .RequireRateLimiting("per-user") chains onto minimal API endpoints, [EnableRateLimiting("per-user")] decorates controllers — audit both whenever you add a new surface.`,`Prove partitioning in CI with dotnet user-jwts create: script the 401 → 200×5 → 429 → second-token-200 sequence, so a middleware reorder fails a test instead of your users.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Rate Limiting Minimal APIs and Controllers the Right Way`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=92_320zm5Uo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`rate-limiting`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-PIfGHbvuAtM`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:6,title:`How To Make an IP Address Rate Limiter in ASP.NET Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 8:12`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`Rate limiter`],story:{headline:`The 2 AM scraper that taught us whose bucket gets emptied`,paragraphs:[`It was 2:14 AM when the pager went off. Our public /products endpoint — the one that runs a fat, unindexed join and hydrates a few megabytes of JSON — had the database pinned at 100% CPU. The access logs told the whole story: a single IP address firing requests in a tight loop, thousands per minute, like it was trying to win a race nobody else knew about.`,`We restarted the service. It came back. We scaled up. The scraper just scaled with us. The fix wasn't more hardware — it was a bouncer at the door. .NET 7 had just shipped built-in rate limiting, and we wired it up in about twenty minutes: AddRateLimiter to configure the policies, app.UseRateLimiter() to actually enforce them.`,`But our first attempt was almost worse than the outage. We reached for the global limiter — one shared bucket for every caller on the planet. The scraper blew through the quota in the first second of its loop, and every legitimate customer behind it got a 429. That night taught us the real lesson: the limiter has to know who's knocking. Partition by IP address, and the abusive client gets locked out while everyone else keeps shopping.`],problem:`A single shared rate limit bucket means one abusive client empties the quota for every legitimate user — the limiter must partition by caller, not just count requests.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`.NET 7`,`RateLimiter Middleware`,`Fixed Window`,`RateLimitPartition`,`Forwarded Headers`],note:`Two calls put rate limiting in the pipeline — AddRateLimiter registers the policies, UseRateLimiter enforces them — while RateLimitPartition turns one fixed window policy into an independent bucket per client IP.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just add the middleware' isn't enough`,body:[`Here's the entire onboarding ritual: call AddRateLimiter in your DI setup to configure RateLimiterOptions, then drop app.UseRateLimiter() into the pipeline. Two calls, that's it. But here's the trap I see constantly — people do the first, skip the second, and then swear rate limiting 'doesn't work.' The options object is just configuration; the middleware is the enforcement point. No middleware, no 429s, ever.`,`The next trap is the GlobalLimiter. It's sitting right there on the options object, it covers every endpoint, and it feels like a free win. But it's one shared bucket for every caller on the planet. Our scraper drained it in the first second, and every legitimate customer got rejected through no fault of their own. That's why I almost never use the global limiter on public APIs — I attach named policies to specific, expensive endpoints with RequireRateLimiting, and partition those policies by caller.`],quote:`A limiter with one bucket is a bouncer who clears the whole club because one guy got rowdy.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`No limiting`,`Zero code to write`,`One scraper can melt the database`,`Never, in production`],[`Global limiter`,`One line, covers every endpoint`,`Shared bucket — one abuser starves everyone`,`Internal tools, hard caps`],[`Partitioned by IP`,`Fair per-caller isolation`,`Shared-NAT users collide; proxies hide real IP`,`Public APIs — the default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make an IP Address Rate Limiter in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PIfGHbvuAtM`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Picking your poison: four limiter flavors`,body:[`.NET 7 ships four algorithms out of the box: Concurrency, Fixed Window, Sliding Window, and Token Bucket. For the products endpoint we started with the dumbest one — Fixed Window. You set a policy name, a PermitLimit, and a Window. Ten permits, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10), done. Ten requests per IP per ten seconds.`,`Why not start fancier? Because every extra knob is a new way to be wrong at 3 AM. Fixed window is O(1) memory per partition and trivially predictable. Its one honest weakness is the boundary burst: a client can spend all 10 permits at the tail of one window and all 10 again at the head of the next — 20 requests in an instant. Sliding window smooths that at the cost of tracking segments; token bucket gives you controlled bursts with a steady refill rate. Start simple, upgrade only when the math actually hurts.`],quote:`Pick the simplest limiter that stops the bleeding — you can always slide the window later.`,table:{headers:[`Algorithm`,`Strength`,`Weakness`,`Best fit`],rows:[[`Concurrency`,`Caps simultaneous in-flight requests`,`Doesn't limit request rate over time`,`Slow, expensive handlers`],[`Fixed window`,`Cheapest, simplest, predictable`,`2x burst at the window boundary`,`Coarse abuse protection`],[`Sliding window`,`Smooths boundary bursts`,`More memory and CPU to track segments`,`Fair public quotas`],[`Token bucket`,`Controlled bursts with steady refill`,`More knobs to tune correctly`,`Bursty-but-legit API clients`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make an IP Address Rate Limiter in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PIfGHbvuAtM`,at:`1:56`}},{heading:`03 · Walking the boundary: one bucket per IP`,body:[`Now the part that makes this an IP limiter instead of a global throttle: options.AddPolicy. You hand it the same policy name — 'fixed' — and a delegate that receives the HttpContext and returns a RateLimitPartition. Inside that delegate, the partition key is context.Connection.RemoteIpAddress. Same algorithm, same numbers — but now every unique IP address gets its own private bucket.`,`This is the mental model shift I want you to leave with: the policy name selects the algorithm and the numbers; the partition key selects who shares a bucket. Change the key from a constant to the remote IP, and you've gone from 'throttle the world' to 'throttle each caller.' And because the policy name is unchanged, the RequireRateLimiting("fixed") call on the products endpoint doesn't move an inch — the partitioned policy simply replaces the global one.`],quote:`The policy name picks the algorithm; the partition key picks the victims.`,table:{headers:[`Partition key`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`RemoteIpAddress`,`Zero config for direct clients`,`Wrong behind proxies; NAT users share a bucket`,`Direct-to-Kestrel public APIs`],[`X-Forwarded-For`,`Real client IP behind a proxy`,`Spoofable unless proxy is trusted`,`Behind your own reverse proxy`],[`User ID / API key`,`Survives IP changes`,`Unauthenticated callers have none`,`Authenticated endpoints`],[`IP + route`,`Per-endpoint quotas`,`More partitions, more memory`,`Mixed-cost endpoint surfaces`]]}},{heading:`04 · The part that bites at 3 AM: proxies, NAT, and naked 429s`,body:[`Here's what bites in production. The moment you put Nginx, a load balancer, or a CDN in front of Kestrel, RemoteIpAddress stops being your customer's IP — it's the proxy's IP. Congratulations: you've just put every human on the internet into one bucket. The fix is UseForwardedHeaders with KnownProxies locked to your own infrastructure, so the connection IP is rewritten to the real client before the limiter ever sees it.`,`Second bite: IP partitioning is fair to abusers and unfair to shared egress. An office behind one NAT, or a whole mobile carrier on CGNAT, shares a single 10-per-10-seconds bucket. So shape the rejection: configure OnRejected to return a proper 429 with a Retry-After header, and log the partition key — because 'attacker' and 'shared NAT' look identical in a 429 storm, and only the key tells you which one you're fighting.`],quote:`Rate limit the client you can actually see — or you'll end up throttling your own load balancer.`,table:{headers:[`Topology`,`RemoteIpAddress returns`,`Fix`],rows:[[`Direct to Kestrel`,`Real client IP`,`Nothing — partition away`],[`Nginx / LB in front`,`Proxy's IP — one shared bucket`,`UseForwardedHeaders + KnownProxies`],[`CDN / WAF edge`,`Edge node IP`,`Trust forwarded headers from CDN ranges`],[`Client behind CGNAT`,`Real IP, shared by many users`,`Looser limits or an auth-based key`]]}},{heading:`05 · Tuning the numbers without guessing`,body:[`Numbers, not vibes. PermitLimit divided by Window is your sustained throughput ceiling per partition — 10 permits over 10 seconds is one request per second per IP, forever. If the products query costs 300 ms of database time, that math caps any single IP at roughly a third of a core-second of DB pressure per second. Run that calculation against your real query cost before you commit to numbers.`,`And leave QueueLimit at its default of zero, at least at first. Queuing sounds kind — 'hold my request until a permit frees up' — but it converts your rate limiter into a memory buffer that an attacker fills for free. Fail fast with a 429, let well-behaved clients back off using Retry-After, and only add queue depth if a legitimate integration genuinely needs it.`],quote:`Throughput per caller is just permits divided by window — do that math before you deploy.`,table:{headers:[`Knob`,`What it controls`,`Starting value`],rows:[[`PermitLimit`,`Requests allowed per window`,`10`],[`Window`,`Window length`,`10 seconds`],[`QueueLimit`,`Requests waiting for a permit`,`0 — fail fast`],[`AutoReplenishment`,`Refills permits each window`,`true (default)`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Partitioned rate limiting request path`,caption:`Every request is resolved to a partition key (the client IP) before it can touch the expensive endpoint — one bucket per IP, not one bucket for the world.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    A["Client<br/>IP 203.0.113.7"] --> B["Kestrel"]
    B --> C["UseRateLimiter<br/>middleware"]
    C --> D{"Resolve partition key<br/>HttpContext.Connection<br/>RemoteIpAddress"}
    D --> E["Fixed window limiter<br/>10 permits / 10 s<br/>one bucket per IP"]
    E -->|"permit available"| F["GET /products<br/>expensive query"]
    E -->|"quota exhausted"| G["429 Too Many Requests<br/>Retry-After header"]
    F --> H["Database"]`},{id:`fixed-window-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Fixed window lifecycle for one IP`,caption:`Ten requests sail through, the eleventh gets a shaped 429, and the bucket refills when the window expires.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant M as RateLimiter Middleware
    participant P as Fixed Window Partition
    participant E as Products Endpoint
    C->>M: GET /products x10
    M->>P: Acquire permit
    P-->>M: Allowed — 10 of 10 used
    M->>E: Forward request
    E-->>C: 200 OK
    C->>M: GET /products — 11th call
    M->>P: Acquire permit
    P-->>M: Rejected
    M-->>C: 429 Too Many Requests
    Note over P: Window expires after 10 s<br/>permits refill to 10
    C->>M: GET /products after reset
    M->>P: Acquire permit
    P-->>M: Allowed
    M->>E: Forward request
    E-->>C: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Two calls or nothing: AddRateLimiter to register policies plus app.UseRateLimiter() to enforce them — forget either one and every policy silently never fires.`,`Never ship the global limiter to a public API — partition every policy by RemoteIpAddress so one abusive client burns only its own bucket, not everyone's.`,`Start at 10 permits per 10 seconds per IP on expensive read endpoints — a sustained ceiling of ~1 request/second per client — then tune from real traffic, not vibes.`,`Budget for the boundary burst: a fixed window can admit 2x its permit limit across a window edge, so switch to sliding window when that spike actually hurts.`,`Behind any proxy, call UseForwardedHeaders with KnownProxies before rate limiting — otherwise every customer shares one bucket sized for your load balancer's IP.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make an IP Address Rate Limiter in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PIfGHbvuAtM`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`rate-limiting`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-piESqhWbAFQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:7,title:`The Right Way To Build Async APIs in ASP.NET Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 20:13`,minutes:20,difficulty:3,tags:[`REST`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The night our thumbnail endpoint starved the whole API`,paragraphs:[`It's 15:10 during a database replica failover and the pager lights up: image uploads are timing out at 30 seconds, health checks are failing, and the entire API — not just uploads — is crawling. The dashboard shows CPU at a lazy 40% while the thread pool queue looks like a subway platform at rush hour. Classic starvation signature: work piling up faster than threads can drain it.`,`The root cause is embarrassingly simple. POST /images accepts a multipart upload and generates five thumbnail sizes inline before responding. One upload is fine. Forty concurrent uploads and every request is fighting for the same thread pool threads — so even endpoints that never touch an image start timing out. We spin up two extra nodes; the cliff just moves to the right.`,`The fix isn't more hardware. It's admitting the client never needed the thumbnails in the same HTTP exchange. We split the request: accept the upload, hand the job to a background worker through a Channel, return 202 Accepted with a job ID, and let the client poll a status endpoint. Accept latency drops from 30 seconds to under 100 milliseconds, and the queue absorbs bursts instead of the thread pool.`],problem:`A CPU-bound thumbnail generation running inline inside an HTTP handler starves the thread pool under concurrency — the endpoint needs to accept work, delegate it to a background worker, and return immediately, with a status contract the client can poll.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`System.Threading.Channels`,`ConcurrentDictionary`,`202 Accepted`],note:`The minimal moving parts for the async request/reply pattern: an in-process job queue (Channels) feeding a background worker, an in-memory status store (ConcurrentDictionary), all behind a 202 Accepted contract.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why a 'working' endpoint is a ticking bomb`,body:[`Let's start with the version that works — the one in every demo. The client sends a multipart form-data POST with an image, the endpoint resizes it into a few widths, and returns links to each thumbnail. One request, one response, done. It's clean, it's testable, and it falls over the moment two people use it at once.`,`Here's the thing I want you to feel: the expensive line in that controller is the thumbnail generation, and it's CPU-bound. Async/await won't save you — await helps when you're waiting on I/O, not when you're burning cores on image resampling. More threads won't save you either, because the work is CPU-bound; you just get more threads queued behind the same cores.`,`So before we touch any code, the reframe: this endpoint has a contract problem, not a performance problem. The client doesn't need the thumbnails in the same HTTP exchange — it needs them eventually, with a way to ask 'are we there yet?'. Once you accept that, the design writes itself.`],quote:`An endpoint that does the work inline doesn't have a performance problem — it has a contract problem.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Sync inline`,`Simple, one round-trip, easy error handling`,`Thread-pool starvation, 30s+ latencies under load`,`Demos only`],[`Async 202 + polling`,`Sub-100ms accepts, burst-tolerant, natural backpressure`,`Client must poll; job state to track`,`Any work over ~2s`],[`More servers`,`No code change`,`Cost scales with load, cliff just moves`,`Last resort`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Right Way To Build Async APIs in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=piESqhWbAFQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what does the client actually need?`,body:[`So what does the client actually need? It uploads a file and, at some point, wants URLs for each thumbnail width — 256 pixels, 128 pixels, and so on. Notice: nothing in that story requires the answer in the same request. That's the boundary we're going to draw.`,`New contract: POST /images accepts the file, enqueues a job, and returns 202 Accepted with the job's identifier and links the client can follow later. The status itself is a tiny enum — Queued while it sits in the channel, Processing while the worker chews on it, then Completed or Failed. That's the entire vocabulary.`,`Why 202 and not 200? Because status codes are a contract with every intermediary — proxies, gateways, SDK generators. 200 says 'here's your result'; 202 says 'I accepted it, the result isn't ready, here's where to ask'. Getting this wrong is how you end up with clients caching a 'done' response that wasn't.`],quote:`202 Accepted is HTTP's way of saying 'it's in the queue, trust me' — and the status endpoint is how you earn that trust.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Right Way To Build Async APIs in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=piESqhWbAFQ`,at:`4:20`}},{heading:`03 · Choosing the queue: Channels, and why not RabbitMQ (yet)`,body:[`Now the queue. .NET ships exactly the primitive we need: System.Threading.Channels. Think of a pipe — a writer on one end, a reader on the other. The controller calls WriteAsync to push a ThumbnailGenerationJob in; a background service loops on ReadAsync to pull them out. No infrastructure, no broker, no YAML.`,`You get two flavors. Unbounded: any number of messages, memory grows forever — great for demos, terrifying under load. Bounded with FullMode.Wait: when the channel is full, WriteAsync awaits until there's room, which turns 'out of memory at 3 AM' into 'slower accepts at 3 AM'. That's backpressure doing its job at exactly the right layer.`,`Why not RabbitMQ or MassTransit on day one? Because a channel is in-process: zero ops, but zero durability and zero cross-instance visibility. If your worker dies mid-job, the job is gone. The honest rule: channels while the API process is the whole world; a real broker the moment durability or multiple consumers enter the chat.`],quote:`A bounded channel is a load-shedding valve you get for free; an unbounded channel is a memory leak with good manners.`,table:{headers:[`Queue option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Bounded Channel (FullMode.Wait)`,`Backpressure, zero infra, awaitable writes`,`In-process only, no durability`,`Single-instance API`],[`Unbounded Channel`,`Never rejects a write`,`OOM risk under sustained bursts`,`Trusted, slow producers`],[`Broker (RabbitMQ / MassTransit)`,`Durable, cross-instance, retries`,`Ops burden, serialization`,`Multi-replica or critical jobs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Right Way To Build Async APIs in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=piESqhWbAFQ`,at:`8:26`}},{heading:`04 · Tracking the job: ConcurrentDictionary and the honest fine print`,body:[`Next: how does the status endpoint know anything? The barebones move from the talk: register a singleton ConcurrentDictionary<string, ThumbnailGenerationJobStatus>. The key is the job ID, the value is that enum. The controller sets Queued right after WriteAsync succeeds — not before, or you'll happily report jobs that were never enqueued.`,`Then the worker flips it: Processing when it picks the job off the channel, Completed or Failed when the resizing finishes or throws. Three writes, one dictionary, and the status endpoint just does a TryGetValue. Simple, thread-safe, and completely honest — for exactly one API instance.`,`Here's the fine print I'd be irresponsible to skip: this dictionary dies with the process. Restart mid-job and every client polling your status endpoint gets a 404 for a job that 'existed'. Run two replicas and each one only sees its own jobs. The moment either of those is real, move the state to Redis or a database row — the pattern doesn't change, only the storage does.`],quote:`In-memory job state is a demo, not a design — the moment you have two replicas, your status endpoint starts gaslighting clients.`,table:{headers:[`State store`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`ConcurrentDictionary (singleton)`,`Zero setup, thread-safe, microsecond reads`,`Lost on restart, not shared across replicas`,`Single instance / demo`],[`Redis`,`Shared across replicas, TTLs, fast`,`New dependency, still not fully durable`,`Multi-replica, short-lived jobs`],[`Database row`,`Durable, auditable, queryable`,`More latency and schema`,`Long-lived or billable jobs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Right Way To Build Async APIs in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=piESqhWbAFQ`,at:`9:15`}},{heading:`05 · The status endpoint and the polling dance`,body:[`Last piece: the polling dance. GET /images/{id}/status returns the enum — and when it's Completed, the links to each thumbnail width, so the client's final poll delivers the payload it originally wanted. Unknown ID? 404, immediately, don't make clients guess. Failed? Return the reason, not just the word.`,`On the client side, don't hammer. Start polling around 500 ms, back off exponentially, and give the whole thing a deadline — 30 to 60 seconds — after which you show 'still processing' and keep going in the background. And if you control both ends, remember there's a step beyond polling: a webhook or callback URL, so the server tells you instead.`,`And notice what the upload endpoint became: validate, write to the channel, set Queued, return 202. Three fast operations, no image code anywhere near the request path. That's the shape you're aiming for — the HTTP layer accepts, the background layer works, the status layer reconciles the two.`],quote:`The status endpoint is your promise made visible — poll it politely and it will never lie to you.`,table:{headers:[`Polling strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fixed interval (1s)`,`Dead simple`,`Wasteful, thundering herd`,`Internal tools`],[`Exponential backoff`,`Cheap early, patient late`,`Slightly more client code`,`Default choice`],[`Webhook / callback`,`Zero wasted requests`,`Needs public endpoint, retry logic`,`Long jobs, B2B integrations`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Right Way To Build Async APIs in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=piESqhWbAFQ`,at:`11:03`}}],diagrams:[{id:`async-api-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Async API topology: accept fast, work in background`,caption:`The HTTP path never touches image code; the bounded channel is the only handshake between the two worlds.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  C["Client"] -->|"multipart POST /images"| A["API Controller"]
  A -->|"WriteAsync(job)"| Q["Bounded Channel<br/>FullMode.Wait"]
  A -->|"status = Queued"| D[("ConcurrentDictionary<br/>id to JobStatus")]
  A -->|"202 Accepted + id + status URL"| C
  Q -->|"ReadAsync()"| W["BackgroundService<br/>Thumbnail Worker"]
  W -->|"status = Processing"| D
  W --> R["Resize: 256px / 128px / 64px"]
  R -->|"Completed / Failed"| D
  C -->|"poll GET /images/:id/status"| S["Status Endpoint"]
  S --> D`},{id:`async-request-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One upload, end to end`,caption:`From a 30-second inline resize to an 80 ms accept, a queued job, and a polite polling loop.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant A as API Controller
  participant Q as Channel Writer
  participant W as Background Worker
  participant S as Status Dictionary
  C->>A: POST /images (multipart file)
  A->>Q: WriteAsync(thumbnail job)
  Q-->>A: enqueued (awaits if channel full)
  A->>S: status[id] = Queued
  A-->>C: 202 Accepted + id + status URL
  W->>Q: ReadAsync(thumbnail job)
  W->>S: status[id] = Processing
  W->>W: resize to 256px, 128px, 64px
  W->>S: status[id] = Completed or Failed
  loop exponential backoff, ~500ms start
    C->>A: GET /images/:id/status
    A-->>C: 200 + status + thumbnail links
  end`}],takeaways:[`Return 202 Accepted within ~100 ms for any work longer than ~2 s — if the client can't make coffee during the request, the contract is wrong.`,`Prefer bounded channels with FullMode.Wait; size capacity at roughly 2× peak jobs/sec × worst-case job seconds so backpressure hits the accept path, not your memory.`,`Run one background reader per CPU core for CPU-bound work like resizing — extra readers add contention, not throughput.`,`Poll status endpoints with exponential backoff starting at ~500 ms and cap the client deadline at ~30–60 s before surfacing 'still processing' to the user.`,`In-memory status (ConcurrentDictionary) survives exactly zero restarts and zero second replicas — move to Redis or a database row the moment either becomes real.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Right Way To Build Async APIs in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=piESqhWbAFQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-cgjifZF8ZME`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:8,title:`Master Claims Transformation for Flexible JWT Auth in ASP.NET Core`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:11`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`Keycloak`,`Azure`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Platinum Customer Who Was Still 'Free' at 2 AM`,paragraphs:[`It's 2:07 AM and a top-tier customer — call her Dana — is rage-refreshing the reports page. She upgraded to Platinum six hours ago. Our API disagrees: every call to the premium analytics endpoint returns 403. The on-call engineer pastes her JWT into a decoder and there it is, printed in black and white: subscription=free. The claim was baked into the token at login — and that token doesn't expire for three months.`,`The first 'fix' was a support script: log out, log back in, pray. The real fix was architectural — stop trusting the token for anything that can change, and enrich the principal at request time with ASP.NET Core's IClaimsTransformation. One class, one policy, and Dana's Platinum status lit up without her ever re-authenticating.`,`Two weeks later, fire number two: a marketing push onboarded 40k users and p95 latency doubled overnight, because the transformation was querying Postgres on every single request. That's the moment caching stopped being an optimization and became part of the auth design.`],problem:`Authorization-critical data like subscription tiers changes far faster than the JWT's lifetime — bake it into the token and permissions go stale for months; enrich per-request and you pay a database round-trip on every authenticated call unless you cache.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`IClaimsTransformation`,`Keycloak`,`JWT Bearer`,`Policy Authz`,`IServiceScopeFactory`,`HybridCache`,`EF Core`],note:`An external IdP (Keycloak/Entra ID) mints the JWT; ASP.NET Core's bearer middleware authenticates it, IClaimsTransformation enriches the principal with fresh per-user data resolved through a scoped provider, and policy-based authorization consumes the result.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why baked-in claims betray you`,body:[`Here's the picture I want in your head before we touch any code. Your SPA logs in against Keycloak or Entra ID, gets back an access token, and ships that token to your backend on every call. The backend validates the signature, the issuer, the expiry — and trusts the claims inside. That trust is the whole problem: the token in my demo expires in three months, and Dana's subscription changed six hours in.`,`Could we shorten the token lifetime? Sure, but now you're chasing refresh tokens and hammering your IdP. Could we ask the IdP to re-mint on upgrade? It doesn't know your billing tables — that's your domain, not theirs. So the honest answer is: let the token carry the stable identity facts, and enrich the principal at the edge of your own API, where the volatile data actually lives. That's exactly the slot claims transformation occupies.`],quote:`A JWT is a snapshot, not a live view — anything that can change while the token lives doesn't belong inside it.`,table:{headers:[`Where the claim lives`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Baked into the JWT`,`Zero per-request cost`,`Stale until token expiry (3 months)`,`Truly immutable facts only`],[`Claims transformation`,`Always fresh at request time`,`Adds per-request work`,`Volatile authz data`],[`Hybrid: token + enrichment`,`Stable in token, volatile enriched`,`Two sources to reason about`,`Most real systems`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Master Claims Transformation for Flexible JWT Auth in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cgjifZF8ZME`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary between authentication and authorization`,body:[`Now, where does this hook actually sit? Dead center between authentication and authorization. The bearer middleware authenticates — it proves who you are and hands you a ClaimsPrincipal. Then IClaimsTransformation.TransformAsync runs — your one chance to say 'before anyone checks permissions, here's what else you need to know about this user.' Then policies evaluate against the enriched principal.`,`Why not just do the lookup inside the policy handler? You could — but now every policy that cares about subscriptions re-resolves the service, re-queries the data, and you've scattered enrichment logic across N handlers. Why not custom middleware? You'd be rebuilding principal plumbing the framework already owns. One pipeline stage, one place to test, and every policy downstream just reads the claim. That's a trade I'll take every time.`],quote:`Authentication proves who you are; transformation decides what else the system must know before it asks what you may do.`,table:{headers:[`Enrichment point`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Policy handlers`,`Lazy — only when needed`,`Duplicated logic across handlers`,`1-2 policies max`],[`IClaimsTransformation`,`One stage, all policies benefit`,`Runs on every request`,`Shared enrichment`],[`Custom middleware`,`Full control of ordering`,`Reinvents principal plumbing`,`Rare edge cases`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Master Claims Transformation for Flexible JWT Auth in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cgjifZF8ZME`,at:`5:37`}},{heading:`03 · The idempotency trap: TransformAsync runs again`,body:[`Here's the gotcha that bites everyone at least once: the framework makes zero promises about how many times TransformAsync runs for a given principal. If you blindly append your subscription claim on every invocation, you get duplicates — and duplicate claims quietly wreck first-claim-wins readers and make policy behavior feel haunted.`,`So the very first line of TransformAsync is a guard: does this principal already carry my subscription claim? If yes, return it untouched — idempotent, done. And when you do add the claim, don't mutate the incoming identity in place; build a fresh ClaimsIdentity, add the claim to it, and append that identity to the principal. New identity, clean semantics, no surprises on the next run.`],quote:`Write TransformAsync like it will run twice in the same request — because one day, it will.`,table:{headers:[`Guard strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`HasClaim early return`,`Idempotent, cheap`,`Easy to forget`,`Always`],[`Rebuild new ClaimsIdentity`,`Clean, no in-place mutation`,`Slightly more code`,`With the guard`],[`Mutate principal in place`,`Fewest lines`,`Duplicate claims on re-run`,`Never`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Master Claims Transformation for Flexible JWT Auth in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cgjifZF8ZME`,at:`7:03`}},{heading:`04 · Scoped services inside a singleton: the scope-factory dance`,body:[`Next friction point: dependency injection. The transformation gets registered as a singleton — AddSingleton<IClaimsTransformation, ...> — but my UserSubscriptionProvider depends on a DbContext, which is scoped. Inject the DbContext straight in and you've built a captive dependency; turn on scope validation and ASP.NET Core will refuse to even start.`,`The dance is simple: inject IServiceScopeFactory, and inside TransformAsync call CreateScope(), resolve the provider from that scope, use it, and let the scope dispose. Why not register the transformation as scoped instead? Because the hook is resolved from the app's singleton container — fighting that is losing. Borrow the scope, don't own it.`],quote:`A singleton holding a DbContext is a bug on a timer — create a scope, borrow the service, let it go.`,table:{headers:[`Getting scoped deps`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`IServiceScopeFactory`,`Correct lifetimes, validated`,`Boilerplate per call`,`Singleton hooks`],[`Inject DbContext directly`,`One line`,`Captive dependency, runtime bugs`,`Never`],[`Register transformation scoped`,`No factory needed`,`Hook lives in singleton container`,`Not viable`]]}},{heading:`05 · The per-request tax: cache it or own it`,body:[`Last thing, and it's the one the original lesson flags as your responsibility: this code runs on every authenticated request. If enrichment means a Postgres round trip, you just added five to twenty milliseconds to every endpoint behind auth — and at a few hundred requests per second, that's your p95 on fire, which is exactly the second incident from my story.`,`So cache it. A simple IMemoryCache with a 30-to-60-second TTL gets you under a millisecond. HybridCache goes further — L1 in-process, L2 distributed, and built-in stampede protection so a cold key doesn't stampede the database. Is 60 seconds of staleness acceptable for a subscription tier? Dana waits a minute after upgrading instead of three months. That's not a trade-off, that's a steal.`],quote:`If your transformation touches a database, you've moved your authorization latency into your data layer — cache it or own it.`,table:{headers:[`Caching strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`No cache`,`Always fresh`,`+5-20 ms per request`,`Never in prod`],[`IMemoryCache + TTL`,`Sub-ms, simple`,`Per-instance, no stampede guard`,`Single instance`],[`HybridCache`,`L1+L2, stampede protection`,`More moving parts`,`Multi-instance APIs`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`auth-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Auth pipeline with claims enrichment`,caption:`Where TransformAsync sits between bearer authentication and policy authorization — and how the scoped subscription lookup reaches the cache.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Client["Client"]
        SPA["SPA / Mobile App"]
    end
    subgraph IdP["Identity Provider"]
        KC["Keycloak / Entra ID"]
    end
    subgraph API["ASP.NET Core API"]
        AUTH["JWT Bearer Middleware"]
        CT["IClaimsTransformation"]
        SCOPE["IServiceScopeFactory"]
        USP["UserSubscriptionProvider"]
        POL["Authorization Policy"]
        EP["Premium Endpoint"]
    end
    subgraph Data["Data Layer"]
        CACHE["HybridCache"]
        DB[("Postgres")]
    end
    SPA -->|"1 login"| KC
    KC -->|"2 access token JWT"| SPA
    SPA -->|"3 Bearer header"| AUTH
    AUTH -->|"4 validated principal"| CT
    CT --> SCOPE
    SCOPE --> USP
    USP --> CACHE
    CACHE -->|"miss"| DB
    CT -->|"5 enriched principal"| POL
    POL --> EP`},{id:`transform-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Inside TransformAsync: idempotent, scoped, cached`,caption:`The guard-first, scope-per-call flow that keeps the transformation safe to run repeatedly and cheap enough to run on every request.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant MW as Auth Middleware
    participant CT as Claims Transformation
    participant SF as IServiceScopeFactory
    participant USP as Subscription Provider
    participant C as HybridCache
    participant DB as Postgres
    MW->>CT: TransformAsync(principal)
    CT->>CT: HasClaim(subscription)?
    alt claim already present
        CT-->>MW: return principal untouched
    else claim missing
        CT->>SF: CreateScope()
        SF->>USP: resolve scoped service
        USP->>C: GetOrCreate(userId)
        alt cache hit
            C-->>USP: subscription level
        else cache miss
            USP->>DB: query subscription
            DB-->>USP: level
            USP->>C: set with 60s TTL
        end
        USP-->>CT: subscription level
        CT->>CT: new ClaimsIdentity + AddClaim
        CT-->>MW: enriched principal
    end`}],takeaways:[`Guard TransformAsync with HasClaim before adding anything — an unguarded transformation that runs twice mints duplicate claims and corrupts policy evaluation.`,`IClaimsTransformation is registered as a singleton: never inject DbContext or scoped services directly — resolve them via IServiceScopeFactory.CreateScope() on each call.`,`Cache enrichment lookups with a 30-60 s TTL (HybridCache): a raw DB call per request adds 5-20 ms to every authenticated endpoint; cached, transformation stays under 1 ms.`,`Keep volatile authorization data (subscription tier, quotas) out of the JWT — a claim baked into a 3-month token is stale the moment the user upgrades.`,`Build a new ClaimsIdentity and append it to the principal instead of mutating the incoming identity — that's what keeps repeated transformations from stacking duplicate identities.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Master Claims Transformation for Flexible JWT Auth in ASP.NET Core`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cgjifZF8ZME`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`jwt-vs-sessions`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-omDiALXQWuw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:9,title:`I Scaled My Transactional Outbox to 2B+ messages/day. Here's how`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 32:35`,minutes:33,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Outbox`],story:{headline:`The Poller That Couldn't Keep Up: Scaling an Outbox to 2 Billion Messages a Day`,paragraphs:[`It's 2:40 AM and the checkout events are piling up. The business side is writing orders just fine — but the outbox_messages table has quietly become the biggest table in the database, and our little BackgroundService wakes up every 5 seconds, does a sequential scan over the whole thing, sorts it, and publishes one message at a time to RabbitMQ. Each poll is slower than the last, because the table never shrinks. The backlog isn't draining; it's compounding.`,`We put a number on the whiteboard: the outbox processor was draining roughly 7,000 messages a minute — barely 120 a second — while the business was writing several times that. At that rate, 'eventually consistent' was going to mean 'eventually next week.' The scary part wasn't the number. It was that nobody could say which step was slow: the fetch, the publish, or the update.`,`So we stopped guessing and turned the outbox processor into a benchmark target. One-minute runs, a cancellation token as the starting gun, stopwatches around every step, and exactly one optimization per run. By the end of the exercise we were pushing 74,000 messages a minute — and we had a roadmap to 2 billion a day.`],problem:`The outbox processor could only drain ~7,000 messages a minute while the business wrote far more — and every poll paid a full sequential scan plus sort over a table that never shrank.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`MassTransit`,`RabbitMQ`,`Outbox Pattern`,`.NET BackgroundService`,`Partial Index`,`ConcurrentDictionary`,`Stopwatch Steps`],note:`Postgres holds the outbox table inside the same transaction as business data; a .NET BackgroundService polls, batches, and publishes through MassTransit to RabbitMQ. Partial covering indexes, cached type resolution, and per-step stopwatch instrumentation are the scaling levers.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: benchmark before you touch anything`,body:[`Before optimizing a single line, I wrap the existing processor in a harness: a cancellation token source with a 1-minute delay, so every run is a clean, comparable benchmark. The baseline processor is the simplest possible thing — fetch from outbox_messages, deserialize, publish via MassTransit to RabbitMQ, mark processed, and on exception record it and skip the message forever. It works. It's just slow: about 7,000 messages a minute.`,`Now the question that actually matters: which step is slow? So I drop in a total stopwatch plus one stopwatch per step — fetch, deserialize/publish, update. No APM vendor, no fancy profiler, just Stopwatches. Why not just crank the poll frequency from 5 seconds to 500 milliseconds first? Because that's guessing. If the fetch is the bottleneck, polling faster just queues up behind the same slow query.`,`The rule I've landed on: one change per benchmark run, one number on the board per run. Otherwise you can't attribute the win to anything.`],quote:`Gut feel is not a profiler — a stopwatch per step is.`,table:{headers:[`Instrumented step`,`What it isolates`,`Question it answers`],rows:[[`Total stopwatch`,`Whole poll cycle`,`Are we fast enough yet?`],[`Fetch stopwatch`,`SELECT + network round-trip`,`Is Postgres the bottleneck?`],[`Publish stopwatch`,`Deserialize + RabbitMQ publish`,`Is it the broker, or reflection?`],[`Update stopwatch`,`Marking messages processed`,`Are write round-trips eating us?`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Scaled My Transactional Outbox to 2B+ messages/day. Here's how`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omDiALXQWuw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · The cheapest ten-x: stop round-tripping one row at a time`,body:[`The first real optimization is embarrassingly simple: increase the batch size. Instead of pulling a handful of messages per poll, pull a proper batch. Result: 74,000 messages in one minute — slightly over 1,000 per second, a bit more than a 10x jump — from essentially one config change. This is always the first lever I reach for.`,`Why does batching dominate? Because every round-trip to Postgres has fixed cost — connection, query parse, plan, network — and when you fetch one row at a time, you pay that cost N times. Amortize it over a batch and the per-message cost collapses. Why not just poll more often instead? Polling faster multiplies the fixed overhead; batching divides it. They are not the same trick.`,`Why not make the batch infinite, then? Three reasons: memory (you hold the whole batch in-process), lock and update duration on the outbox table, and blast radius — if a publish fails mid-batch, you want a contained failure, not a 500,000-message crater. Pick a batch that keeps each cycle comfortably under a second.`],quote:`Every round-trip you don't make is pure profit — batching is the cheapest ten-x you will ever buy.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`1 row per poll`,`Dead simple, strict order`,`~7K msgs/min, round-trip bound`,`Demos only`],[`Batch of N rows`,`>10x throughput, amortized I/O`,`Larger failure blast radius`,`Default choice`],[`Parallel batch workers`,`Scales past a single loop`,`Needs idempotency + ordering care`,`Stage two`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Scaled My Transactional Outbox to 2B+ messages/day. Here's how`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omDiALXQWuw`,at:`4:38`}},{heading:`03 · Reading the query plan like a crime scene`,body:[`With batching banked, the fetch stopwatch is still the biggest slice. So I take the SELECT into the database and run EXPLAIN. The plan tells the whole story: a sequential scan over outbox_messages, then a sort on occurred_on, then a limit. We're scanning and sorting the entire table — processed rows included — every five seconds, just to grab the newest unprocessed head.`,`Two fixes, both cheap. First, stop doing SELECT * — we only need id, type, and content, so select exactly those columns. Second, build a partial covering index: ix_outbox_messages_unprocessed on occurred_on (ascending, matching our ORDER BY), filtered to rows where processed_on is null, including the columns we read. Now the plan is a lean index scan over only the unprocessed head — no sort, no dead rows touched.`,`Why not just delete processed rows aggressively to keep the table small? You can — but now you've traded read cost for vacuum churn and write amplification on your hot path. The partial index gives you a permanently tiny working set regardless of how big the table gets, and the table size stops mattering entirely. That's the property you want at 2 billion a day.`],quote:`A sequential scan over a table that never shrinks is a tax you pay every five seconds — the partial index makes the tax zero.`,table:{headers:[`Query shape`,`Execution plan`,`Cost as table grows`],rows:[[`SELECT * + ORDER BY occurred_on`,`Seq scan + sort + limit`,`Linear — every poll pays for the whole table`],[`Narrow columns + partial covering index`,`Index scan over unprocessed head only`,`Flat — proportional to backlog, not table size`],[`Aggressive deletes of processed rows`,`Smaller heap, but vacuum pressure`,`Write amplification on the hot path`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Scaled My Transactional Outbox to 2B+ messages/day. Here's how`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omDiALXQWuw`,at:`9:06`}},{heading:`04 · Killing reflection and batching the writes`,body:[`Next, the publish path. For every message we resolve the .NET type from the message-type name using reflection against the messaging assembly — and reflection is inherently slow. At 1,000 messages a second, that's a thousand identical lookups a second for types that never change. So: a private static readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, Type>, a GetOrAddMessageType helper, and the lookup cost drops to a dictionary hit after the first message of each type.`,`Then the update path. We're marking each outbox message processed with its own UPDATE — another per-message round-trip. So I introduce a small struct representing an outbox message update, collect the results of the batch, and flush them as a batched update. Read batched, write batched. The per-message fixed costs are now amortized on both sides of the pipeline.`,`Why not jump straight to parallel workers here? Because parallelizing a wasteful loop just multiplies the waste — you'd burn cores doing redundant reflection and one-row updates, faster. Cache the reflection, batch the writes, re-run the benchmark, and only then parallelize whatever's still slow. Order of operations is everything.`],quote:`Cache the reflection, batch the writes — then parallelize whatever's left.`,table:{headers:[`Per-message cost`,`Before`,`After`],rows:[[`Type resolution`,`Reflection lookup on every message`,`ConcurrentDictionary cache hit`],[`Mark processed`,`One UPDATE per message`,`One batched UPDATE per batch`],[`Effect at 20K msgs/sec`,`CPU + round-trips dominate the profile`,`Amortized down to noise`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Scaled My Transactional Outbox to 2B+ messages/day. Here's how`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omDiALXQWuw`,at:`12:24`}},{heading:`05 · Doing the 2-billion math out loud`,body:[`Let's be honest about the target. Two billion messages a day is not a hero number — it's about 23,000 messages per second, sustained, 24/7, with no lunch break. Our batched, indexed, cache-fed baseline sits at roughly 1,200 a second, so the remaining gap is about 19x. That gap doesn't close with one trick; it closes with multiplication: multiple outbox processor instances, each pulling disjoint batches (keyset reads or SKIP LOCKED semantics so workers never fight over rows), each running the optimized loop.`,`Why not ditch the outbox and publish straight to the broker from the request path? Because the entire reason the outbox exists is atomicity — the message commits in the same transaction as the business data. The broker was never the bottleneck; our processor was. Throwing away the consistency guarantee to chase throughput is solving the wrong problem.`,`And the discipline that keeps it at 2 billion: the per-step stopwatches never come out. They run in every benchmark, so when throughput dips at 3 AM, we know in one run whether it's the fetch, the publish, or the update — and the unprocessed set stays small enough that the partial index never stops being a lean scan.`],quote:`Two billion a day isn't a hero number — it's 23K a second, every second, boringly.`,table:{headers:[`Daily volume`,`Sustained msgs/sec`,`vs 74K/min baseline`],rows:[[`100M`,`~1,160`,`On par — batching alone gets you here`],[`500M`,`~5,800`,`~4.7x more needed — indexes + caching + writes`],[`2B`,`~23,100`,`~19x more needed — parallel workers, small hot set`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Scaled My Transactional Outbox to 2B+ messages/day. Here's how`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omDiALXQWuw`,at:`15:45`}}],diagrams:[{id:`outbox-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Outbox pipeline topology`,caption:`Business data and outbox messages commit in one Postgres transaction; the BackgroundService drains the table in batches through MassTransit to RabbitMQ.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph APP["Order Service — write path"]
    TX["DB transaction"] --> INS["INSERT business row<br/>+ outbox message"]
  end
  INS --> PG[("Postgres<br/>outbox_messages")]
  subgraph WORKER["Outbox BackgroundService (poll every 5s)"]
    F["SELECT batch<br/>id, type, content"] --> D["Deserialize<br/>+ type cache"]
    D --> PUB["Publish batch<br/>via MassTransit"]
    PUB --> UPD["Batch UPDATE<br/>processed_on"]
  end
  PG --> F
  UPD --> PG
  PUB --> RMQ[("RabbitMQ")]
  UPD -.->|"on failure: record exception, skip"| PG
  IX["Partial covering index<br/>on occurred_on<br/>WHERE processed_on IS NULL"] -.-> PG`},{id:`poll-cycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One instrumented poll cycle`,caption:`A single batch cycle with the stopwatch steps marked — fetch, publish, update — plus the failure branch that records the exception and skips the message.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant W as Outbox Worker
  participant DB as Postgres
  participant Q as RabbitMQ
  loop every 5s — batch of N
    Note over W: total stopwatch starts
    W->>DB: SELECT id, type, content WHERE processed_on IS NULL ORDER BY occurred_on LIMIT N
    DB-->>W: N rows via partial index (no seq scan, no sort)
    Note over W: fetch stopwatch stops — deserialize + cached type lookup
    W->>Q: publish batch via MassTransit
    Q-->>W: acks
    Note over W: publish stopwatch stops
    W->>DB: batch UPDATE processed_on = now()
    Note over W: update stopwatch stops — log all four numbers
    alt publish fails for a message
      W->>DB: record exception, mark skipped
    end
  end`}],takeaways:[`Baseline first: a 1-minute benchmark harness with per-step stopwatches put the starting number on the board — ~7,000 msgs/min — before a single line was optimized.`,`Batching alone delivered 74,000 msgs/min, a >10x jump, and it's one config change — always take the cheapest ten-x before buying hardware.`,`Replace SELECT * with explicit columns plus a partial covering index on occurred_on WHERE processed_on IS NULL — the plan goes from sequential scan + sort to a lean index scan whose cost is flat as the table grows.`,`Cache reflection-based type resolution in a ConcurrentDictionary and batch your UPDATEs — per-message reflection and per-message round-trips are silent killers once you're past 1K msgs/sec.`,`2B/day ≈ 23,000 msgs/sec sustained, about 19x the batched baseline — close the gap with parallel workers on disjoint batches (keyset/SKIP LOCKED reads) while keeping the unprocessed set permanently small.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Scaled My Transactional Outbox to 2B+ messages/day. Here's how`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omDiALXQWuw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`change-data-capture`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-MzC0PgYocmk`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:10,title:`How To Build Loosely Coupled Microservices With MassTransit`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 23:02`,minutes:23,difficulty:3,tags:[`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`08:45 as mobile traffic surged: Article Creation Died With the Reporting API`,paragraphs:[`It's 13:20 right after the schema migration and marketing's big newsletter push is live. PagerDuty fires: article creation is timing out across the board. The on-call engineer pulls up the dashboards and finds the culprit isn't the Newsletter API at all — it's the Reporting API, pinned at 100% CPU running a metrics aggregation job. One service is drowning. So why is the other one down with it?`,`Because buried inside the create-article flow is a synchronous HTTP call to the Reporting API to record the view event. Temporal coupling in its purest form: the Reporting API's outage became the Newsletter API's outage, and a background metrics job took down content creation. The 3 AM fix was to comment out the call and ship. The real fix took a sprint.`,`That sprint replaced the HTTP call with events: the Newsletter API publishes ArticleCreated and ArticleViewed to RabbitMQ through MassTransit, and the Reporting API subscribes and keeps its own tiny projection of the data. Next time the Reporting API fell over — and it did, three months later — article creation didn't even blink. The events waited in the queue and the metrics caught up.`],problem:`How do two services share facts without sharing fates — so a consumer's outage never becomes the producer's outage?`},tech:{stack:[`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`MassTransit.RabbitMQ`,`ASP.NET Core`,`Docker Compose`,`Vertical Slices`],note:`RabbitMQ carries the events between services, MassTransit turns publish/subscribe into typed C# contracts with DI-friendly bus configuration, and Docker Compose keeps the broker reproducible on every machine.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the HTTP call was the bug`,body:[`So here's the thing I want you to sit with: those two services were already 'microservices' — separate projects, separate deploys, separate containers. And yet at 17:00 as cross-region traffic shifted, one of them took the other down. How? A single synchronous HTTP call. The Newsletter API couldn't record an article view without the Reporting API being alive and fast. That's not loose coupling — that's a distributed monolith with extra steps.`,`My first instinct was always 'just add a timeout and move on.' But think it through: a timeout doesn't remove the coupling, it converts latency into errors. Now article creation fails fast instead of hanging, which is arguably worse for the user. The real question is — does the caller need the answer right now? For recording a view event? No. It's a fact about the past. Facts about the past belong on a wire, not inside a request/response cycle.`,`So the shape of the fix: swap the HTTP call for a published event. The producer fires and forgets to the broker; the consumer catches up whenever it can. If Reporting is down for an hour, the events just wait in the queue. That's the whole trick — temporal decoupling.`],quote:`A timeout doesn't remove coupling — it just converts latency into errors.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Synchronous HTTP`,`Easy to debug, immediate answer`,`Temporal coupling, cascading failures`,`True request/response only`],[`Event messaging`,`Survives consumer outages, easy to add subscribers`,`Eventual consistency, more moving parts`,`State propagation between services`],[`Shared database`,`One source of truth`,`Schema coupling, kills autonomy`,`Avoid`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build Loosely Coupled Microservices With MassTransit`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MzC0PgYocmk`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: events are facts, not instructions`,body:[`Next question I asked myself: event or command? A command says 'you, Reporting API, do X' — and the moment I send it, I've coupled myself to the receiver's name and existence. An event just says 'an article was created' or 'an article was viewed.' I don't know who's listening and I don't care. Today it's the Reporting API; tomorrow it could be a search indexer, an audit log, a recommendation engine — none of them require touching the Newsletter API.`,`And look at what the Reporting API actually stores: an Article entity that only cares about the identifier and when it was created, plus an ArticleEvent for views. It doesn't need the article body, the author, any of it. That's the boundary drawn correctly — each service keeps its own minimal projection of the world. The temptation to 'just query the Newsletter database' is exactly the coupling we're trying to kill.`,`So the contract is two events: ArticleCreated and ArticleViewed. Small, immutable, past-tense. If you can't phrase your message in past tense, it's probably a command wearing a costume.`],quote:`Publish what happened, not what you want done.`,table:{headers:[`Style`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Event: ArticleCreated`,`Publisher knows zero consumers`,`Consumers own their state`,`Facts, fan-out, audit trails`],[`Command: RecalculateMetrics`,`Explicit intent, one handler`,`Sender coupled to receiver's existence`,`Orchestrated workflows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build Loosely Coupled Microservices With MassTransit`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MzC0PgYocmk`,at:`0:40`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring the broker: docker-compose to AddMassTransit`,body:[`Now the plumbing. First, RabbitMQ itself: I drop the rabbitmq:3-management image into docker-compose, name it to match the existing convention, mount volumes so the broker's configuration survives a restart, and set the environment variables for host, user, and password. The management image matters more than people think — being able to see exchanges and queues in a browser is half your debugging superpower.`,`Then MassTransit. I install MassTransit.RabbitMQ — that's the package carrying the RabbitMQ transport — and register it with builder.Services.AddMassTransit. Inside the configuration I set the endpoint name formatter and call UsingRabbitMq, passing the host as a URI pulled from configuration (MessageBroker:Host) plus the username and password from the MessageBroker section. Both services get the identical setup, because they're talking to the same broker instance.`,`Why a library at all? Because raw client libraries give you channels and acks; MassTransit gives you a bus, typed messages, consumers wired through dependency injection, and topology conventions. You're buying back the convenience a framework would've handed you for free in a monolith.`],quote:`The broker is infrastructure; the bus is your application's contract with it.`,table:{headers:[`Broker setup`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`rabbitmq:3-management in Compose`,`UI for exchanges/queues, reproducible locally`,`You own persistence and upgrades`,`Dev and staging`],[`Plain rabbitmq image`,`Smaller footprint`,`No management UI, blind debugging`,`Tiny sandboxes`],[`Managed AMQP (cloud)`,`No ops burden, HA built in`,`Cost, vendor lock-in`,`Production at scale`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build Loosely Coupled Microservices With MassTransit`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MzC0PgYocmk`,at:`1:30`}},{heading:`04 · Naming things so the topology doesn't lie`,body:[`One config line that looks trivial but isn't: the endpoint name formatter. I set it to kebab case, so ArticleCreatedEvent becomes article-created-event — hyphens between words. Why do I care? Because those names become your exchanges and queues in RabbitMQ, and you will be staring at them in the management UI at some point. article-created-event is readable; the default mangling of a class name is not.`,`And here's the part nobody warns you about: queue names are API surface. Once messages are flowing, renaming a queue means rebinding exchanges and draining or migrating the old one — in production. Pick the convention in week one, apply it identically in both services, and never think about it again. Both sides must agree on the topology, or you get the classic 'my consumer never fires' bug — which is really a naming bug in disguise.`],quote:`Queue names are API surface — format them like your routes, not your class names.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build Loosely Coupled Microservices With MassTransit`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MzC0PgYocmk`,at:`2:13`}},{heading:`05 · Hardening: retries, idempotency, and the outbox`,body:[`Before I'd call this production-ready, three hardening moves. First: retries and error queues. MassTransit gives you both out of the box — a transient failure redelivers with backoff, and a poison message lands in an error queue instead of blocking the pipe. Second: idempotency. The default is at-least-once delivery, which means your consumer will eventually see ArticleViewed twice. Key your writes on a message ID or a natural key and make the second write a no-op.`,`Third — and this is the one that bites people at 3 AM: the producer writes to its database and publishes to the broker as two separate operations. If the process dies between them, the event is lost. The transactional outbox pattern fixes that: write the event to an outbox table in the same transaction as your data, and let the transport publish it afterward. MassTransit supports this natively. It's an afternoon of work that saves you a week of 'why is the reporting data missing rows' archaeology.`],quote:`At-least-once delivery means your consumer will see the same event twice — design for it or apologize for it.`,table:{headers:[`Delivery guarantee`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`At-least-once (default)`,`No message loss, simple broker config`,`Duplicates — consumers need idempotency`,`Most event flows`],[`At-most-once`,`No duplicates, lowest latency`,`Drops messages on failure`,`Lossy telemetry`],[`Transactional outbox`,`DB write and publish are atomic`,`Extra table plus dispatcher to run`,`Money and audit-critical events`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build Loosely Coupled Microservices With MassTransit`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MzC0PgYocmk`,at:`7:44`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Event topology: two services, one broker, zero direct calls`,caption:`The Newsletter API publishes facts; the Reporting API subscribes and keeps its own projection. Neither service knows the other's name.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph NL["Newsletter API - ASP.NET Core"]
        A1["Create Article"] --> A2["Publish ArticleCreated"]
        A3["View Article"] --> A4["Publish ArticleViewed"]
    end
    subgraph MQ["RabbitMQ - docker-compose"]
        X1{{"article-created-event"}}
        X2{{"article-viewed-event"}}
        Q1[["article-created queue"]]
        Q2[["article-viewed queue"]]
    end
    subgraph RP["Reporting API"]
        C1["ArticleCreated Consumer"]
        C2["ArticleViewed Consumer"]
        DB[("Own projection - id, created-at, events")]
    end
    A2 --> X1
    A4 --> X2
    X1 --> Q1 --> C1 --> DB
    X2 --> Q2 --> C2 --> DB`},{id:`lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Publish-to-consume lifecycle with the failure path`,caption:`The producer's HTTP request ends at the broker ack; downstream failures become retries and dead-letters, not user-facing 500s.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as Newsletter API
    participant Bus as MassTransit Bus
    participant MQ as RabbitMQ
    participant R as Reporting Consumer
    API->>Bus: Publish ArticleCreated
    Bus->>MQ: publish to article-created-event
    MQ-->>Bus: broker ack
    Note over API: HTTP request done - no downstream call
    MQ->>R: deliver to article-created queue
    R->>R: upsert projection and record event
    R-->>MQ: ack
    alt consumer throws
        R-->>MQ: nack
        MQ->>R: redeliver with retry policy
        MQ->>MQ: retries exhausted - move to error queue
    end`}],takeaways:[`Audit every inter-service HTTP call: if the caller doesn't need the answer to finish its request, replace it with a published event — target zero synchronous calls for state propagation.`,`Name events in past tense (ArticleCreated, ArticleViewed) and publish facts, not instructions — the publisher should be able to name zero consumers.`,`Give each consumer its own minimal projection — two fields (id, created-at) beat one shared database every time.`,`Set the kebab-case endpoint formatter and pin queue names in week one; renaming queues after traffic flows is a production migration, not a refactor.`,`Before launch: persist broker volumes, enable retry plus error queues, and add the outbox — one unpersisted broker restart can drop every in-flight message.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Build Loosely Coupled Microservices With MassTransit`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MzC0PgYocmk`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-5ix52STEPp0`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:11,title:`Why Your .NET Rate Limiter Fails at Scale (And How to Fix It)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 22:35`,minutes:23,difficulty:3,tags:[`Redis`,`.NET`,`Rate limiter`],story:{headline:`The Night Our Rate Limiter Learned to Count to Eight`,paragraphs:[`For eleven months, our rate limiter was the most boring line of code we owned. One API instance, ASP.NET Core's built-in rate limiting middleware, a fixed window of 100 requests per minute per API key, all sitting happily in process memory. It fired 429s exactly when it should. We forgot it existed.`,`Then we scaled to eight instances behind a load balancer for a partner launch, and that partner's integration suite started hammering the endpoint the same night. At 12:05 during a sudden traffic spike the downstream payment provider began throttling us, our queue depth went vertical, and not a single 429 had left our load balancer. Each pod was dutifully counting its own private ledger — and each pod only ever saw about an eighth of the traffic, so no pod's counter ever got anywhere near 100.`,`The morning-after realization was embarrassing: our limit wasn't 100 requests per minute, it was 800, and it silently scaled with our replica count. No amount of extra memory or cleverer local math fixes that. The pods don't need to count harder — they need to agree. One shared counter in Redis, one atomic script, and the limiter started telling the truth again.`],problem:`Multiple API instances must agree on a single count per API key per time window. In-memory state can't provide that consensus — and a naive distributed check (GET, then SET) introduces race conditions that over-admit traffic exactly when the system is under the most load.`},tech:{stack:[`Redis`,`.NET 8`,`ASP.NET Core`,`StackExchange.Redis`,`Lua Script`,`Fixed Window`],note:`Redis holds the shared per-key window counters that every API instance agrees on; an atomic Lua script performs check-and-decrement in one round trip, invoked from ASP.NET Core middleware via StackExchange.Redis.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why in-memory limits lie at scale`,body:[`Here's the trap. ASP.NET Core's built-in rate limiting middleware is excellent — fixed window, sliding window, token bucket, partitioned per API key — but it lives in the process. Every pod keeps its own little ledger, completely unaware of its siblings. The math is brutal in its simplicity: an 8-pod deployment with a 100 requests-per-minute limit is really an 800 requests-per-minute limit, and no single pod ever sees enough traffic to trip its own counter.`,`The instinctive fix is 'just divide the limit by the instance count.' Resist it. Load balancers don't split traffic perfectly evenly, autoscaling changes N under your feet, and sticky sessions to make the math work are a design smell, not a solution. You don't need cleverer local math — you need the pods to agree. That's the entire problem: consensus on one number, per key, per window.`],quote:`Your real rate limit is config times replica count — and autoscaling edits it without telling you.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-memory per instance`,`Zero latency, no extra infra`,`Limit silently multiplies by pod count`,`Single instance only`],[`Divide limit by N`,`No new infrastructure`,`Uneven balancing, autoscaling breaks it`,`Honestly, never`],[`Distributed counter in Redis`,`One shared truth, exact`,`Adds a network hop and a dependency`,`Any multi-instance API`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Your .NET Rate Limiter Fails at Scale (And How to Fix It)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ix52STEPp0`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · What the limiter is actually guarding`,body:[`Before we touch Redis, let's be honest about why we're doing this, because it changes how accurate the limiter has to be. Job one: abuse protection. Someone hammering your API with a flood of requests shouldn't be able to starve legitimate users — that's a denial-of-service shape, and the limiter is your first wall.`,`Job two: fairness — distributing the allowed number of requests over a time window so one noisy tenant doesn't eat the whole pool. Job three, and this is the one people forget: monetization. If you sell subscription tiers, credits, or tokens, the rate limiter is literally the billing enforcement mechanism. That list isn't exhaustive, but notice the implication: a billing limiter has to be right, while an abuse shield can afford to be approximately right. That tolerance decides how much machinery you build.`],quote:`If the limit is your invoice, 'roughly 100' is a support ticket — if it's abuse protection, 'roughly' is fine.`,table:{headers:[`Job`,`Accuracy needed`,`If it's wrong`],rows:[[`Abuse / DoS shield`,`Approximate is fine`,`Some extra load sneaks through`],[`Fair usage between tenants`,`Moderate`,`Noisy-neighbor complaints`],[`Paid quotas and tiers`,`Strict`,`Revenue leaks, billing disputes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Your .NET Rate Limiter Fails at Scale (And How to Fix It)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ix52STEPp0`,at:`0:40`}},{heading:`03 · Picking the algorithm: fixed window and its dirty edge`,body:[`Now the algorithm. The fixed window is what everyone builds first, because it's almost free: one counter per key, INCR on each request, a TTL to reset it every window. It does exactly what the spec says — N requests per window. The catch lives at the edges. A client can fire 100 requests at 0:59 and another 100 at 1:00 and push 200 through in about a second, because two adjacent windows each handed them a fresh budget.`,`The sliding log fixes this by storing a timestamp per request and counting hits inside a true rolling window — exact, but memory grows with traffic. The sliding window counter is the pragmatic middle: weight the previous window's count by how much of it has elapsed, add the current count, and you get a good approximation at a fraction of the memory. The token bucket plays a different game entirely: refill at a steady rate, allow a controlled burst up to bucket capacity — which is what you want when clients are legitimately bursty.`],quote:`Fixed window: two windows, one boundary, twice the traffic.`,table:{headers:[`Algorithm`,`Memory`,`Burst behavior`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fixed window`,`One counter per key`,`Up to 2x limit at boundaries`,`Cheap quotas, tolerant downstreams`],[`Sliding log`,`Timestamp per request`,`Exact, no boundary bursts`,`Strict limits, low traffic`],[`Sliding window counter`,`Two counters per key`,`Small boundary error`,`Best accuracy-per-byte default`],[`Token bucket`,`Bucket state per key`,`Controlled burst capacity`,`Bursty but trusted clients`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Your .NET Rate Limiter Fails at Scale (And How to Fix It)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ix52STEPp0`,at:`1:19`}},{heading:`04 · Why Redis, and why atomicity is the whole game`,body:[`So why Redis? Because it executes commands on a single thread — it's a natural serialization point. Two pods asking 'can this key go through?' get answered in a well-defined order, which is exactly the consensus we were missing. But here's where most implementations quietly break: they do GET, check the count, then SET. Three round trips, three chances for another pod to squeeze in between them. Under load, that race over-admits precisely when you can least afford it.`,`Even INCR-plus-EXPIRE has a wrinkle: refresh the TTL on every call and your window slides forever and never resets; set it only when the counter is new and you're writing branching logic across round trips. The clean answer is one Lua script — read the key, check against the limit, increment, set the TTL only if the window is fresh, return allow or deny — executed atomically in a single round trip. On the .NET side that's StackExchange.Redis and IDatabase.ScriptEvaluate with EVALSHA so the script body is cached server-side, or a library like RedisRateLimiting if you want it wrapped as ASP.NET Core middleware.`],quote:`Two round trips to Redis isn't a design — it's a race condition with extra latency.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Atomic?`,`Round trips`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`GET, check, SET`,`No`,`3`,`Race condition, over-admits`],[`INCR + EXPIRE every call`,`Per-command only`,`2`,`Window never resets`],[`INCR + TTL on first hit`,`Per-command only`,`1-2`,`Works, fiddly edge cases`],[`Single Lua script`,`Yes, whole script`,`1`,`The one you ship`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Your .NET Rate Limiter Fails at Scale (And How to Fix It)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ix52STEPp0`,at:`5:29`}},{heading:`05 · The question nobody asks until 3 AM: what if Redis blinks`,body:[`Last thing, and it's the question nobody asks until the pager goes off: what happens when Redis itself is down? You've just put a network dependency on the hot path of every request. Fail-open — allow everything — preserves availability but suspends your protection exactly during an incident, which is when attackers get curious. Fail-closed — reject everything — protects downstream but turns your limiter into a self-inflicted outage.`,`The compromise I like: a small per-instance token bucket as a shock absorber, sized around 10% of the global limit, used only when Redis is unreachable. A two-second Redis blip degrades you gracefully instead of flatlining you. And keep the happy path fast — same-region Redis, cached script SHA, a dedicated connection pool — because a limiter that adds 30 milliseconds to every request is just a tax your p99 pays forever.`],quote:`A rate limiter that can take your API down is just a slower outage with extra steps.`,table:{headers:[`Redis failure mode`,`Fail-open`,`Fail-closed`,`Local-buffer hybrid`],rows:[[`Brief blip, 1-5 s`,`All requests pass`,`All requests fail`,`Local bucket absorbs it`],[`Extended outage`,`Protection suspended`,`Self-inflicted outage`,`Degraded but alive`],[`Best for`,`Availability-critical APIs`,`Hard billing enforcement`,`Most real systems`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Your .NET Rate Limiter Fails at Scale (And How to Fix It)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ix52STEPp0`,at:`9:41`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Distributed rate limiting topology`,caption:`Every pod asks the same Redis counter before touching downstream — the local fallback bucket only wakes up when Redis is unreachable.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    C["Clients"] --> LB["Load balancer"]
    LB --> P1["API instance 1"]
    LB --> P2["API instance 2"]
    LB --> P3["API instance N"]
    P1 -->|"atomic check"| R[("Redis<br/>shared window counters")]
    P2 -->|"atomic check"| R
    P3 -->|"atomic check"| R
    P1 -->|"allowed"| D["Downstream<br/>DB / payment API"]
    P2 -->|"allowed"| D
    P3 -->|"allowed"| D
    P1 -.->|"Redis down"| F["Local token bucket<br/>~10% of global limit"]
    P2 -.-> F
    P3 -.-> F`},{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One request through the atomic limiter`,caption:`The check, the increment, and the TTL live inside a single Lua script — one round trip, one verdict.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as API instance
    participant R as Redis
    participant D as Downstream
    C->>A: GET /orders with API key
    A->>R: EVALSHA rate_limit_script key, limit 100, window 60s
    R->>R: INCR counter, set TTL only if new window
    alt count <= limit
        R-->>A: allowed, remaining 42
        A->>D: forward request
        D-->>A: 200 OK
        A-->>C: 200 OK, X-RateLimit-Remaining 42
    else count > limit
        R-->>A: denied
        A-->>C: 429 Too Many Requests, Retry-After 12s
    end`}],takeaways:[`The moment you run a second replica, an in-memory limit of X req/min becomes N×X — 8 pods turn a 100 req/min cap into 800. Ship the distributed limiter before the scale-out, not after the 3 AM page.`,`Budget the limiter hop at under ~5 ms: same-region Redis, EVALSHA with a cached script hash, a dedicated connection pool. A 30 ms limiter adds 30 ms to every request's p99, forever.`,`Fixed windows admit up to 2× the limit at boundaries — 100 requests at 0:59 plus 100 at 1:00. If bursts hurt downstream, use a sliding window counter or a token bucket with ~1.5-2× steady-rate burst capacity.`,`Make check-and-decrement one atomic Lua script (or INCR with the TTL set only on the first hit). GET-check-SET across three round trips over-admits exactly under peak load.`,`Decide fail-open vs fail-closed in advance, and size a per-instance fallback token bucket at ~10% of the global limit so a 2-5 second Redis blip degrades you gracefully instead of flatlining you.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Why Your .NET Rate Limiter Fails at Scale (And How to Fix It)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ix52STEPp0`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`rate-limiting`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-pACol04S_Ws`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:12,title:`Building Change Data Capture (CDC) in .NET with Debezium + RabbitMQ`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 21:40`,minutes:22,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`RabbitMQ`,`EF Core`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The night checkout charged $49.99 for a $19.99 product`,paragraphs:[`It's 14:00 during a blue-green cutover and I'm staring at two numbers that should be identical and aren't. The support dashboard says 'Premium Widget — $19.99.' Checkout is happily charging $49.99. Somewhere between our Postgres products table and the read model the pricing service trusts, reality forked — and I'm the on-call engineer holding both ends of the fork.`,`The root cause was embarrassingly classic: a dual-write. Our API committed the UPDATE to Postgres, then published a ProductUpdated event to RabbitMQ — and the pod got OOM-killed between the two lines. The database knew the truth; the broker never heard about it. We taped over the wound with retries, then a reconciliation cron, then a second cron to watch the first cron.`,`At the whiteboard the next morning we flipped the question: stop asking the application to remember to announce every change, and instead read the one record that has to exist anyway — the database's own transaction log. That's how Debezium, RabbitMQ, and MassTransit ended up in my docker-compose file, and how I spent a weekend discovering that Debezium's docs assume Kafka and MassTransit assumes its own envelope. This is the story of wiring all three.`],problem:`How do you convert one database's private log statements into messages a completely different system understands — without a single line of dual-write code?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`Debezium 2.7`,`pgoutput`,`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`EF Core`,`Docker Compose`,`RawJson Serializer`],note:`The pipeline pieces: Postgres exposes its WAL through pgoutput, Debezium Server 2.7 decodes it into JSON change events, RabbitMQ carries them, and MassTransit consumes them in .NET using its raw JSON serializer.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just dual-write?`,body:[`So here's the question I get every single time: why not just write to the database and publish the event from the same method? That's the dual-write, and it feels free until it isn't. The write commits, the process dies before the publish, and now your cache, your search index, and your checkout page are three different realities. I lived that at 2 AM. I don't recommend it.`,`The usual suspects don't save you either. Database triggers can capture changes, but now business logic lives in SQL and every schema change means migrating trigger code. Polling an updated_at column works until you need deletes, sub-second freshness, or you're scanning a table that's outgrown your patience. Both approaches force the app or the database to do a job neither was designed for.`,`Change data capture flips the question: instead of asking the application to announce changes, we listen to the one thing that has to record them anyway — the transaction log. The database already keeps a perfect diary. We just need to read it.`],quote:`If your application is the only thing telling the world about changes, every crash becomes a lie.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Dual-write (app publishes)`,`Trivial to write`,`Not atomic, silent drift on crash`,`Avoid`],[`Triggers + queue`,`Captures every write`,`Logic in SQL, write-path overhead`,`Legacy only`],[`Polling updated_at`,`Zero new infra`,`Misses deletes, latency, table scans`,`Tiny systems`],[`CDC (log tailing)`,`Atomic source, low overhead`,`One more moving part`,`Distributed systems`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Change Data Capture (CDC) in .NET with Debezium + RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pACol04S_Ws`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the log is the truth`,body:[`Let me walk the boundary for a second, because this is the part that made CDC click for me. Every time you execute an insert, update, or delete, Postgres doesn't just mutate pages in place — it appends commands to a transaction log, the write-ahead log. Replay that log from the beginning and you reconstruct the entire state of the database. That's not an implementation detail; that's the design.`,`And because databases expose a way to read that log — Postgres calls it logical decoding, and the built-in output plugin is pgoutput — we can attach a second process that tails it. That process is Debezium: an open-source CDC server running alongside your database, turning each committed row change into a structured event. No triggers, no polling, no app code. The commit is the publish, atomically.`,`The catch — and it's the whole problem statement of this talk — is that the log speaks Postgres, and nobody downstream speaks Postgres. The trick is converting one database's log statements into something the other side understands.`],quote:`The WAL never forgets and never lies — it's the only witness present at every commit.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Change Data Capture (CDC) in .NET with Debezium + RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pACol04S_Ws`,at:`4:40`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring Debezium to RabbitMQ — the Kafka-first tax`,body:[`Now the wiring. Debezium ships as a Docker image — I pinned 2.7, the latest stable at the time — and dropped it into my docker-compose next to Postgres and RabbitMQ. Configuration is a wall of environment variables; the alternative is a config file you pass to the server. You name the connector, set plugin.name to pgoutput so it reads the write-ahead log, then point it at your Postgres host, username, password, and database — mine is called Commerce.`,`Then the part that actually saves you: scoping. Tell it which schema to watch — public — and which tables — in my case just products. Add more tables to the list if you must, but every table you include is event volume you pay for forever. Finally, a topic prefix, which Debezium prepends to the topic where it publishes each change.`,`Here's the friction nobody warns you about: Debezium is built Kafka-first. The docs, the tutorials, the happy path — all Kafka. I didn't want a Kafka cluster for one consumer, so I used Debezium Server's RabbitMQ sink. It works, but it's a second-class citizen and noticeably less flexible — you'll be hand-tuning sink environment variables and verifying events in the RabbitMQ management UI.`],quote:`Debezium speaks Kafka natively; every other broker is a conversation with an accent.`,table:{headers:[`Sink`,`Setup effort`,`Docs coverage`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Kafka Connect`,`Low — golden path`,`Extensive`,`Large event backbones`],[`Debezium Server → RabbitMQ`,`Medium — env-var tuning`,`Thin`,`Lean .NET stacks`],[`Debezium Server → other sinks`,`Medium`,`Sparse`,`Case by case`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Change Data Capture (CDC) in .NET with Debezium + RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pACol04S_Ws`,at:`6:45`}},{heading:`04 · The envelope problem: MassTransit meets raw JSON`,body:[`Next surprise: the messages arrive and MassTransit drops them on the floor. Why? MassTransit expects its own envelope — a wrapper carrying type information so it knows which consumer handles which message. Debezium doesn't send envelopes; it sends raw JSON describing the row: an operation code, the before image, the after image.`,`The fix is MassTransit's raw JSON serializer — you opt in to receiving raw JSON and route it yourself. Then I wrote my consumer: a plain IConsumer<ProductUpdate>, where ProductUpdate is a flat copy of my product entity — Id, Name, Description, Price. Simple, boring, exactly what a message contract should be.`,`But here's the honest part: Debezium's payload is not that shape. You get op, before, after — so there's a mapping step where you pull the after image and project it into your contract. Don't skip it and don't fight it; the mapper is the treaty between Debezium's world and yours. And pin your Debezium version — 2.7 for me — because that payload shape is effectively your integration contract.`],quote:`Your message contract is yours; Debezium's payload is Debezium's — the mapper is the treaty between them.`,table:{headers:[`Aspect`,`MassTransit envelope`,`Debezium JSON`],rows:[[`Type routing`,`Embedded message types`,`Topic / routing key`],[`Payload shape`,`Your contract class`,`op + before + after`],[`Serializer`,`Default JSON envelope`,`RawJson serializer`],[`Consumer binding`,`Direct`,`Via mapping layer`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Change Data Capture (CDC) in .NET with Debezium + RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pACol04S_Ws`,at:`10:06`}},{heading:`05 · Where this pays off — and what I'm building next`,body:[`So what is this firehose actually good for? Four things I keep coming back to. ETL pipelines — extract, transform, load — driven by real changes instead of nightly batch jobs. Database replication across a distributed system, where a second service owns a read-optimized copy. Projections: build read models for CQRS from the same change stream, even into a completely different database engine.`,`And the one I'm actually building toward: an outbox implemented by transaction log tailing. Put your outgoing messages in an outbox table, commit them in the same transaction as your business data, and let CDC watch that table and publish to the broker. You get the outbox pattern's atomicity guarantee without writing a dispatcher. That's the next video.`,`The mental shift is this: CDC isn't a feature you add, it's a firehose you own. Point it at something that compounds — a read model, a replica, an outbox — and every future feature inherits the stream for free.`],quote:`CDC isn't a feature you add — it's a firehose you own. Point it at something that compounds.`,table:{headers:[`Use case`,`Consumer of the stream`,`Win`],rows:[[`ETL pipeline`,`Warehouse / analytics`,`Near-real-time loads`],[`Replication`,`Secondary service DB`,`Decoupled copies`],[`CQRS projections`,`Read-model store`,`Always-fresh read side`],[`Outbox tailing`,`Message broker`,`Atomic publish-on-commit`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Change Data Capture (CDC) in .NET with Debezium + RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pACol04S_Ws`,at:`12:22`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cdc-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`CDC topology: Postgres to .NET via Debezium and RabbitMQ`,caption:`The full pipeline: commits land in Postgres, the WAL is decoded by Debezium Server, and JSON change events flow through RabbitMQ into MassTransit consumers.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph WritePath["Write path"]
        API[".NET API<br/>EF Core"] --> PG[("Postgres<br/>Commerce DB")]
    end
    PG -- "WAL stream<br/>(pgoutput)" --> DBZ["Debezium Server 2.7"]
    DBZ -- "JSON change events<br/>topic prefix: commerce" --> RMQ[("RabbitMQ")]
    RMQ -- "raw JSON" --> C["MassTransit Consumer<br/>RawJsonSerializer"]
    C --> RD[("Read model<br/>/ replica DB")]`},{id:`change-event-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Life of a single product update`,caption:`Request-path trace of one UPDATE, from the EF Core commit through the replication slot to a read-model update in the consumer.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as .NET API (EF Core)
    participant PG as Postgres
    participant DBZ as Debezium Server
    participant MQ as RabbitMQ
    participant C as MassTransit Consumer

    App->>PG: INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE on products
    Note over PG: Change appended to WAL
    DBZ->>PG: Read replication slot (pgoutput)
    PG-->>DBZ: Row image (before / after)
    DBZ->>MQ: Publish JSON to commerce.public.products
    MQ-->>C: Deliver raw JSON message
    C->>C: RawJsonSerializer to ProductUpdate
    C->>C: Map after-image, update read model`}],takeaways:[`One connector, one replication slot: an abandoned slot pins the WAL and can fill your Postgres disk within days — drop slots the moment you decommission a connector.`,`Filter at the source: scope the connector to one schema and an explicit table list — a whole-database stream is easily 10x the events you actually need.`,`Budget for the Kafka-first tax: Debezium-to-Kafka is copy-paste, but Debezium Server to RabbitMQ means hand-tuned sink environment variables — plan roughly an extra day of config.`,`Register MassTransit's RawJson serializer first — without it, 100% of Debezium messages fail the envelope check before your consumer code ever runs.`,`Never bind Debezium's payload straight to your entity: it speaks op + before/after, so keep a mapping layer and pin the version (2.7) as your integration contract.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Change Data Capture (CDC) in .NET with Debezium + RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pACol04S_Ws`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`change-data-capture`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-XALvnX7MPeo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:13,title:`Transactional Outbox Pattern | Clean Architecture, .NET 6`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 20:43`,minutes:21,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`Outbox`],story:{headline:`The welcome emails for members who never existed`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:45 when batch reconciliation started on launch night. Marketing's signup campaign is live and the dashboard shows 412 new members. Then support pings: 38 of them never got their welcome email. You pull the logs — the member row is there, committed, happy. The MemberRegisteredDomainEvent? Nowhere. The publish call fired after SaveChangesAsync, the broker connection dropped for 900 milliseconds, and the event evaporated. No retry, no trace, no email.`,`Worse is the mirror image from the week before: the handler published the event before saving, the welcome email went out, and then the transaction rolled back on a duplicate-email constraint. A warm greeting for a member who doesn't exist. Two incidents, one root cause — we were writing to the database and publishing to the broker as two separate fates.`,`The fix is embarrassingly boring: stop publishing at write time. Raise the domain event, let an EF Core interceptor turn it into an OutboxMessage row, and commit it in the same transaction as the member. A Quartz job drains the outbox later, in batches of 20. Same events, same consumers — but now the database, not hope, guarantees delivery.`],problem:`You cannot make a database write and a broker publish atomic across two systems — the dual-write problem. The outbox pattern folds the message into the same transaction as the business data, then publishes it from a separate process.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET 6`,`EF Core`,`SQL Server`,`Quartz`,`EF Interceptors`,`Domain Events`,`Outbox Pattern`,`JSON`],note:`EF Core save-changes interceptors sweep raised domain events into OutboxMessage rows committed atomically with the aggregate; a Quartz background job polls the outbox table, deserializes the JSON, and publishes each event.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why save-then-publish fails`,body:[`Here's the naive version we've all shipped at least once: the CreateMember command handler checks email uniqueness, calls the Member factory, inserts into the repository, saves changes, and then publishes MemberRegisteredDomainEvent to the bus. Looks fine in a demo. In production you have two systems — SQL Server and the broker — and no transaction on earth spans both.`,`A crash, a deploy, or a dropped connection between the commit and the publish, and the event is gone forever. Flip the order — publish first, save second — and a rolled-back transaction leaves consumers reacting to a member that never existed. We lived both sides of that in the same month.`,`The outbox pattern's insight is that you already have one system that must succeed atomically: the database. So don't publish at write time at all. Write the message as a row, in the same transaction as the member, and publish from a separate process later. The dual-write problem doesn't get solved — it gets collapsed into a single write.`],quote:`Two systems, two fates — until you make the message just another row.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Publish before commit`,`Consumers react instantly`,`Phantom events when the transaction rolls back`,`Never`],[`Publish after commit`,`No phantom events`,`Lost events if the process dies between commit and publish`,`Never`],[`Transactional outbox`,`Exactly one atomic commit, self-healing redelivery`,`Small publish delay, needs a dispatcher job`,`Default for critical events`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Transactional Outbox Pattern | Clean Architecture, .NET 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XALvnX7MPeo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: where the event becomes a row`,body:[`Inside Clean Architecture the domain stays pure: Member.Create calls the constructor and raises a MemberRegisteredDomainEvent in memory. The handler that used to send the welcome email directly now just needs the event to survive the transaction. The question is where to intercept it — and the answer is the same place the transaction lives: SaveChanges.`,`EF Core lets you hook an interceptor into the DbContext. We override SaveChangesAsync, sweep the raised domain events off the entities, and convert each one into an OutboxMessage with a single call: DbContext.Set<OutboxMessage>().AddRange(...). Because this happens inside SaveChanges, the member insert and the outbox insert share one transaction, one commit, one fate.`,`The beauty is that the command handler doesn't change at all. No publisher, no bus dependency in the write path. The domain raises events, the interceptor translates, the database commits. The boundary between 'what happened' and 'who gets told' moves entirely to the persistence edge.`],quote:`Don't publish the event — commit it.`,table:{headers:[`Conversion point`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In the command handler`,`Explicit, easy to read`,`Boilerplate in every handler, easy to forget one`,`Small apps`],[`EF Core interceptor on SaveChanges`,`Zero handler changes, physically impossible to forget`,`Magic at the persistence edge, needs DI wiring`,`Clean Architecture default`],[`Post-commit pipeline hook`,`Decoupled from EF`,`Still runs outside the transaction — no atomicity`,`Avoid for outbox`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Transactional Outbox Pattern | Clean Architecture, .NET 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XALvnX7MPeo`,at:`1:19`}},{heading:`03 · The shape of an outbox message`,body:[`The OutboxMessage entity is deliberately dull: the domain event's type name, the Content serialized as a JSON string, an OccurredOn timestamp, and a ProcessedOnDate that starts life as null. That nullable column is doing more work than it looks — 'ProcessedOnDate IS NULL' is the entire queue.`,`Storing the type as a string plus JSON content means the dispatcher doesn't need a reference to the domain assembly at write time, and can reconstruct the right .NET type later with JsonConvert. It also means one table, one schema, every event — no per-event tables, no migration every time the domain grows a new event.`,`Yes, you could point a real broker queue at this problem. But the broker can't be inside your transaction. The outbox table is the only queue your database already knows how to keep atomic — and once processed, the row stays behind as a free audit log.`],quote:`The outbox table is a queue your database already knows how to keep consistent.`,table:{headers:[`Queue choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Outbox table only`,`Atomic with business data, built-in audit trail`,`Polling latency, table growth`,`Default starting point`],[`Broker only`,`Low latency, horizontal scale`,`The dual-write problem returns`,`Never for critical writes`],[`Outbox + broker relay`,`Atomic write plus broker-scale fan-out`,`Two moving parts to operate`,`High-throughput systems`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Transactional Outbox Pattern | Clean Architecture, .NET 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XALvnX7MPeo`,at:`7:19`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it up: Program.cs and the service-provider overload`,body:[`Now the plumbing. In Program.cs we register the interceptor as a singleton, then register the DbContext — and here's the detail that trips people: the plain AddDbContext overload doesn't let you resolve services, so we switch to the overload that accepts a service provider. From there we grab the interceptor and hand it to UseSqlServer via options.AddInterceptors(...).`,`Why singleton? Interceptors must be stateless and thread-safe — they'll be invoked by every scoped DbContext concurrently. Keep them that way and the lifetime question disappears. It's five lines of wiring for a guarantee that holds for every write in the application, forever.`],quote:`Boring wiring is what makes a strong guarantee boring to trust.`,table:{headers:[`Interceptor lifetime`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Singleton`,`One instance, resolves cleanly in the service-provider overload`,`Must be stateless and thread-safe`,`Recommended`],[`Scoped`,`Per-request state is possible`,`Awkward to resolve inside the DbContext registration`,`Rarely needed`],[`Transient`,`Simplest to reason about`,`Extra allocations per context`,`Fine, slightly wasteful`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Transactional Outbox Pattern | Clean Architecture, .NET 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XALvnX7MPeo`,at:`13:31`}},{heading:`05 · The background job: drain the outbox in batches of 20`,body:[`The second component is a background job — Quartz in our case. Execute is async from the first line: query Set<OutboxMessage>() where ProcessedOnDate is null, Take(20), ToListAsync, passing the cancellation token from the job execution context. Twenty isn't magic — it's just small enough that each cycle stays short and the transaction doesn't grow with your backlog.`,`For each message: deserialize the JSON content back into the typed domain event, publish it so the MemberRegistered handler runs and the email service sends the welcome email, then stamp ProcessedOnDate and save. If the process dies mid-batch, the unstamped rows are simply picked up on the next run — that's at-least-once delivery, which means every consumer must be idempotent. A duplicate welcome email is annoying; a lost signup confirmation is a support ticket at 3 AM.`],quote:`Deliver at-least-once, and make every consumer idempotent.`,table:{headers:[`Batch size`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`1 per run`,`Simplest failure isolation`,`Painfully slow drain under load`,`Low volume`],[`20 per run`,`Short cycles, bounded locks, easy retries`,`Backlog drains linearly`,`Sweet spot for most apps`],[`1000+ per run`,`Fast drain of large backlogs`,`Long transactions, lock contention, big rollbacks`,`Only with careful tuning`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Transactional Outbox Pattern | Clean Architecture, .NET 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XALvnX7MPeo`,at:`18:13`}}],diagrams:[{id:`outbox-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Outbox topology: one atomic write, one async publisher`,caption:`The write path commits the member and the outbox row together; the Quartz job drains the outbox and publishes to consumers.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph W["Write path — one atomic commit"]
        A["CreateMemberCommand handler"] --> B["Member.Create() raises MemberRegisteredDomainEvent"]
        B --> C["EF Core SaveChangesAsync"]
        C --> D["Outbox interceptor"]
        D --> E[("Members table")]
        D --> F[("OutboxMessages table<br/>ProcessedOnUtc = NULL")]
    end
    subgraph P["Publish path — Quartz background job"]
        G["Quartz OutboxJob"] -->|"SELECT TOP 20<br/>WHERE ProcessedOnUtc IS NULL"| F
        G --> H["Deserialize JSON to typed event"]
        H --> I["Publish to message bus"]
        I --> J["MemberRegistered handler"]
        J --> K["Email service: welcome email"]
        G -->|"UPDATE ProcessedOnUtc"| F
    end
    E -. "same transaction" .- F`},{id:`outbox-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request flow: atomic commit, then async drain`,caption:`The interceptor converts domain events during SaveChanges, so the member and its outbox row live or die together; the job publishes later and stamps ProcessedOnUtc.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as CreateMember handler
    participant EF as EF Core + Interceptor
    participant DB as SQL Server
    participant Job as Quartz OutboxJob
    participant Bus as Message Bus
    participant Email as Email Service

    API->>API: Member.Create() raises MemberRegisteredDomainEvent
    API->>EF: SaveChangesAsync()
    EF->>EF: Interceptor converts events to OutboxMessage rows
    EF->>DB: BEGIN TRANSACTION
    EF->>DB: INSERT Member
    EF->>DB: INSERT OutboxMessage (Type, JSON Content, ProcessedOnUtc = NULL)
    EF->>DB: COMMIT
    Note over DB: Member + outbox row commit together — all or nothing
    Job->>DB: SELECT TOP 20 WHERE ProcessedOnUtc IS NULL
    DB-->>Job: Outbox message
    Job->>Job: JsonConvert.DeserializeObject to typed event
    Job->>Bus: Publish MemberRegisteredDomainEvent
    Bus->>Email: Handle(event)
    Email->>Email: Send welcome email
    Job->>DB: UPDATE ProcessedOnUtc = UTCNOW`}],takeaways:[`Commit the outbox row in the same transaction as the aggregate — one SaveChanges, zero dual-write anomalies.`,`Drain the outbox in small batches (20 at a time) so each job cycle stays short and lock contention stays low.`,`Treat 'ProcessedOnDate IS NULL' as your queue cursor — stamp it only after publish succeeds, and unstamped rows self-heal on the next run.`,`Store the event type name plus JSON content so the dispatcher can rebuild the right .NET type minutes or hours later.`,`The outbox buys at-least-once delivery, not exactly-once — make every consumer idempotent, because redelivery is a when, not an if.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Transactional Outbox Pattern | Clean Architecture, .NET 6`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XALvnX7MPeo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`change-data-capture`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-O7oaxFgNuYo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:14,title:`Real-Time Notifications Using Blazor and SignalR from scratch`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 19:44`,minutes:20,difficulty:3,tags:[`SignalR`,`Blazor`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The dashboard that lied: five seconds of silence at 3 AM`,paragraphs:[`It's 3 AM and an on-call engineer is staring at an internal Blazor dashboard that is supposed to show live server events. The numbers haven't moved in twenty minutes. Swagger on the API responds fine, the health endpoint is green, the browser tab is open — and the page is just... quiet. Everyone's first instinct is 'the backend is down.' It isn't. The events are firing; they're just never landing anywhere the user can see.`,`This is the exact gap that SignalR plus Blazor WebAssembly exists to close. The backend is an empty ASP.NET Core Web API — no endpoints, just Swagger staring back at you. The client is the default Blazor WASM scaffold: Home, Counter, FetchData. Nothing about either template hints at real-time anything. The whole feature has to be built from two moving parts: a Hub on the server, and a HubConnection in the browser.`,`The failure mode in the war story is almost always one of three things: the browser can't even negotiate the connection (CORS), the connection drops and nobody reconnects (no automatic reconnect), or the message arrives but the UI never repaints (missing StateHasChanged). All three are invisible in Swagger, because Swagger only knows request/response. Real-time is a different contract entirely.`],problem:`How do you push server events to a Blazor WebAssembly client the instant they happen — typed end to end, resilient to drops, and without hand-rolling your own socket protocol?`},tech:{stack:[`SignalR`,`Blazor WASM`,`ASP.NET Core`,`Typed Hubs`,`IHubContext`,`PeriodicTimer`,`BackgroundService`],note:`The exact moving parts of the demo: a strongly typed SignalR hub hosted on an empty ASP.NET Core API, a BackgroundService that pushes server time every five seconds via IHubContext, and a Blazor WebAssembly client that renders whatever lands.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why polling feels dirty`,body:[`Let me talk this out. The naive answer to 'show live data in the browser' is polling: the client hits an endpoint every N seconds and re-renders. And honestly, for a demo, polling works. But you're paying for every tick whether or not anything changed — N requests per client per interval, and your 'real-time' latency is bounded below by your poll interval. At 5-second polling with 10,000 clients, that's 2,000 requests a second doing mostly nothing.`,`Why not Server-Sent Events, then? SSE is one-way server-to-client, which is exactly our shape here. It's a legitimate choice. But we're in .NET on both ends, and SignalR gives us connection management, automatic negotiation, reconnection primitives, and — the part I care about most — strongly typed client contracts. SSE gives you a raw text stream and you rebuild the plumbing yourself.`,`So the decision writes itself: SignalR over WebSockets, with the Hub as the server-side meeting point. The Blazor WASM client connects once, the server pushes whenever it wants, and the connection stays warm. That's the architecture. Now let's build it from an empty template and see where the friction actually lives.`],quote:`Polling asks 'anything new?' five thousand times a second. A hub waits, then tells you.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`HTTP polling`,`Trivial to build, works everywhere`,`Wasted requests, latency = poll interval, no server push`,`Demos, low-frequency dashboards`],[`Server-Sent Events`,`One-way push, simple protocol`,`Text-only, no typed contract, manual reconnect logic`,`Simple one-way feeds`],[`SignalR (WebSockets)`,`Full-duplex, typed hubs, negotiation + reconnect built in`,`More moving parts, CORS and scale-out to think about`,`Real-time .NET-to-.NET — the sweet spot`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Real-Time Notifications Using Blazor and SignalR from scratch`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O7oaxFgNuYo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the Hub and its typed client`,body:[`First move on the backend: AddSignalR in the service collection, then define the Hub. I'll call it NotificationsHub and derive from the Hub base class — that class is the central component every client connects to. Out of the box, though, Hub is stringly typed: you invoke client methods by name, and a typo in a method name compiles perfectly and fails silently in production. That's the kind of bug that costs you a night of sleep.`,`So here's the pattern I always reach for: define an interface — INotificationClient — with the methods the server is allowed to call on the client. In our case that's one async method, ReceiveNotification, taking a single string message. Then use the generic variant: NotificationsHub derives from Hub<INotificationClient>. Now the compiler enforces the contract. Inside the hub, the only methods I can invoke on Clients are the ones on that interface. Rename the method in the interface and every call site lights up red. That's the boundary made explicit.`,`Why not just send raw JSON over a WebSocket and parse it client-side? You could. But then the contract lives in your head and in a comment somewhere. With a typed hub, the contract lives in the type system, and it survives refactors. One interface, two projects, zero drift.`],quote:`A stringly typed hub is a runtime surprise. A typed hub is a compile-time guarantee.`,table:{headers:[`Hub style`,`Pros`,`Cons`],rows:[[`Hub (untyped)`,`Zero setup, invoke any method name`,`Typos fail silently, no IntelliSense, contract drift`],[`Hub<IClient>`,`Compile-time contract, IntelliSense on both ends`,`One extra interface to maintain`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Real-Time Notifications Using Blazor and SignalR from scratch`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O7oaxFgNuYo`,at:`0:34`}},{heading:`03 · Pushing from the background: IHubContext and PeriodicTimer`,body:[`Here's the part people get wrong: the hub itself is only for when a client triggers something. But our notifications originate on the server — a background job that fires every five seconds. You don't inject the Hub for that; you inject IHubContext<NotificationsHub, INotificationClient>. The context is your broadcast antenna into every connected client, usable from anywhere in the dependency graph. And because we went typed, it's the generic variant — hub type first, client interface second — so context.Clients.All.ReceiveNotification(...) is a real, checked method call.`,`For the timing, I'm registering a hosted service — ServerTimeNotifier — with an ILogger<ServerTimeNotifier> and that hub context injected through the constructor. Inside ExecuteAsync, the modern move is PeriodicTimer, not System.Timers.Timer. I create it with a TimeSpan of five seconds, then loop: while the stopping token hasn't fired, await timer.WaitForNextTickAsync(stoppingToken), then push. No overlapping ticks, no event-handler thread gymnastics, and cancellation is a first-class citizen. When the app shuts down, the loop exits cleanly.`,`Why not a Timer with an elapsed event, or a Task.Delay loop? The elapsed event fires on a thread-pool thread you don't own and can overlap itself under load. Task.Delay drifts and swallows cancellation awkwardly. PeriodicTimer is the one primitive designed exactly for 'do X every N seconds inside a BackgroundService, and stop when I tell you.' It's five lines of code and it's correct.`],quote:`IHubContext is the antenna; PeriodicTimer is the metronome. Neither needs the hub class itself.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Real-Time Notifications Using Blazor and SignalR from scratch`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O7oaxFgNuYo`,at:`4:37`}},{heading:`04 · The Blazor side: HubConnectionBuilder and the re-render trap`,body:[`Over in the client, Index.razor is a Blazor component: a route at the top, a page title, markup, and a code block. In that code block I hold two pieces of state — a HubConnection and a read-only list of strings for the messages. The lifecycle hook that matters is OnInitializedAsync: that's where I build the connection with HubConnectionBuilder, point WithUrl at the backend's hub route (the path must match what the server mapped), and call Build.`,`Then the callback: connection.On("ReceiveNotification", msg => ...) — the string name must match the method on INotificationClient, which is exactly why the typed interface on the server is worth its weight; it's the single source of truth for this magic string. The handler appends the message to the list... and here's the trap. SignalR callbacks fire outside Blazor's render loop. If you mutate state and don't tell the renderer, the message is in memory but invisible on screen. You must call StateHasChanged — or better, wrap the mutation in InvokeAsync so it's marshaled onto the renderer's synchronization context safely.`,`Finally, await connection.StartAsync() to actually open the pipe. Why not connect at app startup in Program.cs instead of in the component? Because component-scoped connections die with the component and are easy to re-create; app-scoped singletons need you to handle every navigation, every reconnect, and every dispose yourself. For a page-level feed, component-scoped is the honest scope.`],quote:`The message arrived. The screen didn't change. That's not a SignalR bug — that's a missing StateHasChanged.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Real-Time Notifications Using Blazor and SignalR from scratch`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O7oaxFgNuYo`,at:`8:43`}},{heading:`05 · What breaks at 3 AM: CORS, reconnects, scale-out`,body:[`Now let's name the three failure modes from the war story. First: CORS. Blazor WASM runs on its own origin, so the browser will refuse the negotiate request unless the API explicitly allows that origin — and with credentials, because SignalR's negotiate uses them. The symptom is maddening: Swagger works, curl works, the page is dead. Check the browser console before you check the server logs.`,`Second: drops. Mobile networks, proxy timeouts, laptop sleep — connections die, and a dead connection looks exactly like 'nothing is happening.' Wire up WithAutomaticReconnect with a backoff array, and handle Reconnecting/Reconnected events so the UI can show a banner instead of silently lying. Third: scale-out. IHubContext broadcasts to clients connected to that one server instance. The moment you run two replicas behind a load balancer, half your clients go deaf unless you add a backplane — Redis is the standard move — or enable sticky sessions.`,`None of these show up in the happy-path demo, and all of them show up in week one of production. The five-second timer is the demo; the reconnect policy and the backplane are the product. Build the demo in an afternoon, budget the product hardening for the sprint after.`],quote:`The demo is a hub and a timer. Production is CORS, reconnects, and a backplane.`,table:{headers:[`Failure mode`,`Symptom`,`Fix`],rows:[[`CORS misconfigured`,`Swagger works, browser negotiate fails`,`Allow the WASM origin with credentials on the API`],[`Connection drop`,`Page silently stops updating`,`WithAutomaticReconnect + Reconnecting UI banner`],[`Missing re-render`,`Messages in memory, not on screen`,`InvokeAsync(() => { ...; StateHasChanged(); })`],[`Multiple server instances`,`Some clients receive, some don't`,`Redis backplane or sticky sessions`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Real-Time Notifications Using Blazor and SignalR from scratch`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O7oaxFgNuYo`,at:`13:13`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Push topology: Blazor WASM to typed hub to background notifier`,caption:`The browser holds a HubConnection; the BackgroundService never touches the Hub class — it broadcasts through IHubContext, and the typed client interface is the contract on both ends.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Client["Blazor WebAssembly in the browser"]
    UI["Index.razor page"] --> HC["HubConnection"]
  end
  subgraph Server["ASP.NET Core Web API"]
    HUB["NotificationsHub (typed hub)"]
    CTX["IHubContext (typed)"]
    BG["ServerTimeNotifier BackgroundService"]
  end
  HC -- "negotiate, then WebSocket" --> HUB
  BG -- "every 5 seconds" --> CTX
  CTX -- "Clients.All.ReceiveNotification" --> HUB
  HUB -- "ReceiveNotification(string)" --> HC
  HC -- "add to list + StateHasChanged" --> UI`},{id:`sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Connection and notification lifecycle`,caption:`One negotiate-and-upgrade handshake, then a five-second push loop driven by PeriodicTimer inside the hosted service, ending in a Blazor re-render.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant B as Blazor WASM client
  participant S as ASP.NET Core API
  participant N as ServerTimeNotifier
  B->>S: POST negotiate (hub route)
  S-->>B: connectionId + connection token
  B->>S: WebSocket upgrade to hub path
  Note over B,S: SignalR connection established
  loop every 5 seconds
    N->>N: PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync
    N->>S: IHubContext.Clients.All
    S->>B: ReceiveNotification(message)
    B->>B: messages.Add + StateHasChanged
  end`}],takeaways:[`Go typed at 2+ client methods: Hub<INotificationClient> plus IHubContext<NotificationsHub, INotificationClient> turns every stringly-typed method-name typo into a compile error instead of a silent production failure.`,`In a BackgroundService, prefer PeriodicTimer over System.Timers.Timer — no overlapping ticks, native CancellationToken support via WaitForNextTickAsync(stoppingToken), and clean shutdown for free.`,`Every SignalR callback that mutates Blazor state must end in InvokeAsync(() => StateHasChanged()) — the renderer doesn't know your handler ran, and one missing line means messages arrive but never appear.`,`Configure CORS (allowed origin + credentials) for the WASM client before first connect — a missing policy presents as 'Swagger works, page is dead,' and costs a 30-minute mystery every time.`,`Demo tick is 5 seconds; production posture is WithAutomaticReconnect with a backoff array (e.g., 0s, 2s, 5s, 10s, 30s) plus a Redis backplane or sticky sessions the moment you run more than one server instance.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Real-Time Notifications Using Blazor and SignalR from scratch`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O7oaxFgNuYo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`realtime-channels`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-O-FEWH4HK7w`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:15,title:`Fix Your Scaling Bottlenecks with Message Queues (Webhooks series part 4)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:23`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`],story:{headline:`12,000 subscribers, one BackgroundService, and a 3 AM delivery backlog`,paragraphs:[`It's 09:30 during a latency regression audit and the delivery lag graph is climbing like it has somewhere to be. An 'order created' event landed on our in-memory channel, the publisher returned instantly — the caller was happy, the API was happy — and then the Webhook Processor, a single BackgroundService, started grinding through the subscriber list. Fetch subscriptions from the database, send one HTTP request, wait, send the next one, wait. Every external API on the internet gets to pick its own response time, and tonight they are all picking 'slow'.`,`Here's the cruel part: the system was already 'async'. We did the right thing in the last video — the channel decoupled the publisher, control returned to the caller immediately. But we didn't remove the cost of fan-out, we just moved it off the request path and into a dark corner where nobody was watching. And the subscriber count is theoretically infinite, which means one process doing every HTTP call was never going to keep up. Worse: the channel lives in RAM, so a restart mid-backlog doesn't pause the deliveries — it deletes them.`,`The fix wasn't more threads or a bigger box. It was changing what a 'unit of work' means: stop sending N HTTP requests per event, and start sending exactly one HTTP request per message.`],problem:`A single in-process consumer fans out to an unbounded subscriber list over HTTP, capping delivery throughput at one machine and silently dropping queued work on every restart.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`MassTransit`,`RabbitMQ`,`In-Memory Channels`,`.NET Aspire`],note:`MassTransit over RabbitMQ replaces the in-memory channel so fan-out and delivery become two independently scalable, durable queues — with Aspire wiring the broker's lifecycle before the API starts.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: name the bottleneck before you scale anything`,body:[`You can't scale what you can't name, so before touching a line of code we walked the path one more time. The publisher writes an event to an in-memory channel and completes immediately — that part is genuinely fine. The problem is what happens next: a background service pulls the message, fetches every subscriber for that event from the database, and sends each one an HTTP request, one by one, in a batch.`,`That loop is the bottleneck, and it's two bottlenecks wearing a trench coat. The database read grows linearly with your subscriber count, and then the HTTP calls grow linearly with your subscriber count — against external systems whose latency you do not control. Adding 'async' earlier moved this work off the request path; it never made it cheaper.`],quote:`Async isn't fast. It's just slow somewhere you weren't looking.`,table:{headers:[`Step in the loop`,`Cost driver`,`Grows with`],rows:[[`Fetch subscribers`,`DB query per event`,`Subscriber count`],[`Send HTTP per subscriber`,`External response time`,`Subscriber count × latency`],[`Total event handling time`,`Sum of the above`,`Effectively unbounded`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Scaling Bottlenecks with Message Queues (Webhooks series part 4)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O-FEWH4HK7w`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the fan-out boundary`,body:[`Let's be honest about the shape of this thing. On one side: our application service. On the other side: a theoretically infinite number of external subscribers. Every message we handle means an unbounded number of outbound HTTP calls to endpoints we don't own, can't SLA, and can't shut up. That boundary — our process talking directly to N strangers inside one handler — is the actual design flaw.`,`So the obvious question: why not just run more processor replicas? Because the in-memory channel is per-process. It's a .NET object living inside one instance's RAM. You can't load-balance work across replicas through it, you can't share the backlog, and if that instance dies, everything buffered in the channel dies with it. Scaling the API pods does precisely nothing for the delivery loop.`],quote:`You can't load-balance a queue that lives inside someone's RAM.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`In-memory channel`,`Zero infra, instant, no serialization`,`Per-process, volatile, unbounded fan-out in one handler`,`Dev and demos only`],[`More processor replicas`,`No new moving parts`,`Channel isn't shared — work duplicated or stranded`,`Doesn't fit`],[`Broker + split queues`,`Durable, shared, horizontally scalable`,`New infrastructure to operate`,`Production`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Scaling Bottlenecks with Message Queues (Webhooks series part 4)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O-FEWH4HK7w`,at:`1:00`}},{heading:`03 · Splitting one overloaded consumer into two cheap jobs`,body:[`Here's the insight that unlocked everything: that poor consumer was doing two completely different jobs. Job one is 'figure out who cares about this event' — that's a database read, it's fast, it's batchable, it's milliseconds. Job two is 'talk to external HTTP endpoints' — that's slow, flaky, and unbounded. We fused them into one handler, so the slow job throttled the fast one.`,`So we split them along the broker. The event lands on an events queue in RabbitMQ. The first consumer picks it up, fetches the subscriptions — in a batch if we like — and instead of making N HTTP calls, it enqueues N messages onto a separate webhooks queue. Then it acknowledges and moves on. A second consumer handles that queue, and its entire job is: one message, one HTTP request, one subscriber. Acknowledge, next.`,`Now the scope of any single unit of work is tiny and predictable. And because the two queues have wildly different latency profiles, they scale independently — which is exactly what we'll lean on in a future video when we scale the consumers.`],quote:`One event with N subscribers should become N messages — not N HTTP calls inside one handler.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Scaling Bottlenecks with Message Queues (Webhooks series part 4)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O-FEWH4HK7w`,at:`3:34`}},{heading:`04 · Swapping the channel for MassTransit — the boring, beautiful part`,body:[`The implementation was almost anticlimactic, which is how you know the design was right. On the publishing side, the Webhook Dispatcher stops writing to a Channel and starts using MassTransit's IPublishEndpoint — call Publish with the message and you're done. While we're in there, we rename WebHookDispatch to WebHookDispatched, because it's an event now, not a command. And we delete the manual ActivityId propagation entirely — MassTransit's diagnostics carry the trace context across the broker for us, so that property and that plumbing just go away.`,`On the consuming side, a consumer is just a class implementing IConsumer<WebHookDispatched> with a Consume method. We inject the DbContext because we still need to read subscriptions, pull the message off the ConsumeContext, and for each subscription, publish the next message. One more thing before any of this runs: Aspire gets a RabbitMQ resource, and the API instance is told to wait for the broker to start. Infrastructure as orchestration, not as a README paragraph.`],quote:`If your transport makes you hand-carry trace IDs, you're using the wrong transport.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Before (channels)`,`After (MassTransit)`],rows:[[`Publish`,`ChannelWriter.WriteAsync`,`IPublishEndpoint.Publish`],[`Consume`,`BackgroundService loop`,`IConsumer<T>.Consume`],[`Tracing`,`Manual ActivityId propagation`,`Automatic via diagnostics`],[`Durability`,`Lost on restart`,`Broker-persisted`],[`Startup`,`Hope the broker is up`,`Aspire waits for RabbitMQ`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Scaling Bottlenecks with Message Queues (Webhooks series part 4)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O-FEWH4HK7w`,at:`8:29`}},{heading:`05 · What 'scalable' actually looks like on the dashboard`,body:[`Step back and look at the new topology. The publisher's path is unchanged — publish and return, caller is happy. The fan-out consumer does batched database reads and cheap enqueues; it'll fly. The delivery consumers are stateless and interchangeable, so when a backlog shows up, you add instances and burn it down. That's the whole scaling story: want more throughput? Add consumers.`,`And the observability story quietly improved too. Queue depth is now a number you can graph, alert on, and reason about — instead of an in-memory buffer you can't even see from outside the process. A slow subscriber used to stall the entire batch; now its latency is contained to its own message, its own retry, its own little lane. Blast radius went from 'everyone' to 'one subscriber'.`],quote:`Queue depth is the honest heartbeat of an async system.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Scaling Bottlenecks with Message Queues (Webhooks series part 4)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O-FEWH4HK7w`,at:`12:57`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology-before-after`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Webhook topology: in-memory channel vs. brokered fan-out`,caption:`Same publisher, same subscribers — but the unit of work changes from 'N HTTP calls per event' to 'one HTTP call per message'.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Before["Before — in-memory channel"]
        A1["POST /orders"] --> A2["Webhook Dispatcher"] --> A3["In-memory Channel"] --> A4["Webhook Processor (BackgroundService)"] --> A5["HTTP to every subscriber, one by one"]
    end
    subgraph After["After — RabbitMQ + MassTransit"]
        B1["POST /orders"] --> B2["IPublishEndpoint"] --> B3[("events queue")] --> B4["WebHookDispatched Consumer"] --> B5[("webhooks queue")] --> B6["Delivery Consumer"] --> B7["One HTTP call per message"]
    end`},{id:`fanout-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path: event to per-subscriber delivery`,caption:`The fan-out consumer acknowledges after enqueueing; the delivery consumer acknowledges only after the subscriber's HTTP call completes.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as Order API
    participant EP as IPublishEndpoint
    participant MQ as RabbitMQ
    participant FC as WebHookDispatched Consumer
    participant WQ as Webhooks Queue
    participant DC as Delivery Consumer
    participant SUB as Subscriber
    API->>EP: Publish(WebHookDispatched)
    EP->>MQ: enqueue event
    MQ->>FC: deliver event
    FC->>FC: fetch subscriptions (DbContext, batch)
    FC->>WQ: enqueue one message per subscriber
    FC-->>MQ: ack event
    MQ->>DC: deliver one message
    DC->>SUB: POST webhook (single HTTP call)
    SUB-->>DC: 200 OK
    DC-->>MQ: ack message`}],takeaways:[`Fan-out is O(subscribers): if one event can reach 10,000 subscribers, that's 10,000 HTTP calls — never let them happen inside a single handler.`,`Split queues by latency class: millisecond DB-read fan-out and multi-second external HTTP delivery get separate queues and separate consumers, so neither throttles the other.`,`One message = one HTTP delivery. Retries, acknowledgments, and per-subscriber backpressure become 1:1 instead of N:1.`,`In-memory channels buffer, brokers persist — anything you can't afford to lose on a restart belongs in RabbitMQ, not in RAM.`,`Watch queue depth and per-subscriber delivery p99; scale delivery consumers until backlog drain time meets your SLA, not until CPU usage looks busy.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Fix Your Scaling Bottlenecks with Message Queues (Webhooks series part 4)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O-FEWH4HK7w`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-dlXCodLxhag`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:16,title:`Saga Pattern For Microservice Architecture With Rebus And RabbitMQ`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:18`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`RabbitMQ`,`Rebus`,`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`412 confirmation emails, zero payments: the night 'eventually' broke`,paragraphs:[`It's 15:40 as connection pools saturated on launch night. The order pipeline is three fire-and-forget consumers: one sends the confirmation email, one kicks off the payment request, both subscribed to OrderCreated. A routine deploy bounces the payment worker mid-flight. RabbitMQ requeues what it still held, but the payment requests in the dead process's hands are simply gone — and nobody notices for six hours.`,`By morning, support is drowning: customers holding confirmation emails with no payment taken. Worse, the retry loops in each consumer fire independently, so a handful of orders get a second payment attempt with no shared memory to dedupe them. Every team points at every other team, and no single component can even answer the question 'where is this order right now?'`,`The fix wasn't a bigger retry loop — it was giving the order a brain. We introduced a saga: one stateful coordinator that owns the order's progress, correlates every incoming message by OrderId, and only fires the payment request after the email step reports back. Rebus on RabbitMQ gave us the messaging spine in an afternoon; the pager went quiet the week after.`],problem:`How do you keep order, email, and payment consistent when each lives in a process that can crash independently — without distributed transactions?`},tech:{stack:[`Rebus`,`RabbitMQ`,`Postgres`,`.NET`,`Docker`,`ISagaData`,`TypeBasedRouter`],note:`Rebus supplies the service bus, saga machinery, and correlation over a RabbitMQ transport; Postgres persists saga data so an order's progress survives restarts. Docker keeps broker and database reproducible on every machine.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just publish an event' isn't consistency`,body:[`Here's the design everyone starts with: the Web API publishes OrderCreated, and whoever cares subscribes. Email listens, payment listens. It feels clean — until you ask what happens when the payment consumer dies mid-handling. You've got two consumers, two independent retry policies, and zero shared memory about where the order actually stands. That's not consistency, that's hope.`,`The saga pattern is the answer to data consistency across microservices — a stateful coordinator that reacts to events and decides what happens next. And notice the deliberate choice here: we're building this decoupled even though it lives in one deployable, so payment processing can move to its own service later as a config change, not a rewrite. Why not two-phase commit instead? Because RabbitMQ isn't enlisted in the transaction, your latency doubles, and you've coupled the uptime of every service in the chain.`],quote:`Fire-and-forget is fine until you care about the outcome.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Two-phase commit (2PC)`,`Atomic across resources`,`Broker not enlisted, locks, latency`,`Rarely — avoid across services`],[`Fire-and-forget events`,`Simple, fast, few moving parts`,`Partial failure leaves inconsistent state`,`Non-critical side effects`],[`Saga (choreography)`,`Decoupled, crash-safe, replayable`,`More messages to reason about`,`Order-like multi-step flows`],[`Synchronous HTTP chain`,`Easy to step through`,`Couples uptime, cascading failures`,`Avoid between services`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Saga Pattern For Microservice Architecture With Rebus And RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dlXCodLxhag`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: routing and transport`,body:[`Before any saga magic, we wire the plumbing. Rebus needs two answers: where do messages go, and how do they travel? The routing config is a TypeBasedRouter that maps the entire Application assembly — every message type gets one destination, a single queue we call eShop. One call: MapAssembly off the Application assembly, destination eShop queue.`,`Then the transport: UseRabbitMQ, pull the 'message broker' connection string from configuration, and name the input queue. Rebus.ServiceProvider plugs the bus into the generic host's DI container so handlers resolve like any other service. Why a single queue? Because scale-out at this stage is just 'run more consumers on the same queue' — RabbitMQ round-robins for you. Split into per-handler queues only when load profiles or retry policies genuinely diverge. Boring topology is a feature.`],quote:`Route by type, scale by consumer — keep the topology boring until the numbers say otherwise.`,table:{headers:[`Topology`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single shared queue`,`One route, trivial scale-out`,`No per-handler isolation`,`Start here`],[`Queue per handler`,`Isolated retries and load`,`More routing rules to own`,`When profiles diverge`],[`Direct exchange bindings`,`Fine-grained control`,`You maintain the bindings`,`Special routing cases`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Saga Pattern For Microservice Architecture With Rebus And RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dlXCodLxhag`,at:`2:23`}},{heading:`03 · The saga data is the state machine`,body:[`Now the saga itself. You inherit from Saga — it's generic, and the type parameter is your saga data class implementing ISagaData. That interface gives you two properties for free: the Id of this saga instance, and Revision. Don't sleep on Revision — that's optimistic concurrency. If two messages for the same order land within milliseconds of each other, one loses, gets rejected, and comes back later. That's your race insurance.`,`Onto that base we add OrderId as a Guid, plus two boolean flags: WelcomeEmailSent and PaymentRequestSent. Why flags instead of a single stage enum? Because real flows interleave — if steps can happen out of order or overlap, booleans survive reordering and an enum lies. And keep the data small: this row is loaded and saved on every single message. Your saga data is a database row wearing a state machine costume.`],quote:`Two flags tell you where you are; Revision makes sure only one message moves you at a time.`,table:{headers:[`Saga state design`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Boolean step flags`,`Reorder-safe, trivial to update`,`No history of when steps ran`,`2–5 step sagas`],[`Single stage enum`,`Readable current stage`,`Breaks when steps interleave`,`Strictly linear flows`],[`Full audit trail`,`Debugging gold`,`More storage and queries`,`Regulated domains`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Saga Pattern For Microservice Architecture With Rebus And RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dlXCodLxhag`,at:`4:33`}},{heading:`04 · Correlation is the whole trick`,body:[`CorrelateMessages is where the saga gets its memory. For each message type — OrderCreated, OrderConfirmationEmailSent, OrderPaymentRequestSent — you map the OrderId on the message to the OrderId on the saga data. That's it. A message arrives, Rebus loads the saga row by that key, runs your handler, bumps Revision, saves. Notice we simplified the events down to just an OrderId Guid — we even deleted the old INotification implementation, because a message contract should carry identity, not behavior.`,`The discipline here is one event per step. The email side publishes OrderConfirmationEmailSent; payment publishes OrderPaymentRequestSent. The saga never polls, never guesses — it reacts. And if a message arrives that correlates to nothing? It goes to the error queue, not the void. Silent drops are how you end up with 3 AM pages.`],quote:`If a message can't find its saga, it must land in the error queue — never vanish.`,table:{headers:[`Correlation key`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Business Guid (OrderId)`,`Stable across services, debuggable`,`Must ride on every message`,`Almost always`],[`Transport MessageId`,`Zero extra fields`,`New per message — useless to correlate`,`Never`],[`Composite key`,`Extra safety on edge cases`,`More mapping code to maintain`,`Multi-instance flows`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Saga Pattern For Microservice Architecture With Rebus And RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dlXCodLxhag`,at:`5:38`}}],diagrams:[{id:`saga-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Saga topology: one queue, one coordinator, two steps`,caption:`Everything routes by type into the eShop queue; the saga persists its state in Postgres and advances one step per completion event.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    API["Web API<br/>publishes OrderCreated"]
    Q[["eShop queue<br/>RabbitMQ"]]
    S["OrderCreateSaga<br/>Rebus saga host"]
    DB[("Postgres<br/>saga data")]
    EH["Email step<br/>sends confirmation"]
    PH["Payment step<br/>sends payment request"]

    API -->|"OrderCreated"| Q
    Q --> S
    S -->|"load / save"| DB
    S -->|"SendConfirmationEmail"| EH
    EH -->|"OrderConfirmationEmailSent"| Q
    S -->|"SendPaymentRequest"| PH
    PH -->|"OrderPaymentRequestSent"| Q`},{id:`saga-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Order lifecycle as a saga sequence`,caption:`Each completion event re-enters the same queue, correlates on OrderId, and flips the next flag until the saga completes.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as Web API
    participant MQ as RabbitMQ eShop queue
    participant SG as OrderCreateSaga
    participant EM as Email Handler
    participant PM as Payment Handler

    API->>MQ: OrderCreated(orderId)
    MQ->>SG: deliver
    SG->>SG: create saga data, OrderId set
    SG->>EM: SendConfirmationEmail(orderId)
    EM-->>MQ: OrderConfirmationEmailSent(orderId)
    MQ->>SG: deliver
    SG->>SG: WelcomeEmailSent = true
    SG->>PM: SendPaymentRequest(orderId)
    PM-->>MQ: OrderPaymentRequestSent(orderId)
    MQ->>SG: deliver
    SG->>SG: PaymentRequestSent = true, saga complete`}],takeaways:[`One completion event per saga step: if a step can't publish something like OrderConfirmationEmailSent, it can't be coordinated — design the event before the handler.`,`Correlate on one business Guid (OrderId) carried by every message — never on transport message IDs, which change with every send.`,`Keep ISagaData minimal: Id and Revision come free; add OrderId plus one flag per step — every extra field is read and written on every message.`,`Start with one type-based route into a single eShop queue; split queues only when a handler's throughput or retry policy actually diverges.`,`Treat Revision as race insurance: two messages for the same order within milliseconds means one is rejected and retried — handlers must be safe under that.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Saga Pattern For Microservice Architecture With Rebus And RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dlXCodLxhag`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`saga-pattern`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-poFf8ZkG5Ag`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:17,title:`The Best Way to Implement Long Running Tasks in .NET - Hangfire, Blazor, SignalR`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:10`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`SignalR`,`Blazor`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The report button that lied: ten seconds in the demo, forty minutes in production`,paragraphs:[`It started as a demo-perfect feature. Click 'Generate Report', the button disables, a spinner spins, and ten seconds later the word 'Completed' appears. The endpoint behind it was a thing of beauty: log 'starting background job', wait ten seconds, log 'completed background job', return the string. In the walkthrough, it was flawless.`,`Then it met production, where reports took forty minutes and the corporate load balancer quietly killed idle connections around the four-minute mark. Users stared at a dead spinner, clicked again, clicked again — and every click stacked another HTTP connection on the pile, each one holding a socket hostage while the actual work died with the request that spawned it.`,`The fix wasn't a faster report. It was admitting that the request and the work are two different lifetimes. Hangfire took ownership of the work — persisted before it ever ran. The endpoint answered in milliseconds with a 202 and a job ID. And after a polling experiment that had two hundred clients pinging the database every single second, SignalR took over the last mile: the server pushes 'Succeeded' to the browser the moment it's true.`],problem:`A blocking HTTP request cannot outlive proxies, load balancers, or user patience — and polling every client every second buries the server and the database in unnecessary requests. We need durable background execution plus a delivery channel that doesn't charge us for silence.`},tech:{stack:[`Hangfire`,`SignalR`,`Blazor WebAssembly`,`.NET`,`BackgroundJobClient`,`PeriodicTimer`,`202 Accepted`],note:`Hangfire owns the durable execution — jobs are written to storage before they run and survive restarts. PeriodicTimer polling and SignalR push are the two competing delivery channels for telling the Blazor client the job is done.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: just let the HTTP call wait`,body:[`Version one is the base case, and I want it on screen because it's the version everyone writes first. One endpoint — reports/version one — that logs 'starting background job', does a Task.Delay for ten seconds, logs 'completed', and returns the string. On the Blazor side I disable the button with a boolean flag, await GetStringAsync against my configured HttpClient, flip the flag back, and show the response. Click, wait, done. Perfect demo.`,`But look at what we've actually built: we've bet that the request and the work share a lifetime. Ten seconds, fine. Real reports run minutes or hours, and somewhere between you and that job sits a proxy, a load balancer, a browser — all of which have opinions about how long an idle connection deserves. Azure's load balancer drops idle connections around four minutes. The browser gives up. And every bored user clicks again, stacking another held-open request on the heap.`,`So version one isn't wrong — it's just honest about its ceiling. It exists as the measuring stick. Everything after this point exists because this one can't survive real durations.`],quote:`A blocking HTTP call is a bet that nothing between you and the job gets bored. It always loses eventually.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Blocking HTTP`,`Simplest possible code, no extra infra`,`Connection held for full job; killed by timeouts; retries duplicate work`,`Jobs under ~10s`],[`Hangfire + polling`,`Durable jobs, request returns instantly, easy to ship`,`Constant request traffic; DB hammered with unchanged-state reads`,`Minutes-long jobs, few clients`],[`Hangfire + SignalR push`,`Zero wasted requests, millisecond-fresh updates`,`Connection state, reconnect logic, backplane at scale`,`Long jobs, many watchers`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Best Way to Implement Long Running Tasks in .NET - Hangfire, Blazor, SignalR`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=poFf8ZkG5Ag`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Hand the work to Hangfire, answer with a 202`,body:[`Version two changes the shape of the conversation. Instead of doing the work inside the request, I inject a BackgroundJobClient — Hangfire's abstraction for scheduling jobs programmatically — and call Enqueue with the generic overload. That overload resolves my LongRunningJob straight out of dependency injection and calls ExecuteAsync on it, so the job keeps its ILogger and every other dependency it already had.`,`Here's the detail I want you to catch: Enqueue returns a job ID, because the job is written to Hangfire's database before it is ever executed. That's the durability — restart the server mid-job and Hangfire still knows the work exists. The endpoint itself just returns an AcceptedResult, a 202 with a URI pointing at the job, and the HTTP request is over in milliseconds. The request's only job was to introduce the work to a system that will outlive it.`],quote:`202 Accepted is HTTP's way of saying: not done, and that's fine — here's your ticket.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Best Way to Implement Long Running Tasks in .NET - Hangfire, Blazor, SignalR`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=poFf8ZkG5Ag`,at:`1:00`}},{heading:`03 · Version two on the client: PeriodicTimer, and the price of asking`,body:[`Now the client holds a jobId and no way to know when it's done, so we poll. I store the jobId, clear the status list, PostAsync to reports/version two, read the jobId back from the response — then spin up a PeriodicTimer on a one-second interval and loop: await the next tick, query the job state, compare it against the last state I stored, and break the loop the moment we see Succeeded.`,`It works, and honestly it's the right thing to ship when you need something tomorrow. But watch the math. Every client fires a request every single second for the entire job duration, whether anything changed or not. Two hundred users watching one report is two hundred requests a second, each one bouncing off the server and down into Hangfire storage to read a state that, most seconds, didn't change. That's the downside I keep coming back to: a pile of unnecessary requests inflating load on the server and the database, paid for by people who are just waiting.`],quote:`Polling is a tax every idle client pays every second — even the seconds where nothing happened.`,table:{headers:[`Poll interval`,`Requests per client per 10-min job`,`Waste`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`1 second`,`600`,`~99% of reads see no change`,`Demo-fine, production-hostile`],[`5 seconds`,`120`,`High but tolerable`,`Reasonable default for short jobs`],[`30 seconds`,`20`,`Low waste`,`Sluggish feedback for fast jobs`],[`SignalR push`,`0 wasted`,`None — server initiates`,`Best once jobs run long`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Best Way to Implement Long Running Tasks in .NET - Hangfire, Blazor, SignalR`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=poFf8ZkG5Ag`,at:`5:25`}},{heading:`04 · Version three: stop asking, let the server tell you`,body:[`Which brings me to the last implementation: SignalR. The job still lives in Hangfire — same Enqueue, same 202, same durability — but the client opens a SignalR connection, and the job, through an injected IHubContext, pushes state changes the moment they happen. No timer, no loop, no requests for unchanged states. The browser learns 'Succeeded' within milliseconds of it becoming true.`,`Is push free? No — and I want to be straight about the trade. You've swapped request load for connection state: the server now holds a live socket per client, you own reconnect handling, and the moment you run more than one instance you need a backplane like Redis so a job on server A can reach a client connected to server B. And I'd still keep Hangfire as the source of truth — on reconnect, the client asks for the current state exactly once, then rides the push from there. Transport is delivery; durability is architecture.`],quote:`Push trades request load for connection state — pick the one your infrastructure hates less.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Polling (PeriodicTimer)`,`SignalR push`],rows:[[`Wasted requests`,`One per client per tick, mostly no-ops`,`Zero — messages only on change`],[`Update latency`,`Up to one full interval stale`,`Near-instant`],[`Server cost`,`Stateless request handling`,`One live connection per client`],[`Scale-out`,`Trivial — any instance answers`,`Needs Redis backplane across instances`],[`Failure mode`,`Silently stale if client stops polling`,`Needs reconnect + one catch-up poll`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Best Way to Implement Long Running Tasks in .NET - Hangfire, Blazor, SignalR`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=poFf8ZkG5Ag`,at:`8:41`}},{heading:`05 · Picking the shape: match the transport to the job`,body:[`So which of the three do you ship? I use duration as the tiebreaker. Under roughly ten seconds with the user staring at the screen: blocking is genuinely fine, keep it simple. Tens of seconds to minutes: 202 plus polling, ideally with a backoff — start at one second, stretch the interval as the job ages. Minutes to hours, or many concurrent watchers: Hangfire plus SignalR push, with a single poll-on-reconnect as the safety net.`,`And whatever you pick, the invariant never moves: job state lives in durable storage, the HTTP request dies fast, and the transport is just the messenger. Get that separation right and swapping polling for push later is a client-side change — not a redesign.`],quote:`The transport is a delivery detail; durable job state is the architecture.`,table:{headers:[`Job duration`,`Pattern`,`Why`],rows:[[`Under ~10s`,`Blocking HTTP call`,`User is watching anyway; zero extra infra`],[`~10s to minutes`,`202 + Hangfire + polling`,`Instant handshake, simple client loop`],[`Minutes to hours`,`202 + Hangfire + SignalR push`,`No wasted polls; survives restarts and refreshes`],[`Any duration, many clients`,`Push + poll-on-reconnect`,`Push for freshness, one poll for correctness`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Best Way to Implement Long Running Tasks in .NET - Hangfire, Blazor, SignalR`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=poFf8ZkG5Ag`,at:`13:37`}}],diagrams:[{id:`three-versions-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Three implementations, one job: blocking, polling, push`,caption:`How the same long-running report travels through each version — the work gets more durable and the delivery gets cheaper as we move right.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph V1["Version 1 - Blocking HTTP"]
        B1["Blazor WASM"] -->|"GET, connection held for full job"| A1["API /reports/v1"]
    end
    subgraph V2["Version 2 - Hangfire + Polling"]
        B2["Blazor WASM"] -->|"POST, gets 202 + jobId"| A2["API /reports/v2"]
        A2 -->|"Enqueue"| H["Hangfire Server"]
        H -->|"writes state"| DB[("Hangfire Storage")]
        B2 -->|"PeriodicTimer - GET every 1s"| A2
        A2 -->|"reads state"| DB
    end
    subgraph V3["Version 3 - Hangfire + SignalR"]
        B3["Blazor WASM"] -->|"POST, gets 202"| A3["API /reports/v3"]
        A3 -->|"Enqueue"| H3["Hangfire Server"]
        H3 -->|"state changes via IHubContext"| S["SignalR Hub"]
        S -->|"push updates"| B3
    end`},{id:`signalr-job-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The 202 + push handshake, end to end`,caption:`The HTTP request dies in milliseconds; Hangfire owns the work; SignalR delivers the finish line the moment it's real.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant B as Blazor WASM
    participant A as Minimal API
    participant H as Hangfire Job
    participant S as SignalR Hub
    B->>A: POST /reports/v3
    A->>H: BackgroundJobClient.Enqueue
    Note over H: Job persisted to storage before execution
    H-->>A: jobId
    A-->>B: 202 Accepted + jobId
    B->>S: connect, register for jobId
    H->>H: ExecuteAsync - real report work runs here
    H->>S: IHubContext push - progress
    S-->>B: "Running"
    H->>S: IHubContext push - final state
    S-->>B: "Succeeded"
    B->>A: fetch result once`}],takeaways:[`Keep blocking HTTP calls under ~10 seconds — beyond ~100–240s, load balancer idle timeouts kill the connection before your job finishes.`,`Return 202 Accepted with a status URI in milliseconds: the request should introduce the work, never perform it.`,`Polling at 1s costs 600 requests per client per 10-minute job, ~99% of them wasted — back off to 5–30s or switch to push before you scale past a handful of users.`,`Hangfire writes the job to storage before execution, so a server restart mid-job costs you a retry, never a lost report.`,`SignalR drops wasted polls to zero but adds one live connection per client — add a Redis backplane the moment you run a second instance, and always poll once on reconnect.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Best Way to Implement Long Running Tasks in .NET - Hangfire, Blazor, SignalR`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=poFf8ZkG5Ag`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-iGXFtUpHTd8`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:18,title:`Microservices Communication Patterns Explained in .NET`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 17:26`,minutes:17,difficulty:3,tags:[`Kafka`,`Azure`,`gRPC`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`3 AM: One Slow HTTP Call Took Down Two Services`,paragraphs:[`It's 3 AM and the pager lights up: POST /notes is timing out across the board. I pull the traces and the story is embarrassingly simple. Every note creation makes a synchronous HTTP call to the Tags API to generate tags before it can respond — and the Tags API is wedged after a bad deploy. One slow dependency, and now the flagship endpoint is dead.`,`We restart things, shed load, and I sit there staring at the trace waterfall: 30 seconds of a request thread parked, waiting on a service it doesn't even own. The Notes API didn't have a bug. It had a coupling problem — its availability was mathematically bound to the Tags API's availability.`,`The fix wasn't more retries or a bigger timeout. It was flipping the direction of control: Notes publishes a NoteCreated event to a queue and returns immediately; the Tags API consumes it whenever it's healthy. That one change dragged in a whole new set of problems — duplicates, contract versioning, tags that show up a beat late — and that's exactly what this session is about.`],problem:`A synchronous REST/gRPC call means the caller's request blocks until the callee answers — so one degraded service cascades into everything upstream of it. We need queue-based async communication, but that trades blocking for delivery-guarantee headaches (at-least-once means idempotent consumers), message contract versioning, and eventual consistency.`},tech:{stack:[`Azure Service Bus`,`.NET Aspire`,`gRPC`,`REST`],note:`REST/gRPC is the blocking path we start with; Azure Service Bus becomes the queue that decouples Notes from Tags, with .NET Aspire wiring the Service Bus emulator into the app host.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the sync call felt safe — and why it lies`,body:[`Let's start where everyone starts: synchronous communication. Two services, each with its own database, and Notes calls Tags over HTTP — REST or gRPC, pick your flavor. It's the pattern you reach for first because it behaves like a function call: send a request, get a response, errors are right there in front of you.`,`But here's the trap. The nature of sync communication is that it's blocking — the current API request parks until Tags answers. And no, async/await in C# does not save you; that's about freeing up threads, not about the request completing. The user is still waiting. Which means Notes' effective uptime is now the product of Notes' uptime and Tags' uptime. Chain enough of those calls and your 99.9% services deliver 95% together.`,`So my first filter is simple: does the caller actually need the answer to finish its job? If tagging is just a side effect of creating a note, blocking on it isn't a design — it's a bug waiting for 3 AM.`],quote:`A synchronous call is a promise that someone else's uptime becomes your uptime.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Sync (REST/gRPC)`,`Simple, immediate answer, easy error handling`,`Blocking, availability coupling, cascading failures`,`Caller truly needs the result to proceed`],[`Async (queue)`,`Loose coupling, survivability, natural retries`,`Contracts, idempotency, eventual consistency`,`Side effects the caller doesn't wait for`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Communication Patterns Explained in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iGXFtUpHTd8`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the message contract is the real API`,body:[`Flip it around. With async communication, Notes doesn't call Tags — it announces. 'A note was created.' It doesn't know or care who's listening. That's loose coupling in the real sense: the only thing two services share is the message contract.`,`But that contract is now load-bearing infrastructure. Someone has to own it, serialize it, and version it over time, because the publisher and the consumer deploy on completely different schedules. Rename a field carelessly and you've shipped a breaking change to a service that might not even be running yet.`,`The payoff is temporal decoupling: Tags can be down, redeploying, or slow, and Notes keeps accepting notes. The queue is the buffer that absorbs the difference in pace between two services that no longer trust each other's uptime.`],quote:`In async systems, the message contract is your API — version it like one.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Communication Patterns Explained in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iGXFtUpHTd8`,at:`0:27`}},{heading:`03 · The at-least-once trap and the idempotency fix`,body:[`Now the part nobody puts in the happy-path demo: delivery guarantees. Brokers offer at-most-once, at-least-once, and exactly-once. And here's the practical truth — most message brokers, Service Bus included, give you at-least-once by default.`,`At-least-once means duplicates are not an edge case; they're a Tuesday. The Tags API crashes after persisting tags but before completing the message? Broker redelivers. Network blips mid-ack? Redelivers. So the consumer must be idempotent: key on the message ID, check 'have I already done this?', and make the duplicate a no-op.`,`Exactly-once sounds like the escape hatch, but it's usually emulated with transactions and it costs you throughput and flexibility. I'd rather spend that complexity budget on an idempotent consumer — which, as a bonus, also protects me from the retry policies I will inevitably add at 4 AM.`],quote:`Design for at-least-once and you'll survive everything the broker throws at you.`,table:{headers:[`Guarantee`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`At-most-once`,`No duplicates, lowest latency`,`Messages can silently vanish`,`Telemetry, cache warmers`],[`At-least-once`,`No message loss, broker default`,`Duplicates are guaranteed`,`Most business events + idempotent consumer`],[`Exactly-once`,`The dream`,`Costly, usually emulated, throughput limits`,`Rarely worth it; fake it with idempotency`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Communication Patterns Explained in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iGXFtUpHTd8`,at:`2:26`}},{heading:`04 · Eventual consistency: learning to wait a beat`,body:[`The trade people actually feel on day one is eventual consistency. There's now a delay between publishing NoteCreated and the tags existing in the read path. Create a note, immediately load it — no tags yet. That's not a bug. That's physics.`,`So decide up front: how stale is acceptable? For tags, seconds is fine. For a payment ledger, maybe not. And the UI has to be honest about it — optimistic placeholders, or a read path that tolerates absence. Async also buys you extra latency and real operational complexity: another broker to monitor, poison messages, dead-letter queues. That's the bill for the coupling you removed.`,`None of this is a reason to avoid queues. It's a reason to pick the pattern per interaction instead of by ideology. Blocking where the user waits, queueing where nobody does.`],quote:`Eventual consistency isn't a bug you fix — it's a contract you design the UI around.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Communication Patterns Explained in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iGXFtUpHTd8`,at:`6:13`}},{heading:`05 · Wiring it in .NET: Aspire plus Service Bus`,body:[`Let's wire it up. The first thing we need is a messaging service — I'm using Azure Service Bus, and .NET Aspire already ships an integration for it. Install Aspire.Hosting.Azure.ServiceBus, and in the app host it's just builder.AddAzureServiceBus("servicebus").`,`I want it running locally via the emulator, configured with a persistent container lifetime — which spins up two additional containers under the hood. Then Notes publishes NoteCreated to a queue, and the Tags API consumes it and runs the same tagging logic it always did. Same behavior, zero blocking, and the 3 AM cascade is structurally impossible.`,`And when a second service needs the same event? That's the moment we graduate from a queue to a topic with subscriptions — one publisher, many consumers, fan-out as broker configuration instead of new publisher code.`],quote:`Start with a queue; reach for a topic the day a second service cares.`,table:{headers:[`Pattern`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Queue`,`One consumer group, dead simple`,`No fan-out`,`Point-to-point events`],[`Topic + subscriptions`,`Fan-out to N subscribers`,`More broker config to manage`,`Broadcasting domain events`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Communication Patterns Explained in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iGXFtUpHTd8`,at:`8:21`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology-sync-vs-async`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Same two services, two communication styles`,caption:`The blocking HTTP/gRPC hop on top versus the queue-mediated flow below — same logic, radically different failure behavior.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph SYNC["Synchronous — blocking call"]
    direction LR
    N1["Notes API"] -->|"HTTP / gRPC"| T1["Tags API"]
    N1 --- DB1[("Notes DB")]
    T1 --- DB2[("Tags DB")]
  end
  subgraph ASYNC["Asynchronous — queue-mediated"]
    direction LR
    N2["Notes API"] --- DB3[("Notes DB")]
    N2 -->|"publish NoteCreated"| Q[["Service Bus queue"]]
    Q -->|"consume"| T2["Tags API"]
    T2 --- DB4[("Tags DB")]
  end
  SYNC -.->|"the fix"| ASYNC`},{id:`sequence-note-created`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`NoteCreated: publish, return, consume, redeliver`,caption:`The client gets 201 immediately; the Tags API processes the event later — and a redelivery becomes a harmless no-op thanks to the idempotency check.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant N as Notes API
  participant Q as Service Bus Queue
  participant T as Tags API
  participant DB as Tags DB
  C->>N: POST /notes
  N->>N: persist note in Notes DB
  N->>Q: publish NoteCreated
  Q-->>N: accepted
  N-->>C: 201 Created — tags pending
  Q->>T: deliver NoteCreated
  T->>T: idempotency check on message ID
  T->>DB: upsert tags
  T->>Q: complete message
  Note over Q,T: crash before complete = redelivery — duplicate becomes a no-op`}],takeaways:[`Default to async for side effects; keep sync calls only where the caller needs the answer — cap any request path at roughly 2 synchronous hops.`,`Assume at-least-once delivery: make every consumer idempotent by keying on the message ID, and treat duplicates as guaranteed, not rare.`,`Version message contracts like public APIs — additive changes only, and never force publisher and consumer to deploy together.`,`Define a staleness budget up front (e.g., tags visible within seconds, not milliseconds) and design the read path and UI around eventual consistency.`,`Start with one queue per consumer pair; graduate to a topic with subscriptions the moment a second service needs the same event — fan-out should be broker config, not new publisher code.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Microservices Communication Patterns Explained in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iGXFtUpHTd8`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-SzLhUq1Hezk`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:19,title:`Production-Ready Distributed Locking in .NET Using a Single Library`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 16:30`,minutes:17,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Redis`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The Night Our lock Keyword Lied: Six Replicas, One Job, Six Payout Runs`,paragraphs:[`02:00 UTC, and our nightly reconciliation job fires. Except that week an autoscale event had left six replicas behind the load balancer, and every single one of them wrapped the job in the same innocent line of C#: lock (_gate). Each replica acquired its own gate, felt very safe, and ran the job. Six payout files. Six calls to the payments provider. Finance noticed the double payouts around 3 AM, which is also roughly when my pager noticed me.`,`I sat there scrolling logs showing six 'acquired lock' lines — all true, all meaningless. The lock keyword had never seen another server, and it never will. The job wasn't thread-unsafe; it was fleet-unsafe. Every instance was guarding a door that only existed inside its own process.`,`The fix took an afternoon: a session-scoped Postgres advisory lock keyed on a hashed job name, so the lock dies with the connection instead of haunting us. Then we wrapped it in a library behind one provider interface, so the next time we swap Postgres for Redis it's a config change, not a rewrite.`],problem:`You have a set of background jobs where only one instance may execute at a given point in time — but the fleet is scaled out, and an in-process lock can only synchronize threads within its own process's memory.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`Redis`,`DistributedLock library`,`Advisory Locks`,`SHA-256 Key Hashing`,`IDistributedLockProvider`],note:`Postgres advisory locks do the coordinating tonight, Redis waits in the wings for a later episode, and the DistributedLock library wraps every backend behind one IDistributedLockProvider registered as a singleton.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: your lock keyword has never seen another server`,body:[`So here's the first thing I want you to internalize before we write a line of code: a C# lock or a SemaphoreSlim lives in the memory of one process. The moment you scale out to N instances, you don't have one lock — you have N completely independent locks that don't even know the others exist. That's not a bug in .NET; that's just what an application-level lock is. It was never designed to be a distributed synchronization primitive.`,`Where does this actually bite you? The classic case is exactly what the story was about: a set of background jobs where only one should execute at a given point in time. Leader election, cache rebuilds, cron jobs in a fleet — same shape. And before you ask 'why not just run one instance?' — because you scale for traffic, not for jobs. The fleet exists whether your scheduler likes it or not.`,`So the conclusion is unavoidable: the synchronization primitive has to move out of process memory into something every instance already shares. The only question is what that shared thing is — and that's the next thought.`],quote:`Your lock keyword has never seen another server — and it never will.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`lock / SemaphoreSlim`,`Zero setup, fastest possible`,`Scoped to one process's memory`,`Single instance only`],[`Distributed lock`,`Coordinates the whole fleet`,`Needs shared store + network hops`,`Scaled-out services`],[`DB unique constraint`,`Strong correctness guarantee`,`Guards inserts, not job execution`,`Idempotent writes`],[`No lock + idempotency`,`Eventually safe, no infra`,`Wasted duplicate work every run`,`Cheap, side-effect-free jobs`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Production-Ready Distributed Locking in .NET Using a Single Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SzLhUq1Hezk`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: who does every instance already trust?`,body:[`A distributed lock needs a component that every instance can reach and agree on. So I always start with the question: what does my fleet already talk to? Nine times out of ten, it's the database. If you've got Redis, that's a candidate. If you're running ZooKeeper, bless you. The library we'll use supports Azure blobs, files, MySQL, Oracle, Postgres, Redis, SQL Server, and ZooKeeper — which tells you something: a lock is just a tiny bit of agreed-upon state, and almost any durable store can hold it.`,`Now the trade-offs. A database lock is coarse and tied to a connection's lifetime, but it's already in your trust boundary and it's transactional-adjacent. Redis is blazing fast with TTL-based leases, but failover can momentarily lose a lock, and then you're having the fencing-token conversation. Consensus stores like ZooKeeper are the strongest, but you don't spin up a ZooKeeper ensemble for one nightly job.`,`My default rule: if the job already lives in the database's world — reads its data, writes its results — use the database for the lock too. One fewer moving part, one fewer failure domain. Save Redis for when the lock frequency actually demands it.`],quote:`A distributed lock is only as trustworthy as the thing every instance already agrees to talk to.`,table:{headers:[`Backend`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Postgres advisory locks`,`Already in your stack, auto-release on disconnect`,`Tied to connection lifetime`,`Jobs living in the same DB`],[`Redis`,`Sub-millisecond, TTL leases`,`Failover can drop a lock, needs fencing`,`High-frequency short locks`],[`SQL Server app locks`,`Native, session or transaction scoped`,`SQL Server only`,`Legacy Microsoft estates`],[`ZooKeeper / Azure blobs`,`Strong consensus / dead cheap`,`Extra infra / coarse granularity`,`Elections, low-frequency guards`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Production-Ready Distributed Locking in .NET Using a Single Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SzLhUq1Hezk`,at:`4:49`}},{heading:`03 · Rolling it by hand: advisory locks and the connection trap`,body:[`Let's build it from scratch first, because you should know what the library is doing for you. Postgres gives us advisory locks, and the session-scoped flavor is the interesting one: you acquire the lock on a connection, and you hold it for exactly as long as that connection lives. You can release explicitly at any point, or you can just let the connection expire and Postgres releases the lock automatically. Right out of the gate, that collapses your deadlock window — a dead client can't hold a lock it has no connection to hold it with.`,`Mechanically: await using var connection, opened via dataSource.CreateConnection inside an async delegate, so disposal is guaranteed. Advisory locks key on a bigint, but humans think in strings like 'invoice-reconciliation-job' — so we define a helper that hashes the key with SHA-256 and pulls out a long via BitConverter.ToInt64. Deterministic, and two instances computing the same hash always land on the same lock.`,`Two traps before you ship this by hand. First: use a dedicated connection, not the pooled one your request is already borrowing — a session lock on a shared pooled connection is a lock on everyone's session. Second: session-level advisory locks don't participate in transactions, and if the connection drops mid-job, the lock vanishes with it. That's a feature for deadlocks and a hazard for long jobs — keep the critical section shorter than your connection's patience.`],quote:`Tie the lock to the connection, and the deadlock dies with the disconnect.`,table:{headers:[`Decision`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Session-scoped advisory lock`,`Auto-releases on disconnect, kills deadlocks`,`Vanishes if connection drops mid-job`,`Great for short critical sections`],[`Transaction-scoped advisory lock`,`Releases on commit or rollback`,`Long transactions hold the lock hostage`,`Purely DB-bound work`],[`Raw bigint key`,`Native advisory lock format`,`Unreadable in logs and dashboards`,`Never hand-write these`],[`SHA-256 → BitConverter.ToInt64`,`Deterministic, string-friendly`,`64-bit space, ~2^-64 collision per pair`,`The standard trick`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Production-Ready Distributed Locking in .NET Using a Single Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SzLhUq1Hezk`,at:`9:36`}},{heading:`04 · The library route: one interface, eight backends`,body:[`Now the version I'd actually take to production. We register a singleton IDistributedLockProvider in DI — you could absolutely new up the lock yourself, and that isn't wrong, but the provider keeps the backend swappable. Pick your provider: Azure blob, file, MySQL, Oracle, Postgres, Redis, SQL Server, ZooKeeper. We choose the Postgres provider, and pleasingly, it expects nothing but a connection string pulled from configuration.`,`Then we re-implement the demo endpoint: app.MapPost to a locking-demo/postgres route, taking the IDistributedLockProvider as an injected parameter. The shape of the handler is the whole philosophy: try to acquire the lock with a timeout — never block forever — wrap the work in an await using so the handle's disposal releases the lock, and let the happy path read like straight-line code.`,`Why not always roll it from scratch? Because the from-scratch version you write on a calm Tuesday handles the happy path, and the version you need at 3 AM handles timeouts, async-safe handles, connection drops, and a backend swap you didn't plan for. That's exactly the code a maintained library has already gotten wrong once, so you don't have to.`],quote:`One interface, eight backends — buy the boring part, spend the saved hours on your actual domain.`,table:{headers:[`Path`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`From scratch`,`Full control, you learn the semantics`,`You own every edge case forever`,`Learning, single backend`],[`DistributedLock library`,`8 backends, one interface, timeout-safe handles`,`One extra dependency`,`Production default`],[`Raw SQL in handlers`,`No DI ceremony`,`Lock logic smeared across the codebase`,`Quick scripts only`],[`Provider via DI`,`Swap backends per environment via config`,`A little indirection`,`Multi-environment apps`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Production-Ready Distributed Locking in .NET Using a Single Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SzLhUq1Hezk`,at:`9:50`}},{heading:`05 · Why Redis waits in the wings`,body:[`Notice we installed the Redis package right away but deliberately saved it for later. That's not laziness — it's sequencing. Postgres goes first because it's already the source of truth for this workload, and its session semantics are forgiving: the lock's lifetime is the connection's lifetime, full stop. No TTL math, no clock drift, no failover what-ifs.`,`Redis is the speed play — sub-millisecond SET-with-expiry, perfect for high-frequency, short-lived locks like per-request throttles. But the moment you lean on Redis for correctness, you inherit the hard questions: what happens when the primary fails over and the replica hadn't replicated your lock yet? Now you're reading about fencing tokens and having opinions about RedLock. Legitimate conversation — just not the one you want to have for a nightly batch job.`,`So the closing heuristic: match the lock store to the blast radius. Money-touching, DB-adjacent work goes to the database where the data already lives. Hot, high-frequency, best-effort coordination goes to Redis. And whichever you pick, treat the lock as a lease, not a law — idempotency is your seatbelt either way.`],quote:`A lock is a lease, not a law — design like it expires mid-flight.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`Postgres lock`,`Redis lock`,`Pick`],rows:[[`Nightly batch job`,`Perfect fit, data is already there`,`Overkill plus failover risk`,`Postgres`],[`Per-request throttle`,`Connection churn per request`,`Sub-ms SET NX with TTL`,`Redis`],[`Money-touching job`,`Sits next to the transaction`,`Needs fencing tokens to be safe`,`Postgres + idempotency`],[`Leader election`,`Workable, connection-bound`,`TTL plus heartbeats`,`Redis or ZooKeeper`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`lock-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Fleet Contention Through a Shared Lock Store`,caption:`Three replicas race for the same job lock; the shared store grants it to exactly one session while the others skip or retry with a timeout.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph Fleet["Scaled-out fleet - 3 replicas"]
        I1["Instance 1"]
        I2["Instance 2"]
        I3["Instance 3"]
    end
    PG[("Postgres<br/>advisory lock on hashed key")]
    RD[("Redis<br/>TTL lease - later episode")]
    I1 -- "try-acquire invoice-job" --> PG
    I2 -- "try-acquire invoice-job" --> PG
    I3 -- "try-acquire invoice-job" --> PG
    PG -- "single session holds lock" --> I1
    I2 -. "rejected: skip or retry" .-> PG
    I3 -. "rejected: skip or retry" .-> PG
    RD -. "drop-in backend via same provider" .-> PG`},{id:`advisory-lock-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Session-Scoped Advisory Lock Lifecycle`,caption:`The winner holds the lock only as long as its connection; disposal auto-releases it, so the next contender's retry succeeds without any manual cleanup.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as Instance A
    participant B as Instance B
    participant P as Postgres
    A->>P: open dedicated connection
    A->>P: pg_try_advisory_lock(bigint key)
    P-->>A: true - session holds lock
    B->>P: pg_try_advisory_lock(same key)
    P-->>B: false - held elsewhere
    B->>B: skip or retry with timeout
    A->>A: run exclusive job
    A->>P: dispose connection
    Note over P: session gone - lock auto-released
    B->>P: retry acquire
    P-->>B: true - B proceeds`}],takeaways:[`A C# lock only counts threads inside its own process — with 6 replicas you have 6 independent locks, not 1; move the primitive into shared infrastructure the moment you scale out.`,`Session-scoped Postgres advisory locks release the instant the connection dies, so your deadlock window equals your connection lifetime — keep the critical section shorter than that.`,`Advisory locks key on a bigint: hash your string key with SHA-256 and BitConverter.ToInt64 — deterministic across instances, with roughly a 1-in-2^64 collision chance per key pair.`,`Register one IDistributedLockProvider as a singleton and you can swap among 8 backends (Postgres, Redis, SQL Server, MySQL, Oracle, Azure blobs, files, ZooKeeper) without touching a single call site.`,`Always try-acquire with a timeout instead of blocking forever, and treat every lock as a lease: add idempotency keys so a rare double-fire costs you a skipped run, not a double payout.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Production-Ready Distributed Locking in .NET Using a Single Library`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SzLhUq1Hezk`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-CTKWFMZVIWA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:20,title:`What Is A Message Queue + RabbitMQ and MassTransit Integration`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 15:15`,minutes:15,difficulty:3,tags:[`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`Docker`],story:{headline:`The catalog refresh that stopped checkout breathing at midnight`,paragraphs:[`It was 11:58 PM on the night of a big catalog refresh. The merch team was bulk-creating products, and every CreateProduct request was doing the whole dance inline: write to Postgres, HTTP POST to the search indexer, then a call to the recommendations service. At 12:04 the indexer's deploy finished and it started answering in eight-second bursts. Every product creation hung with it.`,`By 1 AM support couldn't add a single product. Worse, the retries had made a mess — the database write succeeded, the indexer call timed out, the request failed, someone hit submit again, and now we had duplicate SKUs. The failure of a nice-to-have side effect was taking down the must-have write path.`,`The fix wasn't a faster indexer. It was admitting that 'a product was created' is a fact, not a task list. We stood up RabbitMQ in a Docker container, published ProductCreated through MassTransit, and returned 201 while the message was still in flight. The indexer being down became the indexer's problem, on the indexer's timeline.`],problem:`The command handler must publish ProductCreated without knowing who's listening — inject an IEventBus abstraction backed by MassTransit, point the bus at a Dockerized RabbitMQ via a settings class, and let an IConsumer<T> class pick the message up asynchronously.`},tech:{stack:[`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`Docker Compose`,`ASP.NET Core`,`IConsumer<T>`,`Kebab Case Formatter`],note:`The broker in a container, the bus library that hides all AMQP plumbing, and the consumer interface that turns one small class into a queue subscriber.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just call the other service?`,body:[`Here's the question I always get first: the indexer is one HTTP call away, why add a broker? Because a synchronous call couples two services in time. Your create-product request now lives or dies on the health of a service it doesn't own. Slow downstream, slow you. Down downstream, down you.`,`A queue decouples in time instead of in space. You publish ProductCreated — a fact — and move on. A consumer subscribes, handles it whenever it can, and can even keep a local copy of the product so it never has to call you back for reads. And once every step publishes 'I'm done,' you've got choreography-based sagas for free: each component publishes when it completes its work, which triggers the next step — no orchestrator service to become a single point of failure.`,`The honest trade-off: you give up immediate consistency and take on 'eventually.' You can't return the indexer's response to the caller anymore. If you truly need the answer inside the same request, a queue is the wrong tool. If you just need the work to happen reliably, it's the right one.`],quote:`A queue doesn't make the work disappear — it makes the failure someone else's problem, on someone else's timeline.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inline HTTP calls`,`Immediate result, easy to debug`,`Temporal coupling, cascading failures`,`Only if the caller needs the answer now`],[`Message queue`,`Decoupled, retryable, fans out to many consumers`,`Eventual consistency, more moving parts`,`Side effects, sagas, local copies`],[`In-process events`,`Zero infrastructure, fast`,`Lost on crash, same blast radius`,`Single-service apps only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — What Is A Message Queue + RabbitMQ and MassTransit Integration`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CTKWFMZVIWA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Standing up the broker: docker-compose before code`,body:[`Before we touch C#, the broker needs to exist. In docker-compose we pull the rabbitmq image, give the container a hostname — and that hostname matters, because it's exactly what the app's connection configuration will point at later — and map the ports: 5672 for AMQP, 15672 if you want the management UI to watch messages flow.`,`The piece people skip: a volume. Mount one so the broker's data survives container restarts. Without it, every docker-compose down quietly deletes every message sitting in a queue — and then you'll spend a week not trusting your own dev environment. Default username and password are fine for local dev, but the application should read host, username, and password from appsettings, never hardcode them.`,`Why compose and not a cloud broker for this? Because the point is the pattern. The app only knows a host, a username, and a password — swap localhost for a managed cluster and nothing in the code changes.`],quote:`If your dev broker forgets every message on restart, you'll never trust a green test run again.`,table:{headers:[`Compose knob`,`What it buys you`,`Skip it and...`],rows:[[`hostname`,`Stable name the app's connection config points at`,`Connection strings drift per environment`],[`volume mount`,`Queues and messages survive restarts`,`docker-compose down silently deletes in-flight messages`],[`ports 5672 / 15672`,`AMQP for the app, management UI for you`,`You debug the broker blind`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — What Is A Message Queue + RabbitMQ and MassTransit Integration`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CTKWFMZVIWA`,at:`2:30`}},{heading:`03 · Wiring MassTransit: one lambda, three decisions`,body:[`Now Program.cs. AddMassTransit takes one lambda, and inside it you make exactly three decisions. First: which transport — UsingRabbitMq. Second: how to connect — and here's the part I like — the lambda hands you a bus registration context, so you resolve MessageBrokerSettings straight out of DI: host, username, password, all bound from appsettings. No connection string literals in code.`,`Third: hand those settings to configurator.Host, set the credentials on the host configurator, and you're connected. That's it. MassTransit owns the connection lifecycle, the channels, the reconnects — you never touch a raw AMQP client.`,`Why resolve the settings from the context instead of grabbing IConfiguration directly? Same reason you do it everywhere else: one source of truth, and the bus configuration becomes testable — point the settings at a different host in tests and the exact same wiring code follows.`],quote:`The bus configurator is just dependency injection with opinions.`,table:{headers:[`Connection config`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Hardcoded URI in Program.cs`,`Fastest to write`,`Untestable, leaks per-env values`,`Throwaway demos only`],[`Settings class resolved from DI`,`Bound from appsettings, testable, one source of truth`,`One extra class`,`Default choice`],[`Full options pattern + secret store`,`Environment-specific secrets, rotation-friendly`,`More ceremony`,`Production-grade deployments`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — What Is A Message Queue + RabbitMQ and MassTransit Integration`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CTKWFMZVIWA`,at:`4:29`}},{heading:`04 · Naming queues like an adult`,body:[`One line I refuse to skip: SetKebabCaseEndpointNameFormatter. With it, a consumer named ProductCreatedEventConsumer gets a queue called product-created. Without it, you get PascalCase defaults that look wrong in the management UI and quietly differ between teams.`,`Here's why I'm strict about it: queue names are contracts you write by accident. Rename a consumer class six months in and MassTransit derives a new queue name — now you have two queues, the old one orphaned with unread messages, and nobody remembers the binding. Pick the formatter before your first consumer ships, not after.`,`Kebab-case also reads cleanly in the RabbitMQ management UI, which is exactly where you'll spend your worst debugging sessions. Small decision, outsized payoff.`],quote:`Queue names are API contracts you write by accident — choose the formatter before the first consumer, not after the first incident.`,table:{headers:[`Formatter`,`Queue for ProductCreatedEventConsumer`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Kebab case`,`product-created`,`Readable, stable — pick this`],[`Snake case`,`product_created_event`,`Fine, less idiomatic in RabbitMQ land`],[`Default PascalCase`,`ProductCreatedEvent`,`Drifts with class renames, ugly in the UI`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — What Is A Message Queue + RabbitMQ and MassTransit Integration`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CTKWFMZVIWA`,at:`11:09`}},{heading:`05 · Publish and consume: closing the loop`,body:[`On the publish side, I don't let domain code touch MassTransit directly. One tiny abstraction — IEventBus — wraps the publish call, the command handler injects it, and after saving the product it publishes ProductCreated and returns. The handler doesn't know the indexer exists. That ignorance is the whole feature.`,`On the consume side, a consumer is almost embarrassingly small: a public sealed class implementing IConsumer<ProductCreatedEvent>, a logger injected through the constructor, and a Consume method that logs the product — in real life, that's where you'd store a local copy or kick off indexing. MassTransit wires up the queue, the binding, the deserialization, and the dispatch automatically. You wrote a class; it became a subscriber.`,`And notice what the command handler didn't do: wait. The 201 goes back to the client while the message is still in flight. If the consumer throws, MassTransit retries and eventually dead-letters — the request that created the product was never in danger.`],quote:`Your consumer is ten lines and one interface — MassTransit does the thankless plumbing of listening, deserializing, and dispatching.`,table:{headers:[`Side`,`You write`,`MassTransit handles`],rows:[[`Publish`,`IEventBus call with the event`,`Serialization, exchange routing, connection`],[`Consume`,`IConsumer<T> class with a Consume method`,`Queue creation, binding, delivery, retries`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Publish-consume topology with a Dockerized broker`,caption:`The API publishes through MassTransit; RabbitMQ holds the message; the catalog service's consumer picks it up independently.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph API["Product API - ASP.NET Core"]
        H["CreateProduct Handler"] --> EB["IEventBus"]
        EB --> BUS["MassTransit Bus"]
    end
    subgraph DOCKER["docker-compose"]
        RMQ["RabbitMQ container"]
        VOL[("persistent volume")]
        RMQ --- VOL
    end
    subgraph CATALOG["Catalog Service"]
        C["ProductCreatedEventConsumer"]
        L["ILogger + local product copy"]
        C --> L
    end
    BUS -->|"AMQP 5672"| RMQ
    RMQ --> Q["product-created queue"]
    Q -->|"deliver + ack"| C`},{id:`sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path: create product, publish, consume async`,caption:`The client gets its 201 before the consumer even wakes up — downstream latency never touches the write path.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant API as CreateProduct Handler
    participant BUS as MassTransit Bus
    participant RMQ as RabbitMQ
    participant CON as ProductCreatedEventConsumer
    API->>BUS: Publish ProductCreated event
    BUS->>RMQ: AMQP publish
    RMQ->>RMQ: Route to product-created queue
    API-->>API: Return 201 to client
    RMQ->>CON: Deliver message
    CON->>CON: Log ProductCreated
    CON->>CON: Store local product copy
    CON-->>RMQ: Ack
    Note over API,CON: Consumer latency and failures never block the original request`}],takeaways:[`Budget ~15 lines of docker-compose for RabbitMQ — image, hostname, ports 5672 and 15672, one volume — and never skip the volume, or a restart deletes every in-flight message.`,`Bind exactly three settings (host, username, password) to a MessageBrokerSettings class and resolve it from the bus registration context — zero connection strings hardcoded in Program.cs.`,`Call SetKebabCaseEndpointNameFormatter before your first consumer ships; renaming a consumer class later spawns a second queue and strands messages in the orphaned one.`,`One IConsumer<T> per message type: a ~10-line sealed class with an injected logger gets you queue creation, binding, deserialization, and dispatch for free.`,`Publish facts, not tasks: the handler returns 201 while the message is still in flight, and a dead downstream becomes the consumer's retry problem — not your checkout's 3 AM.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — What Is A Message Queue + RabbitMQ and MassTransit Integration`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CTKWFMZVIWA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-zCBqXJSoCj0`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:21,title:`Using Distributed Locking To Solve Race Conditions (and why a simple solution works better)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:29`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`Two Signups, One Email, and a 3 AM Support Fire`,paragraphs:[`Launch morning of the referral campaign. By 10 AM the signup graph is a hockey stick, and by noon support has a ticket that reads like a paradox: 'I registered, but I can't log in.' I open the users table and there they are — two rows, same email, different IDs, different password hashes. The code checks for an existing user before inserting. It's right there in the handler. So how?`,`The answer was a double-click. The button fired twice, two requests landed on two different pods behind the load balancer, and both ran the same dance: SELECT by email — empty — hash the password — INSERT. Both checks ran before either insert committed. Both inserts succeeded. There was no unique index on email, because 'the code already handles that.' Famous last words.`,`We didn't fix it with a fancier lock. We fixed it with a unique index and a catch block — and then, separately, decided whether we even wanted a lock to fail fast. That distinction, correctness versus convenience, is what this session is about.`],problem:`Checking uniqueness and writing the row are two separate steps; in the gap between them, any concurrent request passes the same check, and without an atomic enforcement point you get duplicate rows and a corrupted identity model.`},tech:{stack:[`C# lock`,`SemaphoreSlim`,`Redlock.net`,`Redis`,`Postgres`,`Advisory Locks`,`Unique Index`,`EF Core`],note:`These are the guards on the check-then-insert path: in-process primitives that don't survive scale-out, distributed locks (Redlock over Redis, Postgres advisory locks) that coordinate across instances, and the unique index — the only atomic enforcement point in the whole picture.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'check first, insert after' is a lie`,body:[`Let's walk the register handler together, because it looks airtight. First statement: query the database for a user with this email. Found one? Throw — that bubbles up to the global exception handler and comes back as a clean error response. Not found? Hash the password, add the user, save changes. Every line is correct. The sequence is not.`,`Between the SELECT and the INSERT there's a window, and password hashing alone is deliberately slow — that's the whole point of hashing. Two requests can both observe 'email is free' and both proceed. Uniqueness is a property of the database's state over time, and no amount of application-side checking can enforce a property across time. The check is itself part of the race.`,`Here's the interleaving that burned us: A checks, B checks, A inserts, B inserts. Both requests 'win.' The database never objected because nobody asked it to object — there was no constraint to object with.`],quote:`You can't check your way out of a race — the check is part of the race.`,table:{headers:[`Step`,`Request A`,`Request B`,`DB state`],rows:[[`1`,`SELECT email → 0 rows`,`—`,`empty`],[`2`,`hashing password…`,`SELECT email → 0 rows`,`empty`],[`3`,`INSERT → committed`,`hashing password…`,`1 row`],[`4`,`201 Created`,`INSERT → committed`,`2 rows, same email`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Distributed Locking To Solve Race Conditions (and why a simple solution works better)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCBqXJSoCj0`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: lock dies on await, SemaphoreSlim almost survives`,body:[`First instinct in C#: wrap the critical section in a lock statement around a private lock object. The compiler says no — you cannot await inside the body of a lock (CS1996), and our handler is async all the way down. Dead on arrival, and honestly, good riddance.`,`Next candidate: SemaphoreSlim with an initial count of one and a maximum count of one — effectively a mutex. You call WaitAsync, optionally with a timeout; if it returns true you're inside the critical section, if not you retry or fail the request. It composes with await, so the whole use case fits inside the if. This works — for about a week.`,`Then two dealbreakers show up at scale. One: it's a static instance, so it lives and dies inside a single process — in a distributed environment it guards nothing across pods. Two: it collapses registration concurrency to one for the entire use case. You could key a semaphore per email value, but now you're managing a concurrent dictionary of semaphores and their lifetimes. That's complexity renting space in your codebase for a problem the database will solve for free.`],quote:`An in-memory lock is a promise your other pods never made.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`lock statement`,`Zero ceremony, familiar`,`No await inside the body — CS1996`,`Sync-only code, single instance`],[`SemaphoreSlim 1-1`,`Await-friendly, timeout support, clean failure path`,`Per-process only; serializes all registrations to 1`,`Single instance, low traffic`],[`Keyed semaphores per email`,`Preserves concurrency`,`Unbounded memory, lifetime management`,`Rarely worth the complexity`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Distributed Locking To Solve Race Conditions (and why a simple solution works better)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCBqXJSoCj0`,at:`1:19`}},{heading:`03 · Going distributed: Redlock, and the lock hiding inside Postgres`,body:[`If we truly need cross-instance coordination, the popular route is Redlock — the Redlock.net package over Redis. Acquire a lock keyed on the email with a TTL, do the work, release. Now any number of application instances share a single arbiter, and the race window closes… mostly.`,`Read the fine print, though. Locks expire while your work is still running — a GC pause or a slow hash and your TTL is gone while another instance waltzes in. Clock drift between nodes, a Redis failover splitting the brain — a distributed lock is a probabilistic courtesy, not a physical law. It's also a new piece of infrastructure with its own uptime SLA, plus a network round trip on every acquire and release.`,`The nicer surprise: if you're already on Postgres, you've been carrying a distributed lock in your pocket. Advisory locks. Call pg_advisory_xact_lock with a key — hash the email down to a bigint — and the lock is scoped to your transaction and released automatically on commit or rollback. No new infrastructure, no TTL tuning, no orphaned locks when a pod dies mid-flight. The database you already run for free becomes the bouncer.`],quote:`A distributed lock doesn't remove the race — it rents a shared waiting room for it.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Redlock over Redis`,`Stack-agnostic, fast acquire, well-known tooling`,`Extra infra, TTL expiry mid-work, failover edge cases`,`Polyglot fleets already running Redis`],[`pg advisory xact lock`,`No new infra, auto-release on commit/rollback`,`Ties you to Postgres, holds a DB session`,`Postgres shops wanting simple coordination`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Distributed Locking To Solve Race Conditions (and why a simple solution works better)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCBqXJSoCj0`,at:`2:43`}},{heading:`04 · The plot twist: the database was the lock all along`,body:[`Here's the bonus, and it's almost embarrassing how boring it is: put a unique index on the email column. Now the second INSERT fails atomically inside the storage engine — no window, no arbiter, no TTL, no cleanup. EF Core surfaces it as a DbUpdateException; crack open the inner exception and you'll find the Postgres error with SqlState 23505, unique_violation. Catch it, translate it, return a 409 with a friendly message. Done.`,`So what are the locks for, then? Fail-fast and user experience. A distributed lock — or even the humble in-process semaphore — can stop the losing request before it burns CPU on password hashing, sends a confirmation email, or spends a database round trip. That's an optimization. The unique constraint is the correctness backstop underneath every single one of them.`,`The rule I've settled on after this incident: locks make the common case polite; constraints make every case correct. If you only have budget for one, buy the constraint — it's the only guard in this entire talk that cannot forget why it exists.`],quote:`Locks are a courtesy; a unique constraint is a law.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Scope`,`Correctness`,`Cost`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`lock statement`,`Single method`,`None for async code`,`None`,`Dead on arrival`],[`SemaphoreSlim`,`One process`,`Per-instance only`,`Serializes to 1`,`Dev environments`],[`Redlock / Redis`,`All instances`,`Probabilistic — TTL, failover`,`New infra + 1-5 ms RTT`,`Fail-fast layer`],[`pg advisory xact lock`,`All instances`,`Strong while tx holds it`,`Holds a DB session`,`Fail-fast layer`],[`Unique index`,`All instances`,`Atomic, cannot be bypassed`,`One index`,`Always ship this`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Distributed Locking To Solve Race Conditions (and why a simple solution works better)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCBqXJSoCj0`,at:`4:35`}}],diagrams:[{id:`race-interleaving`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The race, frame by frame`,caption:`The 40-millisecond heist: both requests pass the same check because neither insert has committed yet.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as Request A
    participant B as Request B
    participant DB as Postgres

    A->>DB: SELECT user WHERE email = ana@x.com
    DB-->>A: 0 rows - email is free
    B->>DB: SELECT user WHERE email = ana@x.com
    DB-->>B: 0 rows - email is free
    A->>DB: INSERT user A
    DB-->>A: 201 Created
    B->>DB: INSERT user B
    DB-->>B: 201 Created
    Note over A,DB: Check and insert are not atomic - both requests win`},{id:`layered-defense`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Guard rails: fail fast, then fall back to the constraint`,caption:`Layered defense: locks decide who goes first, the unique index decides who's right.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    Req[POST /register] --> Check{Email already taken?}
    Check -- yes --> C409[409 Conflict]
    Check -- no --> Scope{Single instance?}
    Scope -- yes --> Sem[SemaphoreSlim mutex - per process]
    Scope -- no --> Dist[Redlock via Redis or pg advisory xact lock]
    Sem -- fail fast --> Ins[Hash password then INSERT]
    Dist -- fail fast --> Ins
    Ins --> Viol{Unique index violated?}
    Viol -- no --> Ok[201 Created]
    Viol -- 23505 --> Catch[Catch DbUpdateException]
    Catch --> C409`}],takeaways:[`The race window is the gap between SELECT and INSERT — often 5-50 ms of password hashing and I/O — and any request landing inside it duplicates the row.`,`lock cannot contain await (compiler error CS1996); for async in-process mutual exclusion use SemaphoreSlim(1, 1) with WaitAsync and a timeout.`,`A static SemaphoreSlim is per-process: on N instances you have N independent locks, i.e. no lock at all — and it throttles all registrations to concurrency 1 unless you key it per email.`,`Distributed locks cost a network round trip per acquire/release (~1-5 ms to Redis) and carry failure modes — TTL expiry mid-work, clock drift, failover — so treat them as a fail-fast optimization, never the source of truth.`,`Always pair any lock with a unique index on email; catch DbUpdateException, check the inner PostgresException SqlState 23505, and return 409 — the constraint is the only guard that is atomic and cannot be bypassed.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Distributed Locking To Solve Race Conditions (and why a simple solution works better)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCBqXJSoCj0`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-smXAgcdJzLc`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:22,title:`How To Make Your API Idempotent To Stop Duplicate Requests`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:27`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`REST`,`Idempotency`],story:{headline:`The Double-Click That Charged Him Twice`,paragraphs:[`It's 09:15 during the morning rush and the checkout service pager goes off. The support dashboard shows a customer with two identical orders, created 900 milliseconds apart, both charged. Nothing failed — that's the part that keeps you up. The mobile client hit a 5-second timeout, quietly retried, and both POSTs sailed through green. Two rows, two payments, one very awake engineer.`,`We didn't need a smarter queue or a retry budget. We needed the endpoint itself to be idempotent: same request in, same response out, side effect fired exactly once — no matter how many times the network, the client, or a nervous user's thumb decides to send it again.`,`The trap is in the details. The moment you add a request ID, you inherit a question the original design never had to answer: when the duplicate shows up, what exactly do you hand back?`],problem:`When a duplicate request arrives with a request ID we've already processed, what should the API return — the default TResponse, an error, or the original response? Get this wrong and the idempotency layer becomes the very thing that corrupts the client's view of the world.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`MediatR`,`Pipeline Behavior`,`EF Core`,`Idempotency Key`,`Unique Constraint`],note:`A MediatR pipeline behavior intercepts every IdempotentCommand, consults a request-ID store persisted with EF Core, and short-circuits duplicates before the handler ever runs.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why isn't POST idempotent already?`,body:[`Start with the spec, because it's doing more work than people realize. GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent by definition — fire them ten times, the server ends up in the same state. POST is not. POST says 'create something new,' and if you fire it ten times, congratulations, you have ten somethings.`,`Now layer reality on top: mobile clients time out and retry, users double-click, message queues deliver at-least-once. Duplicates aren't a hypothetical — they're a scheduled guest. So the real question was never 'will a duplicate arrive?' It's 'what happens when it does?'`,`For our create-product endpoint, today's answer is 'two products.' That's the bug we're here to kill, and the tool is an idempotency key: the client stamps every logical operation with a unique request ID, and the server promises that ID triggers the side effect exactly once.`],quote:`Retries aren't an edge case — they're a guarantee of unreliable networks. Build for the duplicate you know is coming.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Do nothing`,`Zero effort`,`Duplicates create real side effects`,`Never`],[`Client-side dedupe`,`No server changes`,`Every client must comply; leaks abstractions`,`Weak`],[`Natural key upsert`,`Database-enforced`,`Only works when a natural key exists`,`Situational`],[`Idempotency key + store`,`Works for any write; replays original response`,`Needs a table and a pipeline behavior`,`Best`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make Your API Idempotent To Stop Duplicate Requests`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=smXAgcdJzLc`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: one abstract base class marks the intent`,body:[`Here's how I want to mark 'this command must run at most once' in code: an abstract IdempotentCommand — honestly, make it a record — carrying a single RequestId and implementing IRequest from MediatR. That's the entire contract.`,`Then CreateProductCommand inherits from it and passes the RequestId up through its constructor. One line of inheritance and the command now travels with its own dedupe identity. The value itself comes from the caller — the client generates it and we bind it from the request — because the server can't know which two attempts the client considers 'the same operation.'`,`I love this shape because idempotency becomes opt-in by type. You don't remember to check a header inside each handler; if your command inherits IdempotentCommand, the machinery exists. If it doesn't, nothing changes.`],quote:`Make idempotency opt-in by type, not opt-in by discipline.`,table:{headers:[`Key source`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Client-generated GUID header`,`Survives retries across processes`,`Trust the client to reuse it`,`Best`],[`Server-generated`,`No client work required`,`Every retry looks brand new — useless`,`Never`],[`Hash of the payload`,`No client changes`,`Breaks on timestamps, ordering, noise`,`Fragile`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make Your API Idempotent To Stop Duplicate Requests`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=smXAgcdJzLc`,at:`3:13`}},{heading:`03 · The pipeline behavior: intercept before the handler ever wakes up`,body:[`Next, the enforcement point. I'm building an internal sealed generic class implementing IPipelineBehavior<TRequest, TResponse> — with the generic constraint that TRequest must be an IdempotentCommand. That constraint is the magic: the behavior only runs for commands that opted in, and MediatR routes everything else straight past it.`,`Inside, the logic is two lines of intent: does a request with this ID already exist? If yes, break the pipeline — return the stored response and never touch the handler. If no, create the marker and await next() to let the real command run.`,`Registration is one line in the DI setup: AddOpenBehavior with the behavior type, inside the AddMediator call. After that, every idempotent command in the app gets the same guarantee for free. Handlers stay dumb — they process, they don't police.`],quote:`Cross-cutting concerns belong in the pipeline. Handlers should do the work, not stand guard against duplicates.`,table:{headers:[`Enforcement point`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`MediatR pipeline behavior`,`One implementation, all commands, testable`,`Slightly abstract to debug`,`Best`],[`In each handler`,`Explicit and obvious`,`Copy-pasted logic; one missed handler is a bug`,`Never`],[`HTTP middleware`,`Framework-agnostic`,`No access to command semantics or response type`,`Awkward`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make Your API Idempotent To Stop Duplicate Requests`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=smXAgcdJzLc`,at:`6:15`}},{heading:`04 · Ordering is the whole game: write the marker before the work`,body:[`Here's the subtlety that separates a working idempotency layer from a false sense of security: I create the request record BEFORE processing the command. Why? Because both operations can fail, and the order decides which failure you can recover from.`,`Flip it around: suppose the command succeeds but writing the marker fails. The client retries, the marker still isn't there, and the command runs again — a side effect that already happened, happening twice, because of the safety mechanism itself. That's the worst possible outcome. I'd much rather the entire request fail because the marker couldn't be written, and let the client retry cleanly.`,`And under concurrency, the unique constraint on the request ID is your referee. Two identical requests racing in: one insert wins, the other gets a constraint violation and takes the duplicate path. No locks, no distributed mutex — just the database doing what databases do.`],quote:`Write the tombstone first. If you can't record that you're doing it, don't do it.`,table:{headers:[`Ordering`,`Failure mode`,`Duplicate risk`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Marker before command`,`Whole request fails; retry is safe`,`Blocked by unique constraint`,`Correct`],[`Command before marker`,`Command succeeds, marker fails, retry re-runs it`,`Real duplicates possible`,`Dangerous`],[`No marker, check-then-act`,`Race window between check and insert`,`Concurrent duplicates slip through`,`Broken`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make Your API Idempotent To Stop Duplicate Requests`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=smXAgcdJzLc`,at:`7:08`}},{heading:`05 · Persistence: the request table — and what a duplicate gets back`,body:[`Over in the persistence project, this needs a home: an idempotency folder, an entity representing the requests table, and the primary key is the idempotent request ID itself. That key is the whole index — every lookup is a single clustered seek.`,`The service behind it — IIdempotencyService — exposes two operations: does this request ID exist, and create the record. And here's the answer to the question that started all of this: when a duplicate arrives, we return the stored response from the first execution. Not the default TResponse — that's how you hand back an empty shell and confuse the client. Not a 409 — the operation didn't conflict, it already succeeded.`,`One operational note before we close: this table grows forever if you let it. Keep records longer than your clients' retry window — 24 to 48 hours covers almost every real retry pattern — and purge on a schedule. An idempotency store that outlives its usefulness is just a slow table.`],quote:`A duplicate deserves the original answer, not a shrug.`,table:{headers:[`Return on duplicate`,`Client experience`,`Risk`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Stored original response`,`Retry is invisible; same 201 every time`,`Must persist the payload`,`Best`],[`Default TResponse`,`Client gets an empty, meaningless body`,`Silent data corruption at the client`,`Never`],[`409 Conflict`,`Loud and honest`,`Client treats success as failure; retry storms`,`Poor`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make Your API Idempotent To Stop Duplicate Requests`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=smXAgcdJzLc`,at:`7:51`}}],diagrams:[{id:`seq-idempotent-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`First delivery vs duplicate: the pipeline in action`,caption:`The behavior consults the store before the handler; a duplicate never reaches business logic.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant P as Idempotent Pipeline Behavior
    participant S as Idempotency Store
    participant H as CreateProduct Handler
    C->>P: POST /products + X-Request-Id abc-123
    P->>S: Exists abc-123?
    alt First delivery
        S-->>P: false
        P->>S: Insert marker abc-123
        P->>H: await next()
        H-->>P: Product created
        P->>S: Save response
        P-->>C: 201 Created
    else Duplicate delivery
        S-->>P: true + stored response
        P-->>C: Same response, zero side effects
    end`},{id:`flow-decision`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Decision path inside the idempotent pipeline behavior`,caption:`The marker insert with a unique constraint turns a concurrent race into a deterministic winner.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Request arrives with RequestId"] --> B{"RequestId already in store?"}
    B -- "Yes" --> C["Return stored original response"]
    C --> Z["Side effect never re-runs"]
    B -- "No" --> D["Insert marker row keyed by RequestId"]
    D --> E{"Unique constraint held?"}
    E -- "Lost the race" --> F["Fail fast: another worker owns it"]
    E -- "Insert won" --> G["Run command handler"]
    G --> H["Persist domain change"]
    H --> I["Save response against RequestId"]
    I --> J["Return response to caller"]`}],takeaways:[`Generate the idempotency key on the client and reuse it on every retry — one key per logical operation, never per HTTP attempt.`,`Insert the marker row before running the handler; the unique constraint on the request ID is your only real defense against concurrent duplicates.`,`Persist the original response alongside the key so a duplicate returns the exact same 201 — never a default TResponse or a misleading 409.`,`Keep idempotency records longer than your longest client retry window — 24 to 48 hours is a sane default — then purge them on a schedule.`,`Gate the behavior with a generic constraint (TRequest : IdempotentCommand) so only write commands pay the lookup cost — queries and reads stay untouched.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Make Your API Idempotent To Stop Duplicate Requests`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=smXAgcdJzLc`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`idempotency-keys`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-AHzWJ_SMqLo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:23,title:`Using Domain Events To Build A Decoupled System That Scales`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:03`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`RabbitMQ`,`Rebus`],story:{headline:`The Night the Warehouse Went Blind`,paragraphs:[`14:30 right after a canary deploy, the Saturday before Black Friday. The on-call phone lights up: fulfillment has not received a single order since 2:31. Dashboards are green, the orders table is filling up, customers are getting confirmation emails. But RabbitMQ's management UI shows zero messages published to the orders exchange for sixteen straight minutes. Every order in that window was saved, paid, and confirmed — and completely invisible to the warehouse.`,`The postmortem came down to one method. The CreateOrder handler saved to the database, then called bus.Publish on the Rebus bus. Somewhere in that window, the RabbitMQ connection blipped during a load-balancer restart. The save committed; the publish threw. Two external systems, one method, no shared transaction — and the failure mode nobody had tested: success on one side, exception on the other.`,`The week before, we had seen the mirror image: an event published, then the save failed on a constraint, and invoicing spent a morning chasing an order that never existed. Retries and alerting were band-aids. The real fix was structural — stop letting the handler talk to two systems at once. Move the event into the domain, collect it on the entity, and dispatch it in exactly one place, after the save, with one owner.`],problem:`Saving to the database and publishing to the bus are two independent external calls in one handler with no shared transaction — either can fail alone, producing persisted orders that downstream systems never hear about, or messages about orders that were never persisted.`},tech:{stack:[`RabbitMQ`,`Rebus`,`EF Core`,`Mediator`,`Domain Events`,`Outbox Pattern`],note:`The pipeline that replaces the dual-write: the aggregate raises domain events, EF Core collects them at SaveChanges, Mediator dispatches them in-process, and Rebus carries integration events out to RabbitMQ.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does one handler own two failure domains?`,body:[`So here is the handler as I found it: validate, map, save to the database, then bus.Publish an OrderCreated message through Rebus to RabbitMQ. Looks innocent. It is two external systems in one method — Postgres and a broker — and there is no transaction in the universe that spans both. If the save commits and the publish throws, the order exists and nobody knows. If the publish lands and the save fails, half your platform believes in an order that does not.`,`Why not just wrap it in retries? Because retries trade one inconsistency for another — you either lose the event or duplicate it, and now every consumer needs idempotency just to survive your handler. Why not a distributed, two-phase commit across EF Core and RabbitMQ? Because no broker worth running supports it well, and you would couple your domain's fate to a message broker's uptime. The actual fix is structural: stop publishing from the handler at all.`],quote:`Two external systems in one method is not a workflow — it is a coin flip you flip on every request.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inline publish in handler`,`Simple, one method, easy to read`,`Dual-write: no atomicity across DB and broker`,`Prototypes only`],[`Domain events + post-save dispatch`,`Decoupled, testable, single persistence path`,`Handler failure can still roll back the save`,`Default for in-process decoupling`],[`Transactional outbox`,`Atomic persist + event record, durable retries`,`More moving parts, a dispatcher to operate`,`Must-not-lose, money-moving events`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Domain Events To Build A Decoupled System That Scales`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHzWJ_SMqLo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: domain events vs integration events`,body:[`First distinction that untangles everything: a domain event is not an integration event. The OrderCreated I publish over Rebus is an integration event — it crosses the bounded context, it gets serialized, it has a wire format. The domain event is the same fact expressed in the language of the domain, and it never leaves the domain project.`,`So in my Primitives folder I drop a DomainEvent base record — record, class, interface, pick one and be boring about it — with a Guid identifier on it. Why the Guid? Because the moment you introduce integration events, you want a one-to-one correlation key: this domain event became that message on the wire. Then OrderCreatedDomainEvent becomes a record inheriting the base, carrying the strongly-typed OrderId. The domain stays bus-ignorant; translation to integration events happens at the edge.`],quote:`A domain event whispers inside the boundary; an integration event shouts across it.`,table:{headers:[`Aspect`,`Domain Event`,`Integration Event`],rows:[[`Scope`,`Inside the bounded context`,`Crosses bounded contexts`],[`Lives in`,`Domain project, plain record`,`Messaging contracts, serialized`],[`Transport`,`In-process via Mediator`,`Rebus over RabbitMQ`],[`Example`,`OrderCreatedDomainEvent`,`OrderCreated message on the wire`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Domain Events To Build A Decoupled System That Scales`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHzWJ_SMqLo`,at:`5:04`}},{heading:`03 · Raising the event where the decision happens`,body:[`Where do events get raised? Not in the handler — the handler only knows that a command arrived. The aggregate knows that something happened. So Order.Create, the static factory, calls Raise with a new OrderCreatedDomainEvent and appends it to a DomainEvents list on the entity base class — initialized empty so we never null-reference. Same for RemoveLineItem: raise LineItemRemoved with the order ID and the line item ID, right where the line item actually dies.`,`The payoff is embarrassingly good tests: new an order, call the method, assert the event is in the list. No mocks, no broker, no database. Why not raise from the handler instead? Because then your tests need the handler, the mediator, and half the application layer to verify a rule that lives entirely inside the aggregate. Events belong to behavior, and behavior belongs to the aggregate.`],quote:`Raise the event where the decision is made — the aggregate remembers, the handler just asks.`,table:{headers:[`Where to raise`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Inside the aggregate`,`Plain unit-test assertions; facts match behavior`,`Needs entity base-class plumbing`,`Default choice`],[`In the command handler`,`No base-class plumbing`,`Handler must know domain rules; tests need mocks`,`Avoid`],[`Returned from methods`,`Explicit, no hidden state`,`Callers can forget to forward them`,`Small, disciplined teams`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Domain Events To Build A Decoupled System That Scales`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHzWJ_SMqLo`,at:`9:32`}},{heading:`04 · The SaveChanges hook: collect, persist, then dispatch`,body:[`Now the plumbing. The entity base class exposes a read-only collection of DomainEvents. In the application DbContext I override SaveChangesAsync: walk the ChangeTracker, grab the entity entries, filter down to the ones that actually have events, then SelectMany to flatten the list-of-lists into one stream of domain events. From here there are two options — publish before the save or after. I dispatch after base.SaveChangesAsync, through Mediator, because my domain events implement INotification from Mediator.Contracts.`,`Why after? Because handlers will query the database, and they should see committed state, not a maybe. Why not fire-and-forget on a background thread? Because then a failure is silent and you have reinvented losing messages, just in-process. And here is the honest drawback: this is still one transaction's blast radius — if a domain event handler throws, that exception propagates and can roll back the entire save. Better than a dual-write, but it is the tax you pay until you reach for the outbox.`],quote:`Persist first, dispatch second — handlers that read uncommitted state are reading fiction.`,table:{headers:[`Dispatch timing`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Before SaveChanges`,`Handlers see pre-save state`,`Handlers act on uncommitted data; failure blocks the save`,`Rarely`],[`After base.SaveChangesAsync`,`Handlers read committed state; simple`,`Handler exception can roll back the save`,`Default in-process`],[`After commit via outbox`,`Durable, retryable, decoupled from the request`,`Eventual consistency, dispatcher infrastructure`,`Production-critical flows`]]}},{heading:`05 · What this buys you — and the bill that comes due`,body:[`So what did we actually buy? The CreateOrder handler now does one thing: coordinate the command. Persistence and event collection happen in one place, the domain owns its facts, tests are plain object assertions, and every event carries a Guid ready to become the correlation ID for the integration event that Rebus will eventually publish to RabbitMQ.`,`What we did not buy: durability. In-process Mediator dispatch means that if the process dies after the save but before handlers run — or a handler fails — the event is gone or the transaction unwinds. The next rung on this ladder is the outbox pattern: write integration events into the same database in the same transaction, and let a background dispatcher publish them through Rebus with retries. Domain events make the system decoupled and honest; the outbox makes it survive 3 AM. You will want both, in that order.`],quote:`Domain events fix your coupling; only the outbox fixes your durability.`}],diagrams:[{id:`dual-write-trap`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The dual-write trap`,caption:`One handler, two external systems, four outcome combinations — and two of them leave the system inconsistent.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    H["CreateOrder Handler - one method, two external systems"] -->|"save"| DB[("Postgres")]
    H -->|"publish"| MQ["RabbitMQ via Rebus"]
    DB --> D{"Did BOTH succeed?"}
    MQ --> D
    D -->|"yes"| OK["Consistent: order persisted + event delivered"]
    D -->|"save ok, publish fails"| GHOST["GHOST ORDER: persisted, downstream blind"]
    D -->|"publish ok, save fails"| PHANTOM["PHANTOM ORDER: downstream acts on nothing"]`},{id:`domain-event-dispatch`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Domain event dispatch flow`,caption:`The aggregate raises events, SaveChangesAsync collects them from the Change Tracker, persists first, then dispatches through Mediator.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant H as CreateOrder Handler
    participant Agg as Order Aggregate
    participant Ctx as App DbContext
    participant Med as Mediator
    participant Hnd as Domain Event Handlers
    H->>Agg: Order.Create(command)
    Agg->>Agg: Raise(new OrderCreatedDomainEvent(id, orderId))
    Note over Agg: appended to entity.DomainEvents list
    H->>Ctx: SaveChangesAsync()
    Ctx->>Ctx: ChangeTracker - collect entities with events
    Ctx->>Ctx: SelectMany - flatten to one list of DomainEvents
    Ctx->>Ctx: base.SaveChangesAsync() - persist first
    Ctx->>Med: publish each event as INotification
    Med->>Hnd: dispatch handlers
    Note over Ctx,Hnd: handlers run after persist - a handler failure can still roll back the save`}],takeaways:[`One handler, one external system: if a method calls both a database and a broker, it is a dual-write — split it before you build anything on top of it.`,`Raise 100% of domain events inside the aggregate (factory methods and state-changing methods), so every state change is discoverable via entity.DomainEvents in plain unit tests with zero mocks.`,`Dispatch in SaveChangesAsync after base.SaveChangesAsync so handlers always read committed state — and keep it to 3 or fewer handlers per event to cap the blast radius.`,`Stamp every domain event with a Guid so each one maps 1:1 to its future integration event the moment the fact crosses the bounded context.`,`In-process dispatch is not durable: a single handler exception can roll back the entire save — graduate to the outbox pattern before you trust it with money-moving events.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Using Domain Events To Build A Decoupled System That Scales`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHzWJ_SMqLo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-V2CHhVhPw8E`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:24,title:`Reliable Messaging in C#: Wolverine Outbox & Inbox Tutorial`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:55`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`RabbitMQ`,`Wolverine`,`.NET`,`C#`,`Outbox`,`Inbox`],story:{headline:`The registration that vanished between Postgres and RabbitMQ`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:20 during end-of-month billing and the onboarding dashboard is a graveyard: 1,400 users registered, zero onboarding sagas started. The RegisterUser handler looks innocent enough — save the user to Postgres, publish a UserRegistered event to RabbitMQ, done. Except the publish happens after SaveChanges, on a separate connection, and somewhere along the way that connection dies between the commit and the publish.`,`The database row is right there. The broker never saw the message. No error, no retry, no saga — just a silent gap between two systems that each assume the other one did the work. That's the dual write problem, and it never announces itself; it waits for a deploy, a network blip, or a broker restart.`,`The fix is embarrassingly small: route the write and the publish through the same database transaction using Wolverine's outbox, then mirror the trick on the consumer side with the inbox. Two patterns, a couple of lines of code, and the 16:45 on a pre-release Friday page disappears.`],problem:`Writing to your database and publishing to a message broker are two independent operations — you can't make them atomic, so one will eventually succeed while the other fails, leaving your system in an in-between state.`},tech:{stack:[`Wolverine`,`RabbitMQ`,`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Marten`,`Outbox Pattern`,`Inbox Pattern`,`Outbox Processor`],note:`Wolverine brokers messages over RabbitMQ while outbox and inbox tables in Postgres collapse the dual write into a single atomic transaction; Marten or EF Core provide the persistence underneath, including saga state.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the dual write bites`,body:[`Let's start at the crime scene. A handler wants to persist something — say, a new user — and publish a message about it. Those are two different systems: Postgres and RabbitMQ. There's no distributed transaction here, no magic that commits both at once. You write the row, you publish the message, and if the process dies in between, you've got a user with no onboarding event.`,`You might think: publish first, then write? Now a failed write leaves a phantom event sitting in the broker. Write first, then publish? A failed publish leaves a silent row. Order the operations however you like — the gap just moves. And naively retrying the whole handler risks duplicating the row.`,`So the real question isn't 'which order?' — it's 'how do I make both writes behave like one write?' And the answer has been sitting in your database the whole time: transactions.`],quote:`Two systems, one commit — or you don't get to call it reliable.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Write then publish`,`Simple, no extra infra`,`Publish lost on crash → silent gap`,`Fire-and-forget only`],[`Publish then write`,`Broker always gets the event`,`Phantom events on failed write`,`Rarely correct`],[`Distributed 2PC`,`Atomic across systems`,`Fragile, broker support varies`,`Avoid`],[`Outbox pattern`,`One atomic transaction`,`Slight publish latency`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Reliable Messaging in C#: Wolverine Outbox & Inbox Tutorial`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V2CHhVhPw8E`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the outbox: one transaction to rule both writes`,body:[`Here's the trick. Open a database transaction. Write your business row. Then write the message itself as just another row — into an outbox table. Commit. Now either both changes landed or neither did; there is no in-between state to debug at 3 AM.`,`But nobody has talked to RabbitMQ yet, and that's fine, because a second actor enters the stage: the outbox processor. It's a background service polling the outbox table for unprocessed rows and publishing them to the broker, which delivers them to consumers. If the broker is down, the rows just sit there and get retried — the message can no longer get lost between commit and publish.`,`The cost? You've traded instant publishing for eventual publishing, and you've signed up for at-least-once delivery — the processor may retry a row it already sent, so consumers need to tolerate duplicates.`],quote:`The outbox doesn't make publishing faster — it makes it unloseable.`,table:{headers:[`Property`,`Direct publish`,`Outbox publish`],rows:[[`Atomicity vs DB write`,`None`,`Same transaction`],[`Broker outage`,`Message lost or exception`,`Buffered, retried later`],[`Delivery guarantee`,`At-most-once in practice`,`At-least-once`],[`Latency`,`Immediate`,`Poll interval delay`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Reliable Messaging in C#: Wolverine Outbox & Inbox Tutorial`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V2CHhVhPw8E`,at:`1:12`}},{heading:`03 · Flipping the mirror: the inbox on the consumer side`,body:[`The outbox protects the publisher, but the consumer has the mirror-image problem. RabbitMQ hands you a message, you process it, you write results to the database — and crash mid-way. The broker redelivers, you reprocess, side effects double. Or you write first and ack later, and a crash between the write and the ack duplicates the work all over again.`,`The inbox pattern inverts the flow: when a message arrives, the very first thing you do is store it in an inbox table — inside a transaction. If that commit fails, you haven't processed anything, so it's safe to let the broker redeliver until the store succeeds. Once it's stored, you check for pending messages in the inbox table and hand them off to the handler, which is where the actual business logic runs.`,`Notice the symmetry: publisher and consumer are both leaning on the same idea — use the database as the reliable buffer and take the broker's flaky network moment out of the critical path. Processing state lives in the same database as your results, so recovery is a query, not a guess.`],quote:`Store it before you process it — the inbox is the outbox wearing the other team's jersey.`,table:{headers:[`Step`,`Without inbox`,`With inbox`],rows:[[`On message arrival`,`Process immediately`,`Store in inbox table (transaction)`],[`Crash before processing`,`Broker redelivers → duplicate work`,`Redelivered, still unprocessed → safe`],[`Crash mid-processing`,`Partial side effects, redelivery doubles them`,`State tracked in DB, resumable`],[`Duplicate tolerance needed`,`High`,`Lower — delivery tracked per message`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Reliable Messaging in C#: Wolverine Outbox & Inbox Tutorial`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V2CHhVhPw8E`,at:`2:29`}},{heading:`04 · Wolverine makes it two lines — pick your persistence`,body:[`Enough theory — here's the part I love. The baseline app already runs Wolverine with RabbitMQ: UseRabbitMq for the broker connection, conventional local routing disabled so everything actually goes through the broker, and Postgres message persistence keeping saga state — one table per saga class, optimistic concurrency via versioning, and the row deleted when the saga completes.`,`To switch on outbox and inbox, you need a storage mechanism that supports them, and Wolverine gives you exactly two: Marten, the Critter Stack document database that turns Postgres into a document store, or — for this app — the Wolverine.EntityFrameworkCore package. Then swap the plain DbContext for an OutboxDbContext and stop calling SaveChanges().`,`The key move: at the end of the handler, call outboxDbContext.SaveChangesAndFlushMessagesAsync() instead. That single call commits the transaction and flushes the queued outgoing messages through the outbox — one atomic step. RegisterUser saves the user and publishes UserRegistered, which kicks off the onboarding saga, and none of it can half-happen.`],quote:`Don't call SaveChanges — call SaveChangesAndFlushMessagesAsync, and the commit and the publish become the same event.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Marten`,`Document DB + outbox in one, Critter Stack native`,`New data-access paradigm to learn`,`Greenfield Postgres apps`],[`Wolverine.EntityFrameworkCore`,`Drops into existing EF Core code`,`Relational only`,`Existing EF Core codebases`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Reliable Messaging in C#: Wolverine Outbox & Inbox Tutorial`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V2CHhVhPw8E`,at:`3:47`}}],diagrams:[{id:`outbox-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Outbox: one transaction for the row and the message`,caption:`The business write and the message write commit together; the background processor bridges to RabbitMQ.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Publisher["Publishing Service"]
        H["RegisterUser Handler"] --> TX["Open DB Transaction"]
        TX --> BIZ["Insert user row"]
        TX --> OB["Insert message into outbox table"]
        BIZ --> C["Commit atomically"]
        OB --> C
        C --> P["Outbox Processor<br/>background poller"]
    end
    P -->|"publish unprocessed rows"| MQ[("RabbitMQ")]
    MQ -->|"deliver"| CON["Consumer Service"]`},{id:`inbox-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Inbox: store first, process second`,caption:`The consumer commits the message to the inbox table before any business logic runs; failure simply triggers redelivery.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant MQ as RabbitMQ
    participant C as Consumer Service
    participant DB as Postgres
    participant H as Handler
    MQ->>C: Deliver message
    C->>DB: BEGIN transaction
    C->>DB: INSERT into inbox table
    alt Commit succeeds
        DB-->>C: Committed
        C->>H: Hand off pending message
        H->>DB: Execute business logic
    else Commit fails
        DB-->>C: Rollback
        C-->>MQ: No ack
        MQ-->>C: Redeliver until stored
    end`}],takeaways:[`Any handler that writes to Postgres and publishes to RabbitMQ is a dual write — route it through the outbox or accept a silent-gap failure mode.`,`In Wolverine + EF Core, replace DbContext with OutboxDbContext and swap SaveChanges() for SaveChangesAndFlushMessagesAsync() — one call commits the transaction and flushes queued messages atomically.`,`Exactly two storage options unlock outbox/inbox in Wolverine: Marten for document-style Postgres, Wolverine.EntityFrameworkCore for existing EF Core code — pick by your data stack, not by hype.`,`Both patterns trade instant messaging for guaranteed messaging: expect at-least-once delivery end to end, so make handlers idempotent and let the outbox processor retry unprocessed rows.`,`Mirror the pattern on both sides — outbox before the broker, inbox after it — and the database becomes the single source of truth for what has and hasn't happened.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Reliable Messaging in C#: Wolverine Outbox & Inbox Tutorial`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V2CHhVhPw8E`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-vS9_WsNwLlo`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:25,title:`The Inbox Pattern in .NET (The Outbox's Missing Half)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:04`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`.NET`,`Outbox`,`Inbox`],story:{headline:`The Night a Lost Ack Settled the Same Invoice Twice`,paragraphs:[`It's 02:47 on a Tuesday and the payments consumer has just processed an invoice-settled event. The handler updates the ledger, fires the receipt email, and sends its ack back to RabbitMQ. Halfway across the wire, the connection flaps. The ack never lands. RabbitMQ, doing exactly what it was designed to do, holds the message unacknowledged and redelivers it thirty seconds later. The handler runs again. The ledger gets a second settlement row. The customer gets a second receipt. Nobody notices until the reconciliation job screams at 3 AM.`,`The post-mortem is embarrassingly short. The publisher side was bulletproof — we'd shipped an outbox months ago, business data and messages commit atomically, the outbox processor drains it faithfully. The hole was on the other end of the wire: nothing between RabbitMQ and our handler remembered what had already been done. The logs show the same message ID, processed twice, both times 'successfully'. The broker wasn't wrong. Our consumer was amnesiac.`,`The fix wasn't a smarter retry policy, a different broker, or a distributed lock service. It was one table, one unique constraint on message_id, and one INSERT that either lands or gets ignored. That's the inbox pattern — the outbox's missing half — and once you see the symmetry, you can't unsee it.`],problem:`When a broker redelivers a message the consumer already processed — because the ack was lost, not because the work failed — nothing on the consumer side stops the side effects from running twice. The inbox pattern closes that gap by recording every consumed message ID in a table with a unique constraint, so redeliveries are detected and discarded, and the dedupe check can share a transaction with the business work itself.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`.NET`,`Outbox Pattern`,`Inbox Table`,`Unique Constraint`],note:`Postgres hosts the inbox table whose unique constraint swallows redeliveries, RabbitMQ keeps redelivering anything it never saw acknowledged, and MassTransit (the free, open-source version) drives the consume loop in .NET — with the outbox on the publisher side as the inbox's mirror image.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'at-least-once' quietly means 'twice'`,body:[`So here's where I always start: the first rule of distributed systems is that nothing is guaranteed. If you've worked with them long enough, you can recite the fallacies of distributed computing in your sleep. And the hop between our message broker and our consumer is a tiny component of a much larger distributed system — but it's still an external component talking to code running inside our application, across a network that owes us nothing.`,`And there are multiple ways this can fail. The obvious one is 'the consumer crashed mid-processing' — that's easy, the message stays unacked and gets redelivered. The nasty one is: the consumer processes the message successfully, and then fails to notify the broker. The ack dies on a flapping connection. From the broker's point of view, that message was never handled, so it redelivers. From your handler's point of view, it's Tuesday afternoon and the same invoice just arrived twice.`,`Now, why not just buy a smarter broker that promises exactly-once delivery? Because that promise doesn't exist on the wire — every 'exactly-once' system is doing dedupe somewhere, it's just hiding the inbox from you. So the question shifts from 'how do we stop redeliveries' to 'how do we make redeliveries harmless.' That reframing is the whole talk.`],quote:`You can't stop the broker from saying it twice — you can only stop your database from listening twice.`,table:{headers:[`Guarantee`,`What it means`,`Failure mode`,`Fit`],rows:[[`At-most-once`,`Fire and forget, no retries`,`Silent message loss`,`Metrics, logs`],[`At-least-once`,`Broker retries until acked`,`Duplicates on lost acks`,`Default queue mode`],[`Exactly-once effect`,`Duplicates detected and discarded`,`You own the dedupe state`,`Payments, inventory`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Inbox Pattern in .NET (The Outbox's Missing Half)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vS9_WsNwLlo`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the inbox is the outbox, mirrored`,body:[`So let's walk the boundary. On the publisher side we already know the move: an outbox table written in the same transaction as the business data, and an outbox processor that drains unprocessed messages into the queue. The publisher can never publish a change it didn't save. But what about the consumer side? That's the gap, and the inbox pattern fills it with — surprise — another buffer table inside a SQL database, the exact opposite of an outbox.`,`And I love that it's a mirror image. Outbox: written when business data commits, guards against lost publishes, stamps OccurredOnUtc. Inbox: written when a message arrives, guards against duplicate processing, stamps ReceivedOnUtc. Same anatomy, opposite direction. That symmetry is why I don't design the inbox from scratch — I copy the outbox message record, rename the type, fix the namespace, flip the timestamp column, and I'm 80% done.`,`Why start from the outbox at all? Two honest reasons. One: I already have a working example, so I'm not rebuilding infrastructure live on a webinar. Two: it gives us a perfect parallel to hold up side by side when we compare the two patterns — you'll remember the inbox because it rhymes with something you already know.`],quote:`The outbox proves you sent what you saved; the inbox proves you saved what you received.`,table:{headers:[``,`Outbox`,`Inbox`],rows:[[`Side`,`Publisher`,`Consumer`],[`Written when`,`Business data commits`,`Message arrives`],[`Guards against`,`Lost publishes`,`Duplicate processing`],[`Timestamp column`,`OccurredOnUtc`,`ReceivedOnUtc`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Inbox Pattern in .NET (The Outbox's Missing Half)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vS9_WsNwLlo`,at:`1:02`}},{heading:`03 · The whole trick is one unique constraint`,body:[`Here's the mechanism, and it's almost disappointingly small. Open a database transaction — that's your atomicity guarantee. Take the message ID and the payload, store them in the inbox table. If we hit a duplicate message ID, which means the broker redelivered, the INSERT fails on the unique constraint and we simply ignore it. If the insert succeeds, we commit. That's the entire dedupe engine.`,`Now, when you think about it, a single INSERT is atomic on its own — so if all you need is duplicate detection, you don't even need to open an explicit transaction. But why not just SELECT 'does this ID exist' before processing? Because check-then-act is a race: two concurrent deliveries of the same message both pass the check, both process, and you're back to double-settling invoice #8841. The unique constraint is the arbiter, not the SELECT. The database enforces it under concurrency, for free, forever.`,`So the runtime flow is: try the insert, catch the unique violation, ack the duplicate, move on. The broker is satisfied — it got its ack — and your side effects ran exactly once. No distributed locks, no Redis round-trip, no coordinator service. One index doing one job.`],quote:`A unique index is the cheapest idempotency key you will ever buy.`,table:{headers:[`Dedupe approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`SELECT then process`,`Easy to read`,`Race under concurrency`,`Avoid`],[`INSERT with unique constraint`,`Atomic, race-free`,`Must handle the violation`,`Default choice`],[`Distributed lock (Redis)`,`Works without shared DB`,`Extra infra, TTL edge cases`,`Only if no shared DB`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Inbox Pattern in .NET (The Outbox's Missing Half)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vS9_WsNwLlo`,at:`3:21`}},{heading:`04 · The transaction question: dedupe only, or dedupe plus work?`,body:[`Now here's where the transaction actually earns its keep — and where it starts to bite. If you only store the message in the inbox, a lone INSERT is enough. But the moment you want the inbox row and the business logic of the consumer to commit together — insert the inbox record, update the ledger, commit once — that's when you wrap both in one transaction. Either all of it happened or none of it did. If it rolls back, the message stays unacked, the broker redelivers, and you get a clean retry from a clean slate.`,`But this introduces problems, and I want to be honest about them. You're now holding a database transaction open while your handler does real work. Slow I/O inside that transaction stretches locks and pressures the connection pool. Why not split them — commit the inbox row, then do the work? Because a crash in between is the worst outcome: the inbox says 'already processed,' the effect never happened, and the broker will never resend. You've converted a duplicate into a loss.`,`So the trade is atomicity versus transaction duration, and my rule of thumb: reserve the big transaction for database-only effects, and push external side effects — HTTP calls, emails, third parties — to after the commit, with their own idempotency keys. Keep the lease short.`],quote:`Atomicity is rented by the millisecond — know exactly what you're doing inside the lease.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Dedupe-only INSERT`,`Shortest transaction`,`Effect not atomic with dedupe`,`Naturally idempotent handlers`],[`Inbox + work in one tx`,`Exactly-once effects`,`Long-held locks`,`Database-only effects`],[`Inbox, then post-commit effects`,`Short tx, flexible`,`Needs idempotency keys`,`HTTP, email, third parties`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Inbox Pattern in .NET (The Outbox's Missing Half)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vS9_WsNwLlo`,at:`3:57`}},{heading:`05 · Schema, indexes, and two million messages`,body:[`Let's talk schema, because this is where demos die at scale. The inbox table mirrors the outbox: message ID, payload, timestamp — except ours is ReceivedOnUtc instead of OccurredOn. And because we're serializing the payload to JSON, we don't have to do anything special to make it work with the outbox — the same message can flow out of an outbox and into an inbox untouched, byte for byte.`,`Scale check: the demo seeds around two million outbox messages, and the inbox earns the same structure. The trick that keeps queries fast is a filtered index — index only the rows you actually scan, like the unprocessed ones, instead of the whole history. Why not index everything? Because processed rows are dead weight; the filtered index keeps the hot set small and the seeks cheap.`,`And one housekeeping note people skip: purge or archive processed inbox rows on a schedule. Dedupe only needs recent history — a message redelivered three weeks after a deploy is a corner case your retention window should still cover, but you don't need every ID since the beginning of time.`],quote:`Index the slice you scan, archive the history you don't.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Inbox Pattern in .NET (The Outbox's Missing Half)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vS9_WsNwLlo`,at:`5:40`}}],diagrams:[{id:`inbox-duplicate-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Duplicate delivery, defused by the inbox`,caption:`The ack dies, RabbitMQ redelivers, and the unique constraint on the inbox table turns a double-charge into a no-op.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Q as RabbitMQ
    participant C as .NET Consumer
    participant DB as Postgres Inbox

    Q->>C: Deliver message (id 42)
    C->>DB: INSERT inbox (message_id 42)
    DB-->>C: Insert OK
    C->>C: Run business logic
    C->>DB: COMMIT
    C-->>Q: Ack
    Note over Q,C: Ack lost on a flapping connection
    Q->>C: Redeliver (id 42)
    C->>DB: INSERT inbox (message_id 42)
    DB-->>C: Unique constraint violation
    Note over C: Duplicate detected - skip the work
    C-->>Q: Ack duplicate`},{id:`outbox-inbox-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Outbox and inbox: the full round trip`,caption:`The publisher's outbox guarantees a saved change gets published; the consumer's inbox guarantees a delivered message gets processed exactly once.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph PUB["Publisher service"]
        PBIZ[("Business data")] --> POUT[("Outbox table")]
        POUT --> PROC["Outbox processor"]
    end
    Q[("RabbitMQ")]
    subgraph CON["Consumer service"]
        H["Message handler"] --> IN[("Inbox table<br/>unique on message_id")]
        H --> EFF[("Business data")]
    end
    PROC -->|"publish"| Q
    Q -->|"deliver / redeliver"| H
    IN -.->|"duplicate? skip"| H`}],takeaways:[`Assume at-least-once, always: design every handler as if each message will arrive twice, because on a lost ack it will.`,`One unique index on message_id in the inbox table buys race-free duplicate detection for the price of a single INSERT — no locks, no Redis, no coordinator.`,`Skip the explicit transaction when you only need dedupe — a lone INSERT is already atomic; open one only when the inbox row and the business effect must commit together.`,`Mirror the outbox schema for the inbox (MessageId, JSON payload, ReceivedOnUtc) and add a filtered index on unprocessed rows so scans stay fast at millions of messages.`,`Keep payloads as JSON so the same message flows out of an outbox and into an inbox with zero transformation — and purge processed rows so dedupe state stays a hot set, not an archive.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Inbox Pattern in .NET (The Outbox's Missing Half)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vS9_WsNwLlo`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-23TMha14czs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:26,title:`Distributed Messaging Made Easy With Wolverine & RabbitMQ`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:03`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Kafka`,`RabbitMQ`,`Wolverine`,`Azure`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The 2 AM Deploy That Ate 400 Registration Commands`,paragraphs:[`It's 2:07 AM and we're pushing a checkout fix. The deploy script bounces the app pool, the health check goes green, everyone goes to bed. At 9:15 the support queue lights up: hundreds of users had signed up overnight, but their accounts were never actually created. The RegisterUser commands were gone — not failed, gone.`,`Here's the kicker: the setup was textbook. builder.Host.UseWolverine(), an endpoint injecting IMessageBus, sending a RegisterUser command, handled by a RegisterUserHandler class wired purely by naming convention. It had worked flawlessly for months. But Wolverine's out-of-the-box local queue lives inside the process — and our deploy restart killed the process. Every in-flight message vaporized with it.`,`The fix wasn't more code. It was a different boundary: put RabbitMQ between the sender and the handler, stand it up with .NET Aspire, and let Wolverine's RabbitMQ transport handle the AMQP plumbing. Same commands, same handlers, same conventions — but now the messages survive anything we do to the app.`],problem:`Wolverine's local queue is real messaging, but it lives and dies with the process — the moment you need messages to survive a restart or cross a network boundary, you need a durable broker like RabbitMQ in the middle.`},tech:{stack:[`Wolverine`,`RabbitMQ`,`.NET Aspire`,`IMessageBus`,`Postgres`,`Management Plugin`],note:`Wolverine is the messaging framework — local queues, IMessageBus, and handler-by-convention wiring. RabbitMQ is the durable broker, Aspire orchestrates it locally, and Postgres shows up as Wolverine's option for saga persistence and message storage.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the local queue isn't enough`,body:[`Out of the box, builder.Host.UseWolverine() gives you local queues — genuine messaging functionality on a single server with zero infrastructure. For in-process decoupling, that's a fantastic default: fast, simple, no broker to babysit.`,`But the queue lives in your process. Restart the app, recycle the pool, crash on an unhandled exception — everything in flight is gone. So the question I ask on every project now is: can this message afford to die with the process? If the answer is no, the local queue was never the right tool, no matter how clean the code looks.`,`Could we bolt persistence onto the local queue? We could — but the moment you need durability and cross-process delivery, a broker hands you redelivery, competing consumers, and observability for free. You'd be rebuilding RabbitMQ badly, inside your own app.`],quote:`A local queue is a real queue — it's just not a durable one.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Local queue`,`Zero infra, in-process speed`,`Lost on restart, single server only`,`Fire-and-forget work`],[`RabbitMQ transport`,`Durable, cross-process, redelivery`,`A broker to run and monitor`,`Anything you can't afford to lose`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Messaging Made Easy With Wolverine & RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=23TMha14czs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: commands, handlers, conventions`,body:[`The endpoint injects IMessageBus and sends a RegisterUser message. I call it a command because that's what it is — an instruction that something should happen. The message is the entire contract between the two sides of the boundary.`,`And here's the part I love: Wolverine connects message to handler by convention. Name the class RegisterUserHandler and you're wired. No attributes, no registration ceremony, no DI scaffolding. Why not explicit registration everywhere? Because ceremony hides the graph — with conventions, you can read the folder structure and know exactly what handles what.`,`The trade-off is discoverability versus magic, and Wolverine threads it well: conventions cover the 95% case, and you can still override per-message when you genuinely need to. The boundary stays thin, which means swapping the transport behind it — which is exactly what we're about to do — touches almost nothing.`],quote:`Name the handler after the message and the framework disappears.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Messaging Made Easy With Wolverine & RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=23TMha14czs`,at:`0:29`}},{heading:`03 · Standing up the broker with Aspire`,body:[`First thing I obviously need is RabbitMQ itself, and I'm not writing docker-compose by hand for a demo. The Aspire hosting package for RabbitMQ gives me builder.AddRabbitMQ("rabbitmq"), then WithReference from my app project, then WaitFor — so the app doesn't start before the broker does. That WaitFor call is doing real work: without it, your first publish is a race condition.`,`Two more settings earn their keep. Container lifetime persistent, so my queues and bindings survive between debugging sessions. And the management plugin enabled — because I want to see exactly what Wolverine creates inside RabbitMQ: the exchanges, the queues, the bindings. When something doesn't arrive, I want to inspect the broker, not guess.`,`Small aside I appreciate about Aspire: every integration package ships with a readme showing the minimal wiring. AddRabbitMQ, WithReference, WaitFor — three lines and the broker is a first-class resource in your app model.`],quote:`If the broker isn't up before the app, your first message is a race condition.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Messaging Made Easy With Wolverine & RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=23TMha14czs`,at:`1:34`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring the transport: one delegate, many brokers`,body:[`Now the actual integration: install the Wolverine RabbitMQ package and pass a delegate into UseWolverine to configure it. That's the whole move. The transport config lives right next to the rest of your Wolverine setup, so the entire messaging surface of the app is readable in one place.`,`And it's worth noticing how wide that surface is. The same ecosystem ships Postgres — which you can use for saga persistence and even message storage — plus Azure Service Bus, SQS, and Kafka. RabbitMQ isn't a special choice; it's one option in a uniform plug-in model.`,`Why RabbitMQ for this talk? Local dev with Aspire is trivial, the AMQP routing model maps cleanly onto command handling, and the management UI makes the invisible visible. But because the message-to-handler conventions don't change, choosing a different broker later is a package swap and a delegate — not a rewrite.`],quote:`Swapping brokers is a package and a delegate — design your messages so that stays true.`,table:{headers:[`Transport`,`Durability`,`Best for`,`Effort`],rows:[[`RabbitMQ`,`Durable, broker-backed`,`Routing, local dev with Aspire`,`Low`],[`Azure Service Bus`,`Durable, managed`,`Azure-native estates`,`Low`],[`AWS SQS`,`Durable, managed`,`AWS-native estates`,`Low`],[`Kafka`,`Durable append-only log`,`High-throughput streaming`,`Medium`],[`Postgres`,`Message storage + sagas`,`Persistence alongside transport`,`Low`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Messaging Made Easy With Wolverine & RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=23TMha14czs`,at:`2:57`}},{heading:`05 · What durability actually buys you`,body:[`With RabbitMQ in the middle, sender and handler are decoupled in time. The handler can be down when the command is sent — the message waits in the queue. The app can restart mid-flight — the message is already safely on the broker. A handler throws — the message comes back for redelivery instead of vanishing into the void.`,`That's the difference between the 2 AM incident and a non-event: in the local-queue world, a restart is a data-loss event; in the broker world, it's a pause. The management plugin lets you watch it happen — publish a command, kill the app, restart, and watch the message flow through the exchange into the queue and out to the handler.`,`The cost is honest: one more moving part to run and monitor. But Aspire makes that nearly free in development, and in production a broker is a solved problem you'd eventually need anyway. Durability isn't a feature you add to your code — it's a boundary you move.`],quote:`Durability isn't a feature you add — it's a boundary you move.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Messaging Made Easy With Wolverine & RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=23TMha14czs`,at:`8:00`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two Paths for One Command`,caption:`The same RegisterUser command, routed through the in-memory local queue versus the durable RabbitMQ transport — only one survives a restart.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph APP["dotnet API process"]
    EP["POST /register-user"] --> BUS["IMessageBus.Send()"]
    BUS --> LQ["local queue"]
    BUS --> WL["Wolverine RabbitMQ transport"]
  end
  LQ -.->|"lost on restart"| X["commands vanish"]
  WL -->|"AMQP publish"| EX["wolverine exchange"]
  EX --> Q["durable queue"]
  Q --> H["RegisterUserHandler"]
  H --> DB[("Postgres")]`},{id:`sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Restart in the Middle, Message Survives`,caption:`Sequence of a durable send: the command is published, the app dies, and RabbitMQ holds the message until the consumer comes back.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant A as .NET API
  participant W as Wolverine
  participant R as RabbitMQ
  participant H as RegisterUserHandler
  C->>A: POST /register-user
  A->>W: Send(RegisterUser)
  W->>R: publish to exchange
  Note over A: app restarts at 2 AM
  Note over R: message waits durably in queue
  R->>H: deliver when consumer is back
  H-->>R: ack
  H->>H: create the account`}],takeaways:[`Anything you can't afford to lose on a restart goes through a durable broker — in-memory local queues are for fire-and-forget work only.`,`One naming convention — {Message}Handler — replaces hundreds of lines of registration code; let Wolverine wire the graph for you.`,`Two Aspire calls (AddRabbitMQ + WaitFor) guarantee the broker is up before your app starts, on every single run.`,`Enable the RabbitMQ management plugin in dev so you can inspect every exchange and queue Wolverine creates before you debug a routing problem blind.`,`Match transport to need: local queue for single-server, RabbitMQ/SQS/Service Bus for durable cross-process delivery, Postgres when you also need saga persistence and message storage.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Distributed Messaging Made Easy With Wolverine & RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=23TMha14czs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-9_pRk7PwkpY`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:27,title:`Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 7`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:23`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`SignalR`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`10:00 at peak checkout volume: The Dashboard Froze, and the Polling Storm Was the Arsonist`,paragraphs:[`Black Friday, 15:10 during a database replica failover. Our ops dashboard — the one screen everyone stares at during a traffic event — froze. Not crashed, froze: numbers stuck at 23:41, forty-six minutes old, while real orders poured in. The pager lit up with the worst kind of alert: the thing you look at during incidents had become the incident.`,`The root cause was embarrassingly simple. Every open dashboard tab polled /api/orders/summary every five seconds. At 38,000 concurrent viewers, that's roughly 7,600 requests per second asking the same question — 'anything new?' — and getting 'no' 99.9% of the time. The 100-connection Postgres pool drowned, p99 latency hit 40 seconds, and the dashboard's own polling helped kill the data it was polling for.`,`The fix wasn't a bigger pool. It was flipping the direction of control: one persistent WebSocket per tab, and the server pushes a message only when the summary actually changes. That's the night I sat down and properly learned SignalR — AddSignalR, a Hub, OnConnectedAsync, Clients.All.SendAsync — and discovered the Hub model is both smaller and stranger than it first looks.`],problem:`How do you push real-time updates to thousands of clients without a self-inflicted request storm — and what does SignalR's Hub model actually do between the handshake and the broadcast?`},tech:{stack:[`SignalR`,`.NET 7`,`ASP.NET Core`,`WebSockets`,`Hub Class`,`Postman`,`ConnectionId`,`NuGet`],note:`SignalR's Hub sits between ASP.NET Core endpoint routing and connected clients: AddSignalR registers the services, MapHub exposes the route, and the Hub lets the server invoke named methods on clients over a negotiated WebSocket. Postman's WebSocket client stands in as the raw test harness for the handshake.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why polling melts your database`,body:[`Here's the trap I fell into: the dashboard felt 'real-time' because it refreshed every five seconds. But do the math with me — 38,000 open tabs times one request every five seconds is about 7,600 requests per second, forever, even when nothing changed. Polling is a denial-of-service attack you launch against yourself, on a schedule.`,`So why not just poll slower? Because then your 'real-time' dashboard is 30 seconds stale, and stale is worse than slow on an ops screen. Why not long-polling? Now you're holding thousands of half-open HTTP requests and fighting load balancer idle timeouts. What you actually want is the opposite direction of control: the server calls you when something changes. That's the whole pitch behind SignalR — it negotiates WebSockets when the network allows it and degrades to SSE or long-polling when it doesn't, so you write one API and stop caring which transport survived the corporate proxy.`],quote:`Polling is a scheduled denial-of-service attack against your own database.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Client polling (5s)`,`Dead simple, stateless`,`~7.6k req/s at 38k clients, mostly wasted`,`Tiny audiences, relaxed freshness`],[`Long-polling`,`Works through old proxies`,`Held requests, LB timeout pain`,`Legacy fallback only`],[`Raw WebSockets`,`Full duplex, zero idle traffic`,`You own reconnect, fallback, framing`,`Greenfield, single transport`],[`SignalR`,`Auto transport negotiation, hub model, groups`,`Extra abstraction to learn`,`Default choice for .NET real-time`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 7`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_pRk7PwkpY`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Wiring it up: AddSignalR and a Hub that isn't a controller`,body:[`Two lines of setup and one class. First, builder.Services.AddSignalR() registers the SignalR services with dependency injection — skip that and your MapHub call fails at startup, which is honestly the kindest failure mode available. Then a public sealed ChatHub inheriting from Hub. That base class hands you three superpowers: Clients (who you can talk to), Groups (subsets of clients), and Context (who is calling you right now).`,"Here's the part that bites people: a Hub is not a controller. ASP.NET Core spins up a fresh Hub instance per invocation — OnConnectedAsync gets one, your next hub method gets a different one. Stash state on `this` and it's gone by the next call. If you need shared state, inject a singleton service; if you need per-connection state, Context.Items is your shelf. And Context is a HubCallerContext tied to a real connection — touch it from a background service where there's no caller, and there's nothing behind it."],quote:`A Hub instance is a mayfly — it lives for one invocation, so never let it hold your state.`,table:{headers:[`Hub member`,`Gives you`,`Gotcha`],rows:[[`Clients.All`,`Every connected client`,`Blast radius is everyone`],[`Clients.Client(id)`,`One specific connection`,`ConnectionId changes on reconnect`],[`Clients.Group(name)`,`A subscribed subset`,`Membership is manual — add on connect`],[`Context.ConnectionId`,`Caller's unique ID`,`Nothing behind it when invoked server-side`],[`Context.Items`,`Per-connection scratch state`,`Not persisted across restarts`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 7`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_pRk7PwkpY`,at:`0:36`}},{heading:`03 · Walking the boundary: the server calls the client`,body:[`This is the inversion that took me a while to internalize. In HTTP, the client names the endpoint and the server answers. In SignalR, the server names a method — Clients.All.SendAsync('ReceiveMessage', 'connection X has joined') — and the client had better have a handler registered under exactly that name. It's a stringly-typed contract across the wire: no compiler checks it, and no exception fires if the client never defined ReceiveMessage. The message just vanishes. My rule: the method name lives in a shared constant referenced by both sides.`,`Notice what we sent: Context.ConnectionId, the unique identifier SignalR assigns per connection. That ID is your handle for targeted sends later — Clients.Client(connectionId) — but treat it as ephemeral. A reconnect mints a new ID, so don't build user identity on top of it; map connectionId to your real user ID in OnConnectedAsync and clean it up in OnDisconnectedAsync.`],quote:`The client method name is a handshake written in string form — one typo and the message dies silently in transit.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Reality`,`Mitigation`],rows:[[`Ordering`,`Preserved per connection`,`Design handlers as sequential`],[`Delivery guarantee`,`None — a disconnected client misses it`,`Back critical events with a queue/outbox`],[`Client method contract`,`Runtime string match`,`Shared constants + one integration test`],[`Reconnect`,`Mints a new ConnectionId`,`Rejoin groups in OnConnectedAsync`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 7`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_pRk7PwkpY`,at:`2:00`}},{heading:`04 · Blast radius: All, Groups, or one connection?`,body:[`Clients.All is the demo-friendly firehose and the production footgun. In our chat hub, 'connection X has joined' going to every connected client is fine for a tutorial and a nightmare at 38,000 dashboards. The moment your audience splits by tenant, room, or dashboard, reach for Groups: add the caller with Groups.AddToGroupAsync(Context.ConnectionId, 'room-42') inside OnConnectedAsync or an explicit JoinRoom hub method, then broadcast to Clients.Group('room-42').`,`Why not just send everything and filter on the client? Because egress costs money and battery — pushing 38,000 messages so 200 people read one is a ~190x waste. Why not loop over Clients.Client(id) per user? That's N serial invocations instead of one group fan-out; the group broadcast is the primitive you want. And when a background worker with no caller and no Context needs to push, inject IHubContext<ChatHub> and target groups or connections from there.`],quote:`Pick your audience before you pick your message — broadcast is a decision, not a default.`,table:{headers:[`Targeting API`,`Blast radius`,`Use when`],rows:[[`Clients.All`,`Every connection`,`Global announcements, tiny fleets`],[`Clients.Group(name)`,`Subscribed subset`,`Rooms, tenants, per-dashboard feeds`],[`Clients.Client(connId)`,`One connection`,`Targeted replies and notifications`],[`Clients.Others`,`Everyone except caller`,`Echo suppression in chat`],[`IHubContext<T>`,`Any of the above`,`Pushing from background services`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 7`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_pRk7PwkpY`,at:`3:58`}},{heading:`05 · Testing a push protocol with a pull tool`,body:[`How do you verify a hub before any real client exists? Postman. Create a WebSocket request and point it at wss://your-api/chathub — that's the route you declared with MapHub<ChatHub>('/chathub'). But an open socket is not a connected hub: SignalR speaks its own handshake on top. You send the protocol negotiation message — protocol json, version 1, terminated by the 0x1e record separator — and only then does the server consider you connected and fire OnConnectedAsync.`,`Drop a breakpoint inside OnConnectedAsync, hit send, and watch the whole machine light up: the handshake lands, the breakpoint trips, Context.ConnectionId is populated, and Clients.All.SendAsync('ReceiveMessage', ...) fans the 'has joined' message back out — including to the client that just connected. Seeing those raw frames in Postman is the moment SignalR stops being magic: it's a negotiated socket plus a naming convention, and now you can debug it like one.`],quote:`If you can't watch the handshake bytes, you're not debugging — you're guessing.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 7`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_pRk7PwkpY`,at:`8:31`}}],diagrams:[{id:`signalr-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`SignalR Hub Topology: from route to client handler`,caption:`One negotiated WebSocket per client; the Hub picks an audience and invokes a named method on each.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph CL["Connected Clients"]
        B["Browser Tab"]
        P["Postman WebSocket"]
    end
    B -->|"wss:// + SignalR handshake"| R["MapHub route /chathub"]
    P -->|"wss:// + handshake"| R
    R --> H["ChatHub : Hub"]
    H -->|"OnConnectedAsync"| ID["Context.ConnectionId"]
    H --> T{"Choose audience"}
    T -->|"broadcast"| A["Clients.All"]
    T -->|"room / tenant"| G["Clients.Group room-42"]
    T -->|"one user"| C1["Clients.Client connId"]
    A --> S["SendAsync ReceiveMessage"]
    G --> S
    C1 --> S
    S -->|"invokes handler"| B
    S -->|"invokes handler"| P`},{id:`connect-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Connection Sequence: handshake to first broadcast`,caption:`What happens between Postman hitting send and every client seeing 'has joined'.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as New Client (Postman)
    participant K as ASP.NET Core .NET 7
    participant H as ChatHub
    participant A as All Connected Clients
    C->>K: WebSocket upgrade wss://host/chathub
    K-->>C: 101 Switching Protocols
    C->>K: Handshake protocol json version 1 + 0x1e
    K->>H: OnConnectedAsync()
    H->>H: Read Context.ConnectionId
    H->>A: Clients.All.SendAsync(ReceiveMessage, id joined)
    A-->>C: ReceiveMessage handler fires
    C-->>K: Handshake ack - connection live`}],takeaways:[`Do the poll math before shipping: 10,000 clients polling every 5 seconds is ~2,000 req/s of mostly-'no' answers — one persistent WebSocket per client drops idle traffic to zero.`,`Wire-up order matters: builder.Services.AddSignalR() must precede app.MapHub<T>() or the app fails at startup — a two-line checklist that saves a 3 AM deploy.`,"Assume every Hub instance evaporates after one invocation — never cache state on `this`; use a singleton service for shared state and Context.Items for per-connection state.",`Cap your broadcast blast radius: reserve Clients.All for audiences under ~1,000 connections and shard everything larger with Groups per room or tenant.`,`Treat the client method name ('ReceiveMessage') as an unverified runtime contract — centralize it in a shared constant and add one integration test, because a typo fails with zero exceptions.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Real-Time Applications With SignalR & .NET 7`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9_pRk7PwkpY`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`realtime-channels`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-daaiAjZnOm4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:28,title:`Event-Driven .NET: Building a Publish/Subscribe System with RabbitMQ`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:02`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`RabbitMQ`,`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Night Half Our Customers Never Got a Receipt`,paragraphs:[`It's 08:45 as mobile traffic surged on Black Friday and the support channel lights up: customers are ordering, but half of them never get a receipt. The email service swears it's running fine. The analytics dashboard is missing half the night's orders. Two teams, one broker, and a very quiet RabbitMQ management UI.`,`I pull the logs and the numbers don't add up: the order service published 10 OrderPlaced messages, the email service consumed 6, analytics consumed 4. No errors, no drops — RabbitMQ did exactly what it was told. Both services were consuming the same orders queue, so the broker round-robined every message to exactly one of them. Competing consumers, working perfectly, at the worst possible moment.`,`The fix wasn't a second queue and a second publish call — that just moves the coupling into the producer. It was finally internalizing the difference between a queue and an exchange: a queue divides work, an exchange broadcasts. One fanout exchange, two bound queues, publish once, and both services see 100% of the stream.`],problem:`Two services needed the full stream of the same events, but a single queue delivers each message exactly once — we had competing consumers where we needed publish/subscribe.`},tech:{stack:[`RabbitMQ`,`RabbitMQ .NET Client`,`.NET`,`Docker`],note:`RabbitMQ running in Docker, the official RabbitMQ .NET client on both producer and consumer, and the exchange/queue topology that turns competing consumers into publish/subscribe.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why one queue can't be both a work queue and a megaphone`,body:[`Let's start where the talk starts: a producer and a consumer, both using the official RabbitMQ .NET client. The producer declares a queue and publishes 10 messages — the exchange name is string.Empty, so we're hitting the default exchange, and the routing key is just the message text, which doubles as the queue name. Simple, and perfect for a demo.`,`Now spin up two consumer instances against that same queue and watch: 10 messages go in, one instance gets about 5, the other gets about 5. That's the competing consumers pattern — RabbitMQ round-robins across consumers, and each message is processed exactly once. Fantastic when you want to scale out a workload. Catastrophic when you actually wanted every service to see every message.`,`And that's the trap I hit at 3 AM. I assumed a queue was a broadcast channel. It isn't. A queue is a load balancer. If two different services need the same event, pointing them at the same queue means each event goes to exactly one of them — silently, with zero errors.`],quote:`A queue is a load balancer, not a megaphone.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Competing Consumers`,`Publish/Subscribe`],rows:[[`Goal`,`Scale out one job across workers`,`Notify many independent services`],[`Delivery`,`Each message to exactly one consumer`,`One copy per bound queue`],[`Topology`,`One queue, N consumer instances`,`One exchange, N queues`],[`Broker behavior`,`Round-robin across consumers`,`Fanout copies to every queue`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Event-Driven .NET: Building a Publish/Subscribe System with RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=daaiAjZnOm4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the default exchange and string.Empty`,body:[`Before we fix the topology, appreciate the little details that keep the demo from blowing up. The consumer also declares the queue — same name, same settings — even though the producer already declares it. Why? Because declare is idempotent, and it means you can start the consumer first and nothing crashes waiting for a queue that doesn't exist yet.`,`The publish side is the part people gloss over: BasicPublishAsync with the exchange set to string.Empty routes through the default exchange, which is a direct exchange that matches the routing key against queue names. So a routing key of 'messages' lands in the queue called messages. It's a lovely shortcut for hello-world, and a habit you want to break before production.`,`On the consumer side it's three moves: create an AsyncEventingBasicConsumer, wire a delegate that receives each message, then call channel.BasicConsumeAsync to start pulling. That delegate is where your business logic lives — everything else is plumbing.`],quote:`Declare on both sides — the broker doesn't remember who showed up first.`,table:{headers:[`Piece`,`What it does`,`Gotcha`],rows:[[`Default exchange (string.Empty)`,`Direct exchange that routes by queue name`,`Only works when routing key = an existing queue name`],[`Queue declare on both sides`,`Idempotent, kills the startup-order race`,`Mismatched declare settings close the channel`],[`Routing key = message text`,`Doubles as the queue name in the demo`,`In production the routing key belongs to the exchange contract`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Event-Driven .NET: Building a Publish/Subscribe System with RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=daaiAjZnOm4`,at:`0:51`}},{heading:`03 · The 'just add a second queue' trap`,body:[`So the naive fix: we need two consumers to each get everything, so let's just make two queues. Rename messages to messages1, add messages2, and have the producer publish the same message twice — once with each routing key. Run it, and yes, both consumers now see every message. Demo saved.`,`But look at what we just did to the producer. It now knows how many consumers exist. Add a billing service next quarter? That's a producer code change, a redeploy, and a config review — for what should be a purely subscriber-side concern. Every new subscriber reaches back into the publisher and pokes it.`,`This is the coupling smell. The whole point of event-driven design is that producers announce facts and don't care who's listening. The moment the producer enumerates its audience, you've rebuilt point-to-point integration with extra queues.`],quote:`If your producer knows how many consumers exist, your architecture has a leak.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Producer publishes to each queue`,`Explicit, easy to debug`,`N publishes, producer edits per new consumer`,`Two consumers that never change`],[`Fanout exchange + bindings`,`Publish once, broker duplicates`,`One-time topology setup`,`Any number of subscribers`],[`Topic exchange + patterns`,`Selective subscriptions`,`Routing key design overhead`,`Filtered event streams`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Event-Driven .NET: Building a Publish/Subscribe System with RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=daaiAjZnOm4`,at:`3:58`}},{heading:`04 · The fanout exchange: publish once, deliver everywhere`,body:[`Here's the actual fix, and it's almost boring in its elegance: declare a fanout exchange, bind messages1 and messages2 to it, and publish once to the exchange with an empty routing key. Fanout ignores the routing key entirely — it just copies every message to every bound queue. The producer publishes one message; the broker does the duplication.`,`And notice what didn't change: the consumers. Same AsyncEventingBasicConsumer, same BasicConsumeAsync, same delegate. The subscriber story is now 'declare a queue, bind it to the exchange, consume.' Adding consumer number three is one new queue and one binding — the producer never finds out, and that's exactly right.`,`This is publish/subscribe done the RabbitMQ way: the exchange is the contract, bindings are the subscription list, and queues are just per-subscriber inboxes — each of which can still run its own competing consumers for throughput.`],quote:`Fanout is the broker saying: I'll make the copies, you do the work.`,table:{headers:[`Exchange type`,`Routing rule`,`Best for`],rows:[[`Default (direct by name)`,`Routing key = queue name`,`Demos, quick point-to-point`],[`Fanout`,`Ignores routing key, copies to all bindings`,`Broadcast events to every subscriber`],[`Topic`,`Pattern-matched routing keys`,`Selective subscriptions`],[`Direct`,`Exact routing key match`,`Targeted delivery`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Event-Driven .NET: Building a Publish/Subscribe System with RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=daaiAjZnOm4`,at:`5:05`}},{heading:`05 · Consumer mechanics: AsyncEventingBasicConsumer in practice`,body:[`Last thing, because this is where real systems wobble: the consumer loop. AsyncEventingBasicConsumer gives you an async delegate per delivery, which means you can await your handler instead of blocking a thread — do that. And remember, the queue declaration on the consumer side is your insurance policy against startup order.`,`When one queue's workload grows, you don't add bindings — you add consumer instances to that queue and let round-robin divide the work. Keep handlers stateless so any instance can process any message, and tune prefetch so one slow consumer doesn't hoard in-flight messages its peers could be handling.`,`Get those two axes right — fanout for reach, competing consumers for throughput — and the topology almost configures itself. That's the whole lesson: queues divide, exchanges broadcast, and knowing which one you're asking for is the difference between a demo and a 3 AM page.`],quote:`The consumer API is three lines — the semantics behind it are the whole lesson.`,table:{headers:[`Knob`,`Effect`,`Watch out for`],rows:[[`More consumer instances`,`Round-robin divides the work`,`Handlers must be stateless`],[`Prefetch count (QoS)`,`Caps unacked in-flight messages`,`Too low starves throughput`],[`Manual acks`,`No message loss on crash`,`Every delivery needs an ack or nack`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Event-Driven .NET: Building a Publish/Subscribe System with RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=daaiAjZnOm4`,at:`7:21`}}],diagrams:[{id:`competing-consumers`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Competing consumers: one queue, work divided`,caption:`Two consumer instances share one queue — RabbitMQ round-robins, so each of the 10 messages is processed exactly once.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    P["Producer<br/>.NET + RabbitMQ client"] -->|"BasicPublishAsync<br/>exchange = string.Empty"| DX["Default Exchange<br/>routes by queue name"]
    DX --> Q[["orders queue<br/>10 messages"]]
    Q -->|"round-robin"| C1["Consumer instance 1<br/>gets ~5 messages"]
    Q -->|"round-robin"| C2["Consumer instance 2<br/>gets ~5 messages"]`},{id:`fanout-pubsub`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Publish/subscribe: one fanout, copies everywhere`,caption:`The producer publishes once to a fanout exchange; every bound queue receives its own copy, so each consumer sees the full stream.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant P as Producer app
    participant X as orders.fanout exchange
    participant Q1 as messages1 queue
    participant Q2 as messages2 queue
    participant C1 as Consumer 1
    participant C2 as Consumer 2
    P->>X: BasicPublishAsync - exchange orders.fanout, routingKey empty
    X->>Q1: copy of the message
    X->>Q2: copy of the message
    Q1-->>C1: AsyncEventingBasicConsumer delegate fires
    Q2-->>C2: AsyncEventingBasicConsumer delegate fires
    Note over C1,C2: Both consumers receive 100 percent of messages`}],takeaways:[`One queue + N consumers = each message processed exactly once (10 messages split roughly 5/5); one fanout exchange + N bound queues = N full copies.`,`Declare the queue on both the producer and consumer side — declare is idempotent and removes the startup-order race entirely.`,`In production, publish to a named exchange, never via the default exchange with string.Empty — that's a demo crutch that couples you to queue names.`,`Fanout ignores the routing key: publish once with an empty routing key and every bound queue gets its own copy.`,`Adding a subscriber should cost exactly one queue plus one binding and zero producer changes — if it costs a redeploy, your publisher is coupled to its audience.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Event-Driven .NET: Building a Publish/Subscribe System with RabbitMQ`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=daaiAjZnOm4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-sN5YpfOpCHA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-messaging`,order:29,title:`Building Messaging Apps in .NET Using the Official RabbitMQ Client`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:53`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`RabbitMQ`,`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`13:20 right after the schema migration: The Queue That Didn't Come Back`,paragraphs:[`It's 17:00 as cross-region traffic shifted and the dashboard shows the orders queue flatlined at zero while the producer service is still logging 'Sent.' every two seconds. Twenty minutes earlier, the Docker host OOM-killed the RabbitMQ container during a nightly backup. The container restarted cleanly — the queue didn't. It had been declared back when this was a demo, without durable: true, so the broker came back to an empty world and every in-flight order sitting on that queue simply ceased to exist.`,`The cruel part is what the logs said. BasicPublish returned success on every single message after the restart, because publishing to the default exchange with a routing key that matches no queue is not an error — the broker silently drops it. A producer happily writing into the void, a consumer waiting on a queue that no longer exists, and a green monitoring dashboard sitting in between.`,`The fix was two booleans and one habit: durable: true on QueueDeclareAsync, persistent delivery mode on every publish, and publisher confirms so that 'Sent.' actually means stored. We now restart the broker container in CI on purpose — if the pipeline can't survive a docker restart, it doesn't ship.`],problem:`One boolean — durable: true — was the entire difference between a queue that survives a broker restart and one that evaporates with every order sitting on it.`},tech:{stack:[`RabbitMQ`,`RabbitMQ.Client`,`.NET 8 / 9`,`Docker`,`AMQP 0-9-1`,`Default Exchange`,`Durable Queues`,`UUIDv7`],note:`The official RabbitMQ.Client NuGet package (300M+ downloads, targets .NET 8 and .NET Standard 2.0, so it runs fine on .NET 9) talking AMQP to a Dockerized broker. Connections and channels are the two expensive primitives; queues, exchanges, and durability flags are the state that decides whether data survives a restart.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just POST it over HTTP?`,body:[`So the obvious question first: two apps need to talk — why not have the producer POST to a consumer endpoint and call it a day? Honestly, for two services that are always up and chatty in real time, that's a fine answer. The trouble starts the moment the consumer is slow, mid-deploy, or just down. Now the producer is holding a failed request, and you start writing retries. Then a retry budget. Then a local store-and-forward so you don't lose the payload between retries.`,`Pause right there — you've just described a message broker, hand-rolled and worse. RabbitMQ gives you the buffer, the redelivery, and the backpressure as the product: the producer publishes and moves on, the queue absorbs the spike, the consumer pulls at its own pace. The trade is real, though — a broker is another moving part to run (that's our Docker container), and you've signed up for eventual consistency between the two apps. Producer-consumer isn't 'better' than HTTP; it's the right shape when 'the other side might not be there right now' is a requirement.`],quote:`Every retry loop you write by hand is a worse version of a queue you didn't want to run.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Direct HTTP call`,`Zero infra, easy to debug`,`Consumer downtime loses work; spikes hit the consumer directly`,`Tiny always-on internal tools`],[`RabbitMQ queue`,`Buffering, redelivery, temporal decoupling`,`Broker to operate; eventual consistency`,`Producer/consumer split where uptime differs`],[`gRPC streaming`,`Low latency, typed contracts`,`Both ends must be up; tight coupling`,`Real-time service-to-service calls`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Messaging Apps in .NET Using the Official RabbitMQ Client`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sN5YpfOpCHA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: connections, channels, and the expensive-resource rule`,body:[`The client hands you two primitives and they are absolutely not interchangeable. The Connection is a real TCP socket with an AMQP handshake and auth on top — that's the expensive one. The Channel is a lightweight, multiplexed session over that connection, and it's your actual working handle: QueueDeclare, bind and unbind, the exchange APIs, publish, consume, ack. Everything you do with the broker goes through a channel.`,`The rookie move — and I've committed it — is newing up a connection and channel per message. Under load that's a TCP handshake plus AMQP framing on every publish, and the broker melts. The fix is boring: open one connection per app and treat it like a connection pool, then scope channels with a using (inline using in the demo) so they don't leak. Why not one channel for everything, then? Channels are cheap, but a long-running consumer loop and a bursty publisher sharing one channel get in each other's way — one channel per concern keeps the failure domains separate.`],quote:`Treat the connection like a database pool and the channel like a request-scoped handle — never the other way around.`,table:{headers:[`Resource`,`Cost`,`Lifetime`,`Rule of thumb`],rows:[[`Connection`,`TCP socket + AMQP handshake + auth`,`App lifetime`,`One per app, reuse aggressively`],[`Channel`,`Lightweight multiplexed session`,`Per workload or concern`,`using-scoped; don't share across concerns`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Messaging Apps in .NET Using the Official RabbitMQ Client`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sN5YpfOpCHA`,at:`2:06`}},{heading:`03 · Declaring the queue: the booleans that decide whether you lose data`,body:[`QueueDeclareAsync looks like a boring method — a name and some flags — but the flags are the entire durability story. Durable: true means the queue definition itself survives a broker restart. Exclusive and autoDelete are for temporary, single-consumer, RPC-style stuff; leave them false for anything that holds real work.`,`Here's the trap that bit us at 2 AM: a durable queue alone saves nothing. The message has to be published as persistent too — durability is a contract between the queue and the message, and both sides have to sign. And while we're on silent failures: publish to the default exchange with a routing key that matches no queue, and the broker drops the message without a whisper. No exception, no log line. That's why a broker-restart drill belongs in CI, not in a postmortem.`],quote:`Durability is a handshake — durable queue plus persistent message, or you've built a sieve with a lock on one door.`,table:{headers:[`Queue declared`,`Message published`,`Survives broker restart?`],rows:[[`Durable`,`Persistent`,`Yes — the goal`],[`Durable`,`Transient`,`Queue survives, messages don't`],[`Transient`,`Persistent`,`No — queue definition vanishes`],[`Transient`,`Transient`,`No — full wipe`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Messaging Apps in .NET Using the Official RabbitMQ Client`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sN5YpfOpCHA`,at:`3:46`}},{heading:`04 · Publishing into the default exchange: the empty string that routes straight to a queue`,body:[`Now the producer. BasicPublish wants an exchange name and a routing key, and the beginner move is passing the empty string as the exchange — that's the default exchange, a straight wire where the routing key is literally the queue name. So exchange = empty, routingKey = 'orders', and the message lands on the queue we declared. Under the hood we're doing Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes on a string built from the current UTC time plus a UUIDv7 — and the v7 choice is quietly smart, because time-ordered IDs sort naturally and make debugging a treat.`,`Why not target the queue directly? Because AMQP only publishes to exchanges — the default exchange is the broker being kind to simple producer-consumer setups. The moment one message needs to fan out to several consumers, or route by pattern, you keep the exact same publish call and just name a direct, topic, or fanout exchange instead. That's the payoff for learning the exchange model: your producer code barely changes as the topology grows.`],quote:`The default exchange is a straight wire: routing key equals queue name — nothing clever, nothing lost, until the queue isn't there.`,table:{headers:[`Exchange`,`Routing behavior`,`Reach for it when`],rows:[[`Default (empty string)`,`routingKey = queue name, point-to-point`,`Simple producer/consumer, like this lesson`],[`Direct`,`Exact routing-key match`,`Multiple consumer types over one broker`],[`Topic`,`Pattern match with * and # wildcards`,`Flexible subscriptions on event shapes`],[`Fanout`,`Broadcast, routing key ignored`,`One event, many independent listeners`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Messaging Apps in .NET Using the Official RabbitMQ Client`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sN5YpfOpCHA`,at:`4:49`}},{heading:`05 · Consuming and the ack contract: who owns a message in flight?`,body:[`Consumer side. The channel's consume APIs push messages to you as they arrive, and the ack is where ownership actually transfers. With autoAck on, the message is considered done the instant it's delivered — maximum throughput, and a crash mid-process quietly loses it. With manual acks, the broker holds the message unacked until you say done; crash, and it's redelivered to the next consumer. That's at-least-once semantics, which also means your handler had better be idempotent, because duplicates are a when, not an if.`,`The knob people forget is prefetch — basic.qos. Without it, the broker shovels everything at the consumer and you've rebuilt the memory problem the queue was supposed to solve. Set a window of unacked deliveries, let the queue absorb the overflow — that's the entire reason the queue exists — and tune the number against how long processing actually takes.`],quote:`An unacked message isn't lost and isn't done — it's the broker holding you to your word.`,table:{headers:[`Ack mode`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`autoAck`,`Max throughput, zero bookkeeping`,`Crash loses in-flight messages`,`Derivable or tolerable data`],[`Manual ack`,`At-least-once, crash-safe`,`Must ack/nack; design for duplicates`,`Orders, payments, anything that matters`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Messaging Apps in .NET Using the Official RabbitMQ Client`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sN5YpfOpCHA`,at:`6:04`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Producer → Default Exchange → Durable Queue → Consumer`,caption:`The full topology from the lesson: two .NET apps, one Dockerized broker, and the empty-string exchange doing point-to-point routing.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph P["Producer App - .NET 9"]
        PC["Connection + Channel<br/>inline using"]
    end
    subgraph B["RabbitMQ Broker - Docker :5672 / :15672"]
        DX(("Default Exchange<br/>name = empty string")) --> Q[["orders<br/>durable = true"]]
    end
    subgraph C["Consumer App - .NET 9"]
        CC["Channel + Consumer<br/>prefetch window"]
    end
    PC -- "BasicPublish<br/>routingKey = orders" --> DX
    Q -- "BasicConsume<br/>push delivery" --> CC
    CC -- "BasicAck" --> Q`},{id:`lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Publish-consume lifecycle with acks`,caption:`Declare once, publish ten, consume and acknowledge — plus what a broker restart does to a durable queue.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant P as Producer (.NET 9)
    participant B as RabbitMQ Broker (Docker)
    participant C as Consumer (.NET 9)
    P->>B: QueueDeclareAsync orders, durable = true
    B-->>P: Declare-OK
    loop 10 iterations
        P->>P: Build message: UTC now + UUIDv7
        P->>P: Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(message)
        P->>B: BasicPublish exchange = empty, routingKey = orders
        B->>B: Default exchange routes key to queue
    end
    B->>C: Deliver message within prefetch window
    C->>C: Process payload
    C->>B: BasicAck deliveryTag
    B->>B: Remove acked message from queue
    Note over B: Broker restart: durable queue + persistent messages survive`}],takeaways:[`Declare queues with durable: true AND publish messages as persistent — one without the other survives nothing; prove it with a docker restart of the broker in CI.`,`One Connection per app, one Channel per concern: a connection per message pays a full TCP + AMQP handshake every time and will choke a broker at a few hundred publishes per second.`,`The default exchange is the empty string and its routing key is literally the queue name — and a message routed to a nonexistent queue is silently dropped unless you set the mandatory flag.`,`Treat BasicPublish as fire-and-forget until publisher confirms are on: 'Sent.' on the console means handed to the socket, not safely stored on disk.`,`Cap unacked deliveries with prefetch (basic.qos) — start at 10-100 per consumer and tune against processing time, or one slow consumer will hoard the queue while the rest idle.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Building Messaging Apps in .NET Using the Official RabbitMQ Client`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sN5YpfOpCHA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`message-queues`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`}];export{e as lessons};